/workspace/gemma4/refs/notebooks/gemma-4-the-complete-guide-expanded/gemma-4-the-complete-guide-expanded.txt:966:### 12.10 vLLM and PagedAttention (2023): why the context stops at 32K
/workspace/gemma4/refs/notebooks/gemma-4-the-complete-guide-expanded/gemma-4-the-complete-guide-expanded.txt:970:**The method.** vLLM stores the KV cache in fixed-size blocks, the way an operating system stores memory in pages, and keeps a table of which blocks belong to which request. Memory is no longer wasted on fragmentation, so more requests fit on the same GPUs. <span style="font-size:12px">[<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.06180">arXiv:2309.06180</a>, SOSP 2023]</span>
/workspace/gemma4/refs/notebooks/gemma-4-the-complete-guide-expanded/gemma-4-the-complete-guide-expanded.txt:974:<p><i>The vLLM system: a scheduler, a KV cache manager with block tables, and workers that each hold one shard of the model. In this competition there are four workers, one per L4 GPU.</i></p>
/workspace/gemma4/refs/notebooks/gemma-4-the-complete-guide-expanded/gemma-4-the-complete-guide-expanded.txt:978:### 12.11 LoRA (2021): fine-tuning a big model cheaply
/workspace/gemma4/refs/notebooks/gemma-4-the-complete-guide-expanded/gemma-4-the-complete-guide-expanded.txt:982:**The method.** LoRA freezes the original weight matrix $W$ and learns a correction $\Delta W = BA$, where $B$ is $d \times r$ and $A$ is $r \times k$ with a small rank $r$ (4 to 128). Only $A$ and $B$ are trained. At the start $B = 0$, so the model begins exactly as the original. <span style="font-size:12px">[<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.09685">arXiv:2106.09685</a>]</span>
/workspace/gemma4/refs/notebooks/gemma-4-the-complete-guide-expanded/gemma-4-the-complete-guide-expanded.txt:986:<p><i>Figure 1 of the LoRA paper. The input passes through the frozen pretrained weights and, in parallel, through two narrow matrices A (initialised randomly) and B (initialised to zero); the two outputs are added.</i></p>
/workspace/gemma4/refs/notebooks/gemma-4-the-complete-guide-expanded/gemma-4-the-complete-guide-expanded.txt:990:**What it means for you.** Up to 8 adapters of rank up to 128 fit in the 3 GiB limit, and each agent can use its own. One detail explains a starter-kit surprise: the adapters in `sample_submission` have every `lora_B` equal to zero, so by the formula above $BA = 0$ and they change nothing. They demonstrate the file format and contain no training. Chapter 14.8 computes adapter sizes; Chapter 16 covers the open question of whether the scoring server loads adapters at all.
/workspace/gemma4/refs/notebooks/gemma-4-the-complete-guide-expanded/gemma-4-the-complete-guide-expanded.txt:1004:**What it means for you.** This is the most direct recipe for the “post-train Gemma 4” part of the competition: collect trajectories in an exact copy of the harness, keep the successful ones, fine-tune a LoRA adapter on them. The 129 public tasks are too few and too close to FastAPI and Rich to train on; they are better used to measure.
/workspace/gemma4/refs/notebooks/gemma-4-the-complete-guide-expanded/gemma-4-the-complete-guide-expanded.txt:1300:The speeds come from a participant’s measurements of vLLM 0.19.1 on 4× L4: a median of 12.2 tokens per second in a conservative serving mode and 31.4 with CUDA graph compilation. <span style="font-size:12px">[notebook “Gemma and the Shape of Doubt”, versions 8 and 10]</span> The scoring server’s exact configuration is not published.
/workspace/gemma4/refs/notebooks/gemma-4-the-complete-guide-expanded/gemma-4-the-complete-guide-expanded.txt:1335:### 14.8 How big a LoRA adapter is
/workspace/gemma4/refs/notebooks/gemma-4-the-complete-guide-expanded/gemma-4-the-complete-guide-expanded.txt:1337:**Formula.** For a weight matrix of size $d_{\text{out}} \times d_{\text{in}}$, a rank-$r$ LoRA adds $r\,(d_{\text{in}} + d_{\text{out}})$ trainable numbers.
/workspace/gemma4/refs/notebooks/gemma-4-the-complete-guide-expanded/gemma-4-the-complete-guide-expanded.txt:1343:The README estimates a rank-16 adapter on all linear layers at 110–220 MB, so up to 8 adapters fit in 3 GiB; the server allows up to 8 adapters of rank up to 128. <span style="font-size:12px">[HARNESS_README §3.4]</span> Size is not the constraint for LoRA here. Whether the scoring server loads adapters is (Chapter 16).
/workspace/gemma4/refs/notebooks/gemma-4-the-complete-guide-expanded/gemma-4-the-complete-guide-expanded.txt:1381:| the starter kit’s adapters | zero weights, and the model is addressed as `openai/main_lora`, which the server must know | leave adapters out until the organisers confirm they load |
