/workspace/gemma4/refs/notebooks/gemma-4-the-complete-guide-expanded/gemma-4-the-complete-guide-expanded.ipynb:1:{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"display_name":"Python 3","language":"python","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.12.13","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#0f1f33;padding:38px 36px;border-radius:14px;color:#ffffff\">\n<div style=\"font-size:12px;letter-spacing:3px;color:#9fb7d9\">KAGGLE · GOOGLE · GEMMA 4 DEVELOPER AGENT COMPETITION</div>\n<h1 style=\"color:#ffffff;margin:12px 0 8px 0;font-size:34px;line-height:1.15\">📘 Gemma 4 Developer Agent: The Complete Guide Expanded</h1>\n<p style=\"font-size:16px;color:#d6e1f0;margin:0\">Everything in one place, explained from the ground up: what a coding agent is, why companies want one that runs locally, how this competition scores it step by step, the research it builds on, what the data says, the maths behind the leaderboard, and the strategies worth trying.</p>\n<p style=\"font-size:13px;color:#9fb7d9;margin:16px 0 0 0\">State as of September 25, 2026 (day three of the competition). Sources: official competition pages, the forum, the organisers’ harness source code, public notebooks and research papers. Every fact carries its source next to it.</p>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## How to read this guide\n\n| Part | What you will understand after it |\n|:--|:--|\n| **I. The problem** | what a coding agent is, what problem the competition solves, where companies use such agents and why they want them local |\n| **II. The competition, mechanically** | what exactly you upload, what every setting does, how one task is scored from start to finish, what the agent sees on every turn |\n| **III. The research** | the dozen papers this field is built on, each explained: the problem, the method, the result and what it means for your agent |\n| **IV. The data** | what the 129 public tasks look like and what that implies for an agent, chart by chart |\n| **V. The maths** | how noisy the leaderboard is, where an agent loses its probability of success, how search, embeddings, context and time budgets work |\n| **VI. Practice** | results so far, traps that cost submissions, strategies from cheap to expensive, the paper track, rules |\n\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Part I. The problem\n\n## 1. What a coding agent is\n\nA chat model answers one message with one message. That is enough to explain a bug, but not to fix one in a real project. To fix a bug, a developer opens files, searches the codebase, runs the program, reads the error, edits a line, runs the tests again. None of that happens in a single reply.\n\nAn **agent** closes that gap by putting the model in a loop with tools. On every step the model sees everything that happened so far and chooses the next action: read a file, run a command, edit a line, or declare that it is done. A program outside the model (the harness) carries out the action and shows the result to the model. The model never touches the computer directly; it only writes requests.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Shared setup for the charts. This notebook does not submit anything: it is a guide.\nimport os, re, json, pathlib\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom matplotlib.patches import FancyBboxPatch\nfrom scipy.stats import binom\n\nBLUE, ORANGE, AQUA, YELLOW, MAG, RED = \"#2a78d6\", \"#eb6834\", \"#1baf7a\", \"#eda100\", \"#e87ba4\", \"#d03b3b\"\nINK, INK2, GRID, SURF = \"#0b0b0b\", \"#52514e\", \"#e6e5e0\", \"#ffffff\"\nplt.rcParams.update({\"figure.dpi\": 110, \"figure.facecolor\": SURF, \"axes.facecolor\": SURF, \"font.size\": 10, \"text.color\": INK,\n    \"axes.labelcolor\": INK2, \"xtick.color\": INK2, \"ytick.color\": INK2, \"axes.edgecolor\": GRID, \"axes.grid\": True, \"grid.color\": GRID,\n    \"axes.axisbelow\": True, \"axes.spines.top\": False, \"axes.spines.right\": False, \"axes.titleweight\": \"bold\",\n    \"axes.titlelocation\": \"left\", \"legend.frameon\": False})\nRC = {\"fastapi/fastapi\": BLUE, \"Textualize/rich\": ORANGE, \"psf/requests\": AQUA, \"encode/httpx\": YELLOW}\nREPOS = [\"fastapi/fastapi\", \"Textualize/rich\", \"psf/requests\"]\n\ndef find_data():\n    cands = [pathlib.Path(os.environ[\"GEMMA_AGENT_DATA\"])] if os.environ.get(\"GEMMA_AGENT_DATA\") else []\n    cands += [pathlib.Path(\"/kaggle/input/competitions/gemma-4-developer-agent\"), pathlib.Path(\"/kaggle/input/gemma-4-developer-agent\")]\n    return next((c for c in cands if (c / \"tasks.jsonl\").exists()), None)\n\ndef files_of(p):\n    out, cur = [], None\n    for l in (p or \"\").splitlines():\n        if l.startswith(\"+++ b/\"):\n            cur = {\"path\": l[6:].strip(), \"h\": [], \"n\": 0}; out.append(cur)\n        elif cur is not None and l.startswith(\"@@\"):\n            cur[\"h\"].append(l)\n        elif cur is not None and l[:1] in \"+-\" and not l.startswith((\"+++\", \"---\")):\n            cur[\"n\"] += 1\n    return out\n\nT = pd.DataFrame()\nDATA = find_data()\ntry:\n    if DATA is not None:\n        T = pd.read_json(DATA / \"tasks.jsonl\", lines=True)\n        if {\"patch\", \"problem_statement\", \"repo\"} <= set(T.columns):\n            T[\"problem_statement\"] = T[\"problem_statement\"].fillna(\"\")\n            T[\"fix\"] = T[\"patch\"].fillna(\"\").map(files_of)\n            T[\"lines\"] = T[\"fix\"].map(lambda f: sum(x[\"n\"] for x in f))\n            T[\"nf\"] = T[\"fix\"].map(len)\n        else:\n            T = pd.DataFrame()\nexcept Exception:\n    T = pd.DataFrame()\nprint(\"competition data:\", DATA if not T.empty else \"not attached, data charts will be skipped\", \"| tasks:\", len(T))","metadata":{"_kg_hide-input":true,"jupyter":{"source_hidden":true},"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-25T22:27:38.975962Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-25T22:27:38.976306Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-25T22:27:40.239043Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-25T22:27:38.976275Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-25T22:27:40.237831Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def canvas(w, h, xlim, ylim, title=None):\n    fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(w, h)); ax.set_xlim(*xlim); ax.set_ylim(*ylim); ax.axis(\"off\")\n    if title: ax.set_title(title)\n    return fig, ax\n\ndef rbox(ax, x, y, w, h, text, color, bold=False, fs=10, fc=\"#ffffff\", tc=None):\n    ax.add_patch(FancyBboxPatch((x, y), w, h, boxstyle=\"round,pad=0.02,rounding_size=0.12\", fc=fc, ec=color, lw=1.8))\n    ax.text(x + w / 2, y + h / 2, text, ha=\"center\", va=\"center\", fontsize=fs, color=tc or INK, fontweight=\"bold\" if bold else \"normal\")\n\ndef arr(ax, x1, y1, x2, y2, label=None, color=None, dx=0, dy=.15, style=\"-|>\", ls=\"-\", fs=9, rad=0):\n    ax.annotate(\"\", xy=(x2, y2), xytext=(x1, y1),\n                arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle=style, color=color or INK2, lw=1.5, linestyle=ls, connectionstyle=f\"arc3,rad={rad}\"))\n    if label: ax.text((x1 + x2) / 2 + dx, (y1 + y2) / 2 + dy, label, ha=\"center\", fontsize=fs, color=color or INK2)\nprint(\"diagram helpers ready\")","metadata":{"_kg_hide-input":true,"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-25T22:27:40.240683Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-25T22:27:40.241115Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-25T22:27:40.252094Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-25T22:27:40.240978Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-25T22:27:40.251028Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"fig, ax = canvas(13, 4.6, (0, 13), (0, 4.6), \"What an LLM agent is: a model in a loop with tools\")\nrbox(ax, .2, 1.6, 2.6, 1.3, \"Task\\n(GitHub issue)\", \"#8a8984\", fs=11)\nrbox(ax, 4.0, 1.6, 2.8, 1.3, \"LLM\\n(Gemma 4 31B)\", BLUE, True, 12)\nrbox(ax, 8.1, 3.0, 2.3, .95, \"Tools\\nread · edit · run\", ORANGE, fs=10.5)\nrbox(ax, 8.1, .6, 2.3, .95, \"Environment\\nrepo in a sandbox\", AQUA, fs=10.5)\nrbox(ax, 11.2, 1.6, 1.6, 1.3, \"Patch\\n(git diff)\", AQUA, True, 11)\narr(ax, 2.8, 2.25, 4.0, 2.25)\narr(ax, 6.8, 2.7, 8.1, 3.45, \"action:\\ntool call\", dy=.25)\narr(ax, 9.25, 3.0, 9.25, 1.55, \"executes\", dx=.6, dy=0)\narr(ax, 8.1, 1.05, 6.8, 1.8, \"observation:\\ntool result\", dy=-.55)\narr(ax, 10.4, 1.05, 11.2, 2.0, \"submit\", dy=-.1)\nax.text(5.4, 4.25, \"repeat: think → act → observe, until the model submits\", ha=\"center\", fontsize=10.5, color=INK2, style=\"italic\")\nplt.show()","metadata":{"_kg_hide-input":true,"jupyter":{"source_hidden":true},"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-25T22:27:40.25344Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-25T22:27:40.25387Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-25T22:27:40.594626Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-25T22:27:40.253831Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-25T22:27:40.59314Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**How to read the diagram.** The task enters the model on the left. The model and the tools exchange messages in a cycle: the model writes an *action* (a tool call), the tool acts on the *environment* (the repository inside a sandbox) and returns an *observation* (the result). When the model is satisfied, it submits, and the changes it made become a *patch*.\n\n**Why this matters for the competition.** The quality of an agent depends on two things: how well the model reasons, and how well the loop around it is designed: which tools exist, what they return, what the model is told, when it must stop. In this competition the model is fixed. Almost everything you control is the loop.\n\n## 2. The task the competition sets\n\nThe organisers describe the goal as follows: today’s strongest coding agents rely on huge models running in the cloud, which puts them out of reach for many developers. They want an agent built on an **open model that runs offline on everyday hardware** and can navigate complex codebases to draft fixes for real issues. <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[<a href=\"https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/gemma-4-developer-agent/overview\">Overview</a>, <a href=\"https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/gemma-4-developer-agent/discussion/743007\">organisers’ welcome post</a>]</span>\n\nPut concretely, each task in the competition is:\n\n1. a **real issue** from a Python project, written by a person (for example “Console.print crashes when the markup is empty”);\n2. the project’s **repository** frozen at the commit just before the real fix was made;\n3. a set of **hidden tests** written by the project’s maintainers together with the real fix.\n\nThe agent gets items 1 and 2. It has to produce a patch that makes item 3 pass. Nobody answers its questions, it has no internet, and it has a limited amount of time.\n\nThree requirements are hidden in this setup, and each one shapes the solution:\n\n- **A mid-size open model.** Gemma 4 31B, compressed to 4 bits, fits on four NVIDIA L4 cards together with its working memory. A model this size cannot hold every library’s internals in its head, so the agent’s method (how it searches, checks and edits) matters as much as the model’s knowledge.\n- **Full autonomy.** There is no human to clarify an ambiguous issue. The agent must decide what the issue means, where the bug is and when it is finished.\n- **Execution-based grading.** A patch that looks right but fails the tests scores exactly zero, and so does a patch that fixes the bug but breaks another test. Being careful is worth more than being clever.\n\n## 3. Why companies care, and why they want it local\n\n### What coding agents do in companies today\n\nThe pattern “assign an issue to an agent, receive a pull request” has become a product at several of the largest software companies. Looking at how they describe it tells you what a real deployment expects.\n\n- **GitHub Copilot coding agent.** A developer assigns a GitHub issue to Copilot exactly as they would assign it to a colleague. The agent starts a development environment on GitHub Actions, plans the work, writes the code, runs the tests and opens a pull request for review. If the reviewer leaves comments, it revises the pull request. GitHub’s own description of where it works best is precise: *low-to-medium complexity tasks in well-tested codebases*, such as adding features, fixing bugs, extending tests, refactoring and improving documentation. <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[<a href=\"https://github.blog/news-insights/product-news/github-copilot-meet-the-new-coding-agent/\">GitHub Blog</a>]</span>\n- **Google Jules.** An asynchronous agent built on Gemini 2.5 Pro. It clones the repository into a cloud virtual machine, works in the background while the developer does something else, writes tests, fixes bugs and opens pull requests. Every change still requires human review. Generally available since August 6, 2025. <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[<a href=\"https://blog.google/technology/google-labs/jules-now-available/\">Google Blog</a>]</span>\n- **Devin (Cognition).** Announced in March 2024 as an autonomous “AI software engineer”. Its technical report put a number on the idea: 13.86% of a random quarter of SWE-bench (79 of 570 real issues) resolved end to end, against 1.96% for the best model without an agent at the time. That comparison is what convinced the industry that the agent loop, and not only the model, drives results. <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[<a href=\"https://cognition.com/blog/swe-bench-technical-report\">Cognition</a>]</span>\n\nAll three follow the same workflow, and it is worth seeing it as a pipeline, because the competition measures only one stage of it:\n\n| Stage | Who does it | What can go wrong | Covered by this competition? |\n|:--|:--|:--|:--|\n| 1. A task appears in the tracker | a person | the description is vague or incomplete | yes: many task texts are short or templated |\n| 2. The agent reads the code and writes a fix | the agent | wrong file, wrong fix, fix breaks something else | **yes, this is what is scored** |\n| 3. Continuous integration runs the test suite | CI | tests fail | yes: hidden tests play this role |\n| 4. A developer reviews the pull request | a person | the fix is correct but ugly, risky or not what was wanted | no |\n| 5. Merge and deploy | a person | — | no |\n\nA resolve rate in this competition is therefore close to the share of an agent’s pull requests that would pass CI on the first try. That is the number that decides whether an agent saves a team time or wastes it.\n\n### Why run the agent locally\n\nEvery product above sends the company’s source code to a cloud model. For many organisations that is not acceptable or not affordable:\n\n- **Code may not leave the building.** Banks, defence contractors, healthcare and public-sector organisations often have security or regulatory rules that forbid sending source code to a third party. For them a cloud agent is not an option at any price.\n- **Air-gapped networks.** Some development environments have no internet access at all. The competition sandbox is air-gapped for the same reason: an agent that needs the network cannot be deployed there.\n- **Predictable cost.** A cloud model is paid per token, and an agent reads a lot of tokens per task. On thousands of routine tasks per month, a model running on the company’s own GPUs has a fixed cost that does not grow with every file the agent reads.\n- **Customisation.** An open model can be fine-tuned on the company’s own code, conventions and past fixes. A closed model can only be prompted.\n- **Independence.** A model you host does not change or disappear when a vendor updates its product.\n\nThe competition’s constraints mirror this deployment: one open model, fixed hardware (4× L4, a common data-centre card), no network, a fixed time budget per job, and a winner licence that allows commercial use. A solution that does well here transfers almost directly into an internal company tool.\n\n### The economics in one formula\n\nA company does not pay for attempts, it pays for solved tasks. If one attempt costs $c$ (GPU time or tokens) and the agent solves a share $p$ of tasks, the expected cost of one solved task is:\n\n$$\\text{cost per solved task} = \\frac{c}{p}$$\n\nReviewing a proposed patch also costs developer time. If reviewing one attempt costs $r$ in the same units as compute, the total becomes $(c + r)/p$. The resolve rate sits in the denominator, so it dominates everything else.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Cost per SOLVED task falls as 1/p. Units are relative: the cost of one attempt = 1.\np = np.linspace(.03, .6, 200)\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(12, 3.8))\nax[0].plot(p, 1 / p, color=BLUE, lw=2)\nfor q in (.06, .12, .24, .48):\n    ax[0].plot([q], [1 / q], \"o\", color=INK, ms=5); ax[0].text(q + .01, 1 / q + .8, f\"p={q:.2f}: {1/q:.1f} attempts\", fontsize=9)\nax[0].set_xlabel(\"resolve rate p\"); ax[0].set_ylabel(\"attempts paid per solved task\"); ax[0].set_ylim(0, 22)\nax[0].set_title(\"Every doubling of p halves the cost per solved task\")\nr = np.linspace(0, 3, 100)                     # review cost of one proposed patch, relative to its compute cost\nfor pp, c in ((.12, ORANGE), (.24, BLUE), (.48, AQUA)):\n    ax[1].plot(r, (1 + r) / pp, color=c, lw=2, label=f\"p = {pp}\")\nax[1].set_xlabel(\"review cost per attempt r (compute cost = 1)\"); ax[1].set_ylabel(\"total cost per solved task\")\nax[1].legend(); ax[1].set_title(\"Cost per solved task = (1 + r) / p\")\nplt.tight_layout(); plt.show()","metadata":{"_kg_hide-input":true,"jupyter":{"source_hidden":true},"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-25T19:25:27.804241Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-25T19:25:27.804522Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-25T19:25:28.069404Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-25T19:25:27.804497Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-25T19:25:28.0687Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**How to read the charts.** The left chart shows how many attempts you pay for, on average, to get one solved task, as a function of the resolve rate $p$. The right chart adds review cost: each line is one resolve rate, and the horizontal axis is how expensive it is to review one proposed patch compared with the compute that produced it.\n\n**What they show.** At $p = 0.12$ (today’s best public score) a company pays for about 8 attempts per solved task; at $p = 0.24$ it pays for 4. The curves are steep on the left: small improvements at low resolve rates change the cost a lot. When review is expensive (right chart, large $r$), a low resolve rate becomes very costly, because people spend time on patches that turn out to be wrong.\n\n**Why it matters for you.** This is why the competition scores only resolved tasks and gives no partial credit, and why an agent that knows when it is *not* sure (and says so, or keeps its patch minimal) is valuable in practice.\n\n### Where the field stands\n\nProgress on real-issue benchmarks has been fast. The chart below collects results reported in the papers discussed in Part III.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"pts = [(\"Claude 2 + BM25\\n(SWE-bench, 2023)\", 1.96, \"#8a8984\"), (\"SWE-agent + GPT-4 Turbo\\n(SWE-bench, 2024)\", 12.47, BLUE),\n       (\"Devin\\n(25% subset, 2024)\", 13.86, BLUE), (\"SWE-Gym 32B + verifier\\n(Verified, 2024)\", 32.0, AQUA),\n       (\"SWE-smith 32B\\n(Verified, 2025)\", 40.2, AQUA), (\"SWE-RL 70B\\n(Verified, 2025)\", 41.0, AQUA)]\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(12, 3.8))\nb = ax.bar([p[0] for p in pts], [p[1] for p in pts], color=[p[2] for p in pts], width=.6)\nfor bb, (_, v, _) in zip(b, pts):\n    ax.text(bb.get_x() + bb.get_width() / 2, v + .8, f\"{v}%\", ha=\"center\", fontsize=9.5)\nax.set_ylabel(\"% of tasks resolved\"); ax.grid(axis=\"x\", visible=False); ax.tick_params(axis=\"x\", labelsize=8.5)\nax.set_title(\"Reported results over time (different benchmark variants, so compare trends, not bars)\")\nplt.tight_layout(); plt.show()","metadata":{"_kg_hide-input":true,"jupyter":{"source_hidden":true},"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-25T19:25:28.070253Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-25T19:25:28.070499Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-25T19:25:28.228009Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-25T19:25:28.070478Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-25T19:25:28.227378Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**How to read the chart.** Each bar is one published system with its reported share of resolved tasks. The benchmarks differ: the first three bars are on the original SWE-bench or a subset of it, the last three on SWE-bench Verified, a cleaner 500-task subset (Part III). Treat the chart as a trend, since the bars are not directly comparable.\n\n**What it shows.** In about two years, reported resolve rates went from under 2% to over 40%, and the systems on the right are open models of 32–70 billion parameters trained specifically for software engineering. The jump came from two sources: better agent loops (2024) and training on agent trajectories and with reinforcement learning (2024–2025).\n\n**Why it matters for you.** Gemma 4 31B is in the same size class as the open models on the right, but it has not been trained for this task, and it runs compressed to 4 bits in a 32K context. The gap between 0.12 on this leaderboard and 40% in the papers is the space the competition asks you to close.\n\n### Risks that businesses weigh\n\n- **Wrong patches that pass the tests.** A fix can satisfy the tests and still be wrong, for example by special-casing the tested input. Hidden tests reduce this risk in the competition; in a company, reviewers catch it.\n- **Security.** An agent runs commands. That is why it must live in a sandbox without network access and with limited memory and CPU, exactly as the competition’s harness does.\n- **Review burden.** Each proposed patch takes reviewer time. A low-precision agent can cost more time than it saves (the right chart above).\n- **Licensing.** The winning solution must be released under an open licence that allows commercial use, which is what makes the result usable by companies.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Part II. The competition, mechanically\n\n## 4. The facts on one page\n\n| Item | Value | Source |\n|:--|:--|:--|\n| Hidden test set | about 120 tasks from **private** repositories, split evenly between the public and the private leaderboard | [Data](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/gemma-4-developer-agent/data) |\n| Score | share of tasks whose hidden tests pass; each task is worth 0 or 1 | [Evaluation](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/gemma-4-developer-agent/overview/evaluation) |\n| Model | only `gemma-4-31b-it-qat-w4a16-ct`, for every agent and sub-agent | [Model Selection rules](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/gemma-4-developer-agent/overview/model-selection-budget-and-harness-rules) |\n| Hardware | 4× NVIDIA L4 (24 GB each), vLLM, context 32,768 tokens | HARNESS_README §3.1 |\n| Time | 12 hours for all tasks, sandbox setup included, verification excluded | Evaluation |\n| Task order | sequential, one task at a time | organiser’s answer, [forum 743063](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/gemma-4-developer-agent/discussion/743063) |\n| Submissions | 1 per day; select up to 2 for the final ranking | [Rules 2.2](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/gemma-4-developer-agent/rules) |\n| Team size | up to 5 | Rules 2.1 |\n| Prizes | 37,000 / 18,000 / 10,000 USD, plus 35,000 USD for the separate Paper Track | [Prizes](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/gemma-4-developer-agent/overview/prizes) |\n| Timeline | start Sep 23; papers due Nov 12; entry and team merger deadline Nov 25; final submission Dec 2, 2026 | [Timeline](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/gemma-4-developer-agent/overview/timeline) |\n| Winner licence | open source (OSI-approved), commercial use allowed | Rules 2.5 |\n\n## 5. What you upload\n\n### The archive\n\nYou upload a Kaggle notebook. When it runs, it must leave a file `submission.zip` in `/kaggle/working`. Inside the archive, one file is required and the rest are optional:\n\n```text\nsubmission.zip\n├── agent.yaml                     REQUIRED: the root agent\n├── configs/sampling.yaml          generation settings, pulled in with !include\n├── prompts/system.md              the root agent's instructions\n├── prompts/analyzer.md            a sub-agent's instructions\n├── sub_agents/code_analyzer.yaml  a second agent the first one can call\n├── eval_config.yaml               optional per-task limits (time, tool calls, turns)\n├── adapters/main_lora/            optional LoRA: adapter_config.json + adapter_model.safetensors\n└── skills/repo_navigation/        optional skill: SKILL.md + scripts/ + resources/\n```\n\nAllowed file types are `.yaml`, `.yml`, `.md`, `.txt`, `.py` (only as skill scripts), `.json` and `.safetensors`. The unpacked total must stay under 3 GiB. There is no Python entry point: you describe the agent, and the organisers’ compiler builds it. <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[Evaluation; HARNESS_README §2]</span>\n\n### Why YAML and not Python\n\nThe Agent Development Kit (ADK) can load an agent from a YAML file, but the standard loader may import arbitrary Python code named in the file. Running unknown code on a scoring machine is a security risk, so the organisers wrote their own compiler, `adk-submission`. It reads your YAML and allows references only to things it knows: the 9 harness tools, the one allowed model, your prompts and skills. Anything else is rejected before the model even starts. <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[HARNESS_README §2.1]</span>\n\nThe directive `!include path` pastes the content of another file in place. A `.md` or `.txt` file becomes a text string (handy for long prompts); a `.yaml` file is parsed as YAML (handy for shared settings). Paths are relative to the file that contains the directive, and paths that leave the archive (`..`, absolute paths, symlinks) are refused.\n\n## 6. Five complete example submissions\n\nThe five designs below go from simplest to most ambitious. The diagram shows how the pieces connect in each.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"fig, axs = plt.subplots(1, 5, figsize=(16, 4.4))\ndef g(ax, title, nodes, edges):\n    ax.set_xlim(0, 4); ax.set_ylim(0, 5); ax.axis(\"off\"); ax.set_title(title, fontsize=10.5)\n    for name, (x, y, c, b) in nodes.items():\n        rbox(ax, x - .9, y - .35, 1.8, .7, name, c, b, 9)\n    for a, b, lab in edges:\n        (x1, y1, *_), (x2, y2, *_) = nodes[a], nodes[b]\n        arr(ax, x1, y1 - .37 if y1 > y2 else y1 + .37, x2, y2 + .37 if y1 > y2 else y2 - .37, lab, fs=8, dx=.55, dy=0)\ng(axs[0], \"A. Single agent\", {\"coder\\n6 tools\": (2, 3.8, BLUE, True), \"sandbox\": (2, 1.2, AQUA, False)}, [(\"coder\\n6 tools\", \"sandbox\", \"tool calls\")])\ng(axs[1], \"B. Coder + analyzer\", {\"coder\": (2, 4.1, BLUE, True), \"code_analyzer\\n(read-only)\": (2, 2.4, ORANGE, False), \"sandbox\": (2, .7, AQUA, False)},\n  [(\"coder\", \"code_analyzer\\n(read-only)\", \"agent_tool\"), (\"code_analyzer\\n(read-only)\", \"sandbox\", \"search, read\")])\ng(axs[2], \"C. + reviewer\", {\"coder\": (2, 4.1, BLUE, True), \"analyzer\": (1, 2.4, ORANGE, False), \"reviewer\": (3, 2.4, MAG, False), \"sandbox\": (2, .7, AQUA, False)},\n  [(\"coder\", \"analyzer\", \"\"), (\"coder\", \"reviewer\", \"\"), (\"analyzer\", \"sandbox\", \"\"), (\"reviewer\", \"sandbox\", \"\")])\ng(axs[3], \"D. + skill (own script)\", {\"coder\": (2, 4.1, BLUE, True), \"skill: locate.py\": (2, 2.4, YELLOW, False), \"sandbox\": (2, .7, AQUA, False)},\n  [(\"coder\", \"skill: locate.py\", \"run_skill_script\"), (\"skill: locate.py\", \"sandbox\", \"runs inside\")])\ng(axs[4], \"E. + LoRA adapters\", {\"coder\\n+ main_lora\": (2, 4.1, BLUE, True), \"analyzer\\n+ tool_lora\": (2, 2.4, ORANGE, False), \"sandbox\": (2, .7, AQUA, False)},\n  [(\"coder\\n+ main_lora\", \"analyzer\\n+ tool_lora\", \"agent_tool\"), (\"analyzer\\n+ tool_lora\", \"sandbox\", \"\")])\nfig.suptitle(\"Five ways to wire an agent (all use the same base model)\", fontweight=\"bold\", x=.01, ha=\"left\")\nplt.tight_layout(); plt.show()","metadata":{"_kg_hide-input":true,"jupyter":{"source_hidden":true},"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-25T19:25:28.229132Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-25T19:25:28.229381Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-25T19:25:28.637036Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-25T19:25:28.229349Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-25T19:25:28.636156Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**How to read the diagram.** Each panel is one design. Boxes are LLM agents (all running the same Gemma model) or the sandbox; arrows show who calls whom. The bold box is the root agent, the one the harness talks to.\n\n### Example A. One agent with the core tools\n\nThe smallest thing that can score. One agent reads, edits, runs commands and submits.\n\n**`agent.yaml`**\n\n```yaml\n# agent.yaml\nname: swe_coder\nmodel: gemma-4-31b-it-qat-w4a16-ct        # the only allowed model\ndescription: Fixes one repository issue with a small, verified patch.\ninstruction: !include prompts/system.md   # the rules the agent follows\ngenerate_content_config: !include configs/sampling.yaml\ntools:\n  - run_command      # shell commands inside /workspace\n  - read_file        # read up to 150 lines of a file\n  - edit_file        # replace a fragment of a file\n  - write_file       # create a file\n  - get_status       # remaining budget (free)\n  - submit_patch     # freeze the answer (free)\n```\n\n**`configs/sampling.yaml`**\n\n```yaml\n# configs/sampling.yaml\ntemperature: 0.2          # low randomness: tool calls stay well-formed\ntop_p: 0.95\ntop_k: 40\nmax_output_tokens: 8192   # upper bound for one model reply\nthinking_config:\n  include_thoughts: false # the model does not reason before replying (see \"the thinking switch\" below)\n```\n\n**What happens at run time.** The harness sends the model your system prompt, the tool descriptions and the task. The model calls tools one after another until it calls `submit_patch`. Every file it reads stays in its history, so on a large repository the 32K context fills up quickly.\n\n**Pros:** simple, few failure points. **Cons:** exploration and editing share one context. Public result of a design close to this: 0.06 (“Black Cat SWE Agent”, which also used the graph tools).\n\n### Example B. Coder plus a read-only analyzer\n\nThis is the structure of the best public notebook (0.12). The coder keeps its context for editing and delegates the search to a second agent.\n\n**`agent.yaml`**\n\n```yaml\n# agent.yaml\nname: swe_coder\nmodel: gemma-4-31b-it-qat-w4a16-ct\ninstruction: !include prompts/system.md\ngenerate_content_config: !include configs/sampling.yaml\ntools:\n  - run_command\n  - read_file\n  - edit_file\n  - write_file\n  - get_status\n  - submit_patch\n  - agent_tool:                                 # the analyzer becomes a tool the coder can call\n      config_path: sub_agents/code_analyzer.yaml\n      skip_summarization: true                  # return the analyzer's answer as is\n```\n\n**`sub_agents/code_analyzer.yaml`**\n\n```yaml\n# sub_agents/code_analyzer.yaml\nname: code_analyzer\nmodel: gemma-4-31b-it-qat-w4a16-ct\ndescription: Read-only code navigator. Given an issue, returns the location, root cause and a fix plan.\ninstruction: !include ../prompts/analyzer.md\ngenerate_content_config: !include ../configs/sampling.yaml\ntools:                      # no edit tools: it can only look\n  - run_command\n  - read_file\n  - search_similar_code\n  - get_code_neighbors\n  - get_code_subgraph\n```\n\nThe analyzer’s prompt asks for a fixed answer format, for example:\n\n```text\nLOCATION: rich/console.py:1650-1700 (Console.print)\nROOT CAUSE: empty markup string reaches render() and indexes an empty list\nFIX PLAN: return early when the rendered text is empty\nTESTS: tests/test_console.py\nCONFIDENCE: medium\n```\n\n**What happens at run time.** The coder calls `code_analyzer` like any tool, passing a short request. The analyzer starts with a **fresh context**, runs its own tool calls and returns only its final report. All the file contents it read stay in its context and never reach the coder. Part III (“Lost in the Middle”) explains why that helps.\n\n**Pros:** the coder’s context stays small and focused. **Cons:** two agents share one time budget; the analyzer may be confidently wrong, so the coder must verify its claim by reading the reported lines.\n\n### Example C. Adding a reviewer before submission\n\nScoring is binary, so a patch that fixes three of four requested behaviours is worth nothing. A read-only reviewer can check the diff against the task before the coder submits.\n\n**`addition to agent.yaml tools`**\n\n```yaml\n# addition to agent.yaml tools\n  - agent_tool:\n      config_path: sub_agents/reviewer.yaml\n      skip_summarization: true\n```\n\n**`sub_agents/reviewer.yaml`**\n\n```yaml\n# sub_agents/reviewer.yaml\nname: reviewer\nmodel: gemma-4-31b-it-qat-w4a16-ct\ndescription: Checks a proposed diff against the issue. Reports missing requirements and stray files.\ninstruction: !include ../prompts/reviewer.md      # can use {problem_description} to see the full issue\ngenerate_content_config: !include ../configs/sampling.yaml\ntools:\n  - run_command     # read-only use: git diff, git status, targeted tests\n  - read_file\n```\n\n**Pros:** catches partial fixes and junk files. **Cons:** costs one or two more model calls per task. Not yet measured on the leaderboard.\n\n### Example D. A skill: your own Python script inside the sandbox\n\nA skill is the only way to ship code of your own. It is a folder with a `SKILL.md` manifest, optional scripts and optional reference files. The agent reads the manifest, then runs a script through the `run_skill_script` tool; the script runs inside the task’s sandbox with access to the repository and uses the task’s time budget. <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[Model Selection rules; HARNESS_README §2]</span>\n\n```text\nskills/repo_locator/\n├── SKILL.md\n└── scripts/locate.py\n```\n\n```markdown\n---\nname: repo-locator\ndescription: Ranks source files and functions against the issue text. Use once at the start.\n---\nCall run_skill_script with skill_name=\"repo-locator\", file_path=\"scripts/locate.py\"\nand args [\"--root\", \"/workspace\", \"--query\", \"<identifiers and error text from the issue>\"].\nIt prints the top 5 files with the best-matching functions and their line numbers.\n```\n\n**`in agent.yaml`**\n\n```yaml\n# in agent.yaml\nskills:\n  - skills/repo_locator\n```\n\n**Pros:** deterministic search in one call, can cover what the graph tools miss (for example async functions). **Cons:** no public submission with a score has used a skill yet; the script must be fast and robust because it runs inside every task.\n\n### Example E. LoRA adapters and per-task limits\n\nLoRA adapters are small trained add-ons to the model (Part III explains how they work). Each agent can use a different one.\n\n```text\nadapters/main_lora/adapter_config.json\nadapters/main_lora/adapter_model.safetensors\n```\n\n**`in agent.yaml`**\n\n```yaml\n# in agent.yaml\nmodel: gemma-4-31b-it-qat-w4a16-ct\nadapter: main_lora        # this agent's requests go to the model with this adapter applied\n```\n\n**`eval_config.yaml`**\n\n```yaml\n# eval_config.yaml: the scorer reads exactly these four fields\nevaluation:\n  max_time_minutes: 8     # wall-clock limit for the agent on one task\n  max_tool_calls: 60      # tool calls per task (submit_patch and get_status are free)\n  max_turns: 80           # model calls per task\n  timeout_seconds: 300    # limit for one shell command\n```\n\n**Status on September 25:** the starter kit ships adapters, and it fails to score; an organiser is preparing a fix (Chapter 16). The starter kit’s own `eval_config.yaml` sets 1 minute and 10 tool calls per task, which is far too little to fix anything. The scoring notebooks ship no `eval_config.yaml` at all.\n\n## 7. Every setting explained\n\n### Agent fields\n\n| Field | What it does | Detail that matters |\n|:--|:--|:--|\n| `name` | identifier of the agent | also the tool name when the agent is used through `agent_tool` |\n| `model` | which model serves the agent | must be `gemma-4-31b-it-qat-w4a16-ct` everywhere |\n| `description` | one-line summary of the agent | the parent agent reads it to decide when to call this one, so write it as an instruction |\n| `instruction` | the system prompt | ADK replaces `{problem_description}` with the task text; any other `{word}` in braces causes an error at run time |\n| `tools` | tool names, and `agent_tool` entries for sub-agents | only the 9 harness tools, sub-agents and skills are allowed |\n| `agent_tool.skip_summarization` | whether the parent’s model re-summarises the sub-agent’s answer | `true` returns the report unchanged and ends the coder’s current turn; the harness then prompts it to continue |\n| `sub_agents` | agents to hand control over to (transfer), as opposed to calling them as tools | control does not come back automatically; `agent_tool` is the usual choice here |\n| `skills` | skill folders | scripts run inside the sandbox |\n| `adapter` | LoRA adapter name | the agent’s model address becomes `openai/<adapter>` on the vLLM server |\n| `include_contents` | `default` or `none` | `none` hides the conversation history from this agent |\n| `output_key` | save the agent’s final answer in the session state | later agents can read it as `{output_key}` |\n| `agent_class` | `LlmAgent` (default), `SequentialAgent`, `ParallelAgent`, `LoopAgent` | fixed pipelines are possible: a `SequentialAgent` could run “locate → fix → review” in order |\n\n<span style=\"font-size:12px\">[HARNESS_README §2.3; `adk_submission` source]</span>\n\n### Generation settings (`generate_content_config`)\n\nA language model does not output one fixed word. At every step it computes a probability for every possible next token and then samples one. The generation settings control that sampling and the length of replies.\n\n| Setting | What it does | Typical values |\n|:--|:--|:--|\n| `temperature` | sharpens or flattens the probabilities before sampling. Near 0 the model almost always picks the most likely token (predictable, can get stuck repeating itself); at 1 it samples from its natural distribution (more varied, occasionally sloppy) | 0.2 in every scoring notebook; 1.0 recommended by Google for Gemma 4 |\n| `top_k` | sample only among the *k* most likely tokens | 40 (scoring notebooks), 64 (Google’s recommendation) |\n| `top_p` | sample only among the smallest set of tokens whose probabilities add up to *p* | 0.95 |\n| `max_output_tokens` | the longest reply the model may write in one call (reasoning included) | 8,192; the limit is 32,768 |\n| `stop_sequences`, penalties | stop strings, repetition penalties | rarely needed |\n| `seed` | random seed | accepted by the compiler but **not sent** to the model, so runs are not reproducible |\n| `thinking_config` | whether the model reasons before replying | see below |\n\nChapter 14.7 shows the maths of temperature and a research result on which temperature is best when only one attempt counts.\n\n### The thinking switch\n\nGemma 4 can “think”: before its visible reply it writes a hidden chain of reasoning. It sounds like an obvious win, but in this harness it behaves differently from what the settings suggest. The behaviour is visible only in the compiler’s code (`adk_submission/resolvers/generation.py`):\n\n| You write | What vLLM receives | What happens |\n|:--|:--|:--|\n| `include_thoughts: false` (with any `thinking_budget`) | `enable_thinking: false` | **no reasoning at all**; this is what every scoring notebook uses |\n| `include_thoughts: true` | `enable_thinking: true` | reasoning on, limited only by `max_output_tokens` |\n| `thinking_budget: 4096` | nothing | the budget is validated but never sent to the model |\n| `thinking_level: HIGH` | `reasoning_effort: high` | participants report that LiteLLM in the Kaggle image rejects it for this model |\n\nWhy this matters: at the generation speeds measured on 4× L4 (12–31 tokens per second, Chapter 14.6), 4,000 tokens of reasoning take 2–5 minutes, and the average time available per task is 6 minutes. One participant’s local run with thinking on resolved 0 of 4 tasks, all four hitting the time limit; the next version without thinking resolved 2 of 4 (Chapter 15).\n\n## 8. How one task is scored, step by step","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"fig, ax = canvas(15, 5.6, (0, 15), (0, 5.6), \"What happens after you press Submit\")\nlanes = [(\"Kaggle\", 4.6, \"#8a8984\"), (\"Scorer + vLLM\", 3.5, BLUE), (\"Container A (agent)\", 2.4, ORANGE), (\"Container B (tests)\", 1.3, AQUA)]\nfor name, y, c in lanes:\n    ax.plot([2.2, 14.8], [y, y], color=GRID, lw=1); ax.text(.05, y, name, va=\"center\", fontsize=10, fontweight=\"bold\", color=c)\nsteps = [(0, 2.3, 4.6, \"re-run your\\nnotebook\"), (0, 3.9, 4.6, \"take\\nsubmission.zip\"),\n         (1, 5.5, 3.5, \"compile YAML\\n(adk-submission)\"), (1, 7.1, 3.5, \"start vLLM\\nGemma 4 31B\"),\n         (2, 8.7, 2.4, \"setup repo,\\nbaseline commit\"), (2, 10.3, 2.4, \"agent loop\\n(tools, turns)\"), (2, 11.9, 2.4, \"extract\\ngit diff\"),\n         (3, 13.5, 1.3, \"apply patch +\\nhidden tests,\\npytest → 0/1\")]\nfor i, (lane, x, y, t) in enumerate(steps):\n    c = lanes[lane][2]; rbox(ax, x - .7, y - .42, 1.4, .84, t, c, fs=8.2)\n    if i:\n        px, py = steps[i - 1][1], steps[i - 1][2]\n        arr(ax, px + .7, py, x - .7, y)\nax.annotate(\"\", xy=(8.0, .45), xytext=(14.3, .45), arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle=\"-|>\", color=RED, lw=1.4, connectionstyle=\"arc3,rad=0\"))\nax.text(11.2, .15, \"repeat for every hidden task, one at a time (~120 tasks, 12 hours in total)\", ha=\"center\", fontsize=9.5, color=RED)\nax.text(14.9, 3.5, \"score =\\nshare of\\ntasks with\\nexit code 0\", ha=\"right\", va=\"center\", fontsize=9, color=INK2)\nplt.show()","metadata":{"_kg_hide-input":true,"jupyter":{"source_hidden":true},"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-25T19:25:28.637891Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-25T19:25:28.63815Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-25T19:25:28.794504Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-25T19:25:28.638126Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-25T19:25:28.793799Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**How to read the diagram.** Each horizontal lane is one participant in the process, and time flows left to right. The red arrow at the bottom means the last four steps repeat for every hidden task.\n\nThe full sequence, from the organisers’ code (`swegemma/harness/agent_runner.py`, `verification.py`, `submission.py`):\n\n1. **Kaggle re-runs your notebook** in the scoring environment, with the hidden data mounted in place of the public data.\n2. **The scorer finds your agent.** It looks for `agent.yaml` in the submission, in `/kaggle/working` and in `/kaggle/tmp` (recursively) and picks the shortest path. You never create `submission.parquet`; the scorer writes it.\n3. **Compilation.** `adk-submission` checks the archive (size, file types, one model, no path tricks) and builds the agent tree.\n4. **The model server starts:** vLLM with Gemma 4 31B on 4 GPUs.\n5. For **each task, one after another**:\n   1. A fresh container A starts. The repository snapshot is unpacked at the task’s commit (future git history removed), the project is installed in editable mode from offline wheels, and the harness writes its own `pytest.ini` and `conftest.py` and commits everything as `baseline`.\n   2. The agent loop runs (next section) until the agent submits, runs out of time or turns, or stops responding.\n   3. The patch is taken with `git add -N . && git diff HEAD`, even if the agent never called `submit_patch`.\n   4. A fresh container B starts with the same snapshot and setup, applies the patch (four fallback methods), resets every test file named in the hidden `test_patch`, applies the hidden tests and runs `PYTHONSAFEPATH=1 python3 -m pytest <targets> -p no:anyio`.\n   5. Exit code 0 means the task counts as resolved.\n6. The score is the number of resolved tasks divided by the number of tasks.\n\nThe two containers never share state, so the agent cannot see or influence the grading tests.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(13, 3.8)); ax.set_xlim(0, 13); ax.set_ylim(0.15, 3.6); ax.axis(\"off\")\ndef box(x, y, w, h, title, lines, color):\n    ax.add_patch(FancyBboxPatch((x, y), w, h, boxstyle=\"round,pad=0.02,rounding_size=0.12\", fc=\"#ffffff\", ec=color, lw=1.8))\n    ax.text(x + .2, y + h - .33, title, fontsize=11.5, fontweight=\"bold\", color=INK)\n    for i, l in enumerate(lines):\n        ax.text(x + .2, y + h - .74 - i * .33, l, fontsize=9.5, color=INK2)\nbox(.1, .3, 5.6, 3.15, \"Container A · the agent works\", [\"snapshot at base_commit (no history after it)\", \"pip install -e . from offline wheels\",\n    \"harness pytest.ini + conftest.py, git commit baseline\", \"agent loop: tools, 32K context\", \"no network · 4 GB RAM · 2 vCPU · 300 s per command\",\n    \"patch = git add -N . && git diff HEAD\", \"(taken even without submit_patch)\"], BLUE)\nbox(7.2, .3, 5.6, 3.15, \"Container B · verification\", [\"clean snapshot, same setup\", \"apply the agent's patch (4 attempts)\",\n    \"reset test files named in the hidden test_patch\", \"apply the hidden test_patch\", \"PYTHONSAFEPATH=1 python3 -m pytest <targets>\",\n    \"exit code 0 → task solved (1)\", \"anything else → 0\"], ORANGE)\nax.annotate(\"\", xy=(7.15, 1.85), xytext=(5.75, 1.85), arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle=\"-|>\", color=INK2, lw=1.6))\nax.text(6.45, 2.05, \"patch\", ha=\"center\", fontsize=10, color=INK2)\nax.set_title(\"Two phases of scoring for every task\"); plt.show()","metadata":{"_kg_hide-input":true,"jupyter":{"source_hidden":true},"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-25T19:25:28.795334Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-25T19:25:28.795542Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-25T19:25:28.910528Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-25T19:25:28.795524Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-25T19:25:28.909916Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**How to read the diagram.** The left box is everything that happens while the agent works; the right box is the independent check. The only thing passed between them is the patch.\n\n**What follows from it:**\n\n- **Editing tests never helps.** Every test file in the hidden `test_patch` is reset before the hidden tests are applied.\n- **Everything left in `/workspace` becomes part of the answer**, including a forgotten scratch script.\n- **Git history cannot reveal the fix.** Commits after the task’s base commit do not exist in the snapshot.\n- **There is no network**, so `pip install` only wastes time. All dependencies are preinstalled.\n- **`pytest.ini` and `conftest.py` at the root belong to the harness.** Changing them adds noise to the patch.\n\n**Why a patch fails even when the fix is right.** Typical causes seen in participants’ local runs: the patch does not apply in container B (for example because it touches generated files); another test in the same test file breaks; the new code imports a module that is not installed; a scratch file at the repository root is collected by pytest.\n\n## 9. The agent loop in detail\n\n### What the model sees on its first turn\n\nOn the first call the model receives your system prompt, the list of tools with their parameters, and a task message that the harness builds with `build_agent_prompt`. That message has up to seven parts:\n\n1. **Task header and problem statement:** “You are evaluating a software engineering task for repository {repo}. Problem Statement: …” followed by the issue text.\n2. **Hints**, only if the task has any (none of the 129 public tasks does).\n3. **Task budget:** the time, tool-call and turn limits in force.\n4. **Execution environment rules:** 300-second command timeout, 5,000-character output limit, 150-line and 10,000-character `read_file` limits, and a reminder that the environment is offline with all dependencies installed.\n5. **Standard instructions:** work only under `/workspace`, inspect conventions and tests before editing, verify before submitting, call `submit_patch` when done, and end with a short text reply.\n6. **Code-intelligence tools**, described only when a non-empty graph and embedding file exist for the task.\n7. **Workspace layout:** the first 150 lines of `find . -maxdepth 3`, so the agent already sees the repository structure.\n\n### What one turn looks like\n\nA turn is one run of the ADK runner. Inside it the model can call several tools, one after another:\n\n```text\nmodel → tool call      {\"name\": \"read_file\", \"args\": {\"filepath\": \"rich/console.py\", \"start_line\": 1640, \"end_line\": 1710}}\nharness → tool result  {\"status\": \"ok\", \"filepath\": \"rich/console.py\", \"content\": \"...\", \"start_line\": 1640, \"end_line\": 1710,\n                        \"total_lines\": 2600, \"is_truncated\": false}\nmodel → tool call      {\"name\": \"edit_file\", \"args\": {\"filepath\": \"rich/console.py\", \"old_string\": \"...\", \"new_string\": \"...\"}}\nharness → tool result  {\"status\": \"ok\", \"occurrences\": 1, \"strategy\": \"exact\", \"diff\": \"--- a/rich/console.py ...\"}\nmodel → text reply     \"Edited Console.print to return early on empty markup.\"\n```\n\nThe turn ends when the model replies with plain text. What happens next depends on whether it has submitted.\n\n### Nudges and the three rules that end a task\n\nIf a turn ends without `submit_patch`, the harness sends the model a short “continue” message, called a nudge. It picks one of three texts depending on how the turn ended:\n\n| How the turn ended | The nudge text (abridged) |\n|:--|:--|\n| the reply was cut off in the middle of a tool call | “Your previous response reached the token limit before the tool call finished closing … split the change into smaller incremental edits.” |\n| the reply hit the token limit while reasoning | “… keep reasoning under a few sentences and emit your next tool call immediately …” |\n| a normal text reply without submitting | “Please continue your work using the available tools, or call submit_patch when you have completed and verified your changes.” |\n\nA task ends when any of these happens:\n\n- the agent calls `submit_patch` (the loop stops right after that turn);\n- **three turns in a row contain no tool call** (the counter resets whenever a turn has at least one tool call);\n- the time limit, tool-call limit or turn limit (500 by default) is reached.\n\n<span style=\"font-size:12px\">[`agent_runner.py`, lines 498–739; HARNESS_README §5.3]</span>\n\nThe second rule is easy to trip over. A model that answers “I will now look at the console module” three times without actually calling a tool loses the task, even with plenty of time left. The system prompt should require a tool call in every reply until the patch is submitted.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(13, 3.9)); ax.set_xlim(0, 13); ax.set_ylim(0, 4); ax.axis(\"off\")\ndef node(x, y, w, h, text, color, bold=False):\n    ax.add_patch(FancyBboxPatch((x, y), w, h, boxstyle=\"round,pad=0.02,rounding_size=0.1\", fc=\"#ffffff\", ec=color, lw=1.7))\n    ax.text(x + w / 2, y + h / 2, text, ha=\"center\", va=\"center\", fontsize=10, color=INK, fontweight=\"bold\" if bold else \"normal\")\ndef arrow(x1, y1, x2, y2, label=None, dy=.14):\n    ax.annotate(\"\", xy=(x2, y2), xytext=(x1, y1), arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle=\"-|>\", color=INK2, lw=1.4))\n    if label: ax.text((x1 + x2) / 2, (y1 + y2) / 2 + dy, label, ha=\"center\", fontsize=9, color=INK2)\nnode(.1, 2.8, 2.5, .9, \"task prompt\\n(issue · budget · tree)\", \"#8a8984\")\nnode(3.3, 2.8, 2.6, .9, \"model turn\", BLUE, True)\nnode(6.7, 2.8, 2.6, .9, \"submit_patch()\\npatch frozen\", AQUA, True)\nnode(10.1, 2.8, 2.8, .9, \"task ends\", AQUA)\nnode(3.3, .5, 2.6, 1.0, \"turn without a\\ntool call → nudge\", ORANGE)\nnode(6.7, .5, 2.6, 1.0, \"3 nudges in a row\\n→ task ends\", RED, True)\nnode(10.1, .5, 2.8, 1.0, \"time or 500 turns\\n→ task ends\", RED)\narrow(2.6, 3.25, 3.3, 3.25); arrow(5.9, 3.25, 6.7, 3.25, \"tool call\"); arrow(9.3, 3.25, 10.1, 3.25)\narrow(4.35, 2.8, 4.35, 1.5, \"no tool call\", dy=0); arrow(4.85, 1.5, 4.85, 2.8); arrow(5.9, 1.0, 6.7, 1.0)\nax.set_title(\"The agent loop in agent_runner.py\"); plt.show()","metadata":{"_kg_hide-input":true,"jupyter":{"source_hidden":true},"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-25T19:25:28.91182Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-25T19:25:28.912089Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-25T19:25:29.035117Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-25T19:25:28.912069Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-25T19:25:29.034413Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**How to read the diagram.** The top row is the normal path: the model calls tools until it submits. The bottom row is what happens when a turn has no tool call: a nudge, and after three in a row the task ends. The box on the right shows the hard limits.\n\n### How the context fills up, and compaction\n\nEvery tool result stays in the conversation history, and the whole history is sent to the model on every call. The 32,768-token window has to hold the system prompt, the tool descriptions, the task, the history and room for the next reply. To stop long sessions from overflowing, the harness turns on ADK’s *event compaction*: every 15 events, the older part of the history is replaced by a summary, and the last 5 events stay in full. <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[HARNESS_README §7.2]</span>","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Illustrative simulation of how the coder's 32K context fills up. Numbers are assumptions, not measurements.\nrng = np.random.default_rng(3)\nbase = 700 + 900 + 2500          # system prompt + tool schemas + task prompt (with repository tree)\nreserve = 8192                   # max_output_tokens reserved for the next answer\nlimit = 32768\nkinds = rng.choice([\"read_file\", \"run_command\", \"edit_file\", \"analyzer report\"], size=26, p=[.4, .35, .15, .1])\ncost = {\"read_file\": (1500, 2800), \"run_command\": (300, 1400), \"edit_file\": (250, 700), \"analyzer report\": (350, 500)}\nhist, used, marks = [], [], []\nfor t, k in enumerate(kinds, 1):\n    hist.append(rng.integers(*cost[k]) + rng.integers(80, 250))   # tool result + the model's own call\n    if t % 15 == 0:                                               # compaction: fold older events into a summary\n        hist = [1200] + hist[-5:]; marks.append(t)\n    used.append(base + sum(hist))\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(12, 3.9))\nax.fill_between(range(1, len(used) + 1), 0, base, color=GRID, label=\"fixed part: system prompt, tool schemas, task\")\nax.plot(range(1, len(used) + 1), used, color=BLUE, lw=2, marker=\"o\", ms=4, label=\"context used before the next model call\")\nax.axhline(limit - reserve, color=ORANGE, ls=\"--\", lw=1.2); ax.text(1, limit - reserve + 400, \"32,768 − 8,192 reserved for the answer\", fontsize=9, color=ORANGE)\nax.axhline(limit, color=RED, lw=1.2); ax.text(1, limit + 400, \"hard limit 32,768\", fontsize=9, color=RED)\nfor m in marks:\n    ax.annotate(\"compaction\", xy=(m, used[m - 1]), xytext=(m + .3, used[m - 1] + 5000), fontsize=9, color=INK2, arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle=\"->\", color=INK2))\nax.set_xlabel(\"turn\"); ax.set_ylabel(\"tokens\"); ax.set_ylim(0, 36000); ax.legend(loc=\"lower right\", ncol=1, framealpha=1)\nax.set_title(\"Illustration: how the coder's context fills up and when compaction kicks in\"); plt.show()","metadata":{"_kg_hide-input":true,"jupyter":{"source_hidden":true},"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-25T19:25:29.0375Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-25T19:25:29.03776Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-25T19:25:29.199509Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-25T19:25:29.037713Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-25T19:25:29.1988Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**How to read the chart.** This is a simulation with assumed sizes (a file read costs 1,500–2,800 tokens, a command output 300–1,400, and so on), not a measurement. The grey band is the fixed part that is sent every time. The blue line is the total context before each model call. The dashed line is where the context meets the space reserved for the reply, and the red line is the hard limit.\n\n**What it shows.** Reading files is what fills the context: about seven full-size reads use up the free space. Compaction pulls the line back down, but the summary replaces exact text with a description of it.\n\n**Why it matters for you.** Two practical rules follow. Keep file reads narrow (a range of lines, not whole files), or delegate reading to a sub-agent whose context is separate. And after a compaction, re-read the exact lines before calling `edit_file`, because `edit_file` needs the text character for character.\n\n### A healthy trajectory\n\nThe illustration below shows the shape of a good run on a small bug. It is constructed for explanation, not taken from a log.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"steps = [(\"turn 1\", \"code_analyzer\", \"“find where empty markup is printed”\", ORANGE),\n         (\"\", \"→ report\", \"LOCATION rich/console.py:1650-1700 …\", ORANGE),\n         (\"turn 2\", \"read_file\", \"rich/console.py, lines 1640-1710\", BLUE),\n         (\"turn 3\", \"run_command\", \"cat > /tmp/repro.py … ; python /tmp/repro.py\", BLUE),\n         (\"turn 4\", \"edit_file\", \"old_string → new_string (3 lines)\", BLUE),\n         (\"turn 5\", \"run_command\", \"python -m py_compile rich/console.py && python /tmp/repro.py\", BLUE),\n         (\"turn 6\", \"run_command\", \"PYTHONSAFEPATH=1 python3 -m pytest tests/test_console.py -q\", BLUE),\n         (\"turn 7\", \"run_command\", \"git status --short && git diff\", BLUE),\n         (\"turn 8\", \"submit_patch\", \"patch frozen, session ends after this turn\", AQUA)]\nfig, ax = canvas(14, 5.2, (0, 14), (0, len(steps) + .6), \"Illustration: a healthy trajectory for one task (tool calls in order)\")\nfor i, (turn, tool, what, c) in enumerate(steps):\n    y = len(steps) - i - .2\n    ax.text(.1, y, turn, fontsize=9.5, color=INK2, va=\"center\")\n    rbox(ax, 1.2, y - .3, 2.4, .6, tool, c, True, 9.5)\n    ax.text(3.9, y, what, fontsize=9.5, va=\"center\", family=\"monospace\", color=INK)\nplt.show()","metadata":{"_kg_hide-input":true,"jupyter":{"source_hidden":true},"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-25T19:25:29.200381Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-25T19:25:29.200608Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-25T19:25:29.345478Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-25T19:25:29.200589Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-25T19:25:29.344676Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**How to read it.** Each row is one tool call in order. Orange rows belong to the analyzer, blue to the coder, green is the final submission.\n\n**What makes it healthy:** it localises first (analyzer report, then reading exactly the reported lines), reproduces the bug with a scratch script in `/tmp`, makes one small edit, checks that the file still compiles, reruns the reproduction and the nearest test file the same way the grader does, looks at the final diff and only then submits. Eight turns, each with a tool call, well inside the time budget.\n\n## 10. The nine tools, with examples\n\n| Tool | What it does | Counts against the tool budget |\n|:--|:--|:--|\n| `run_command(command)` | runs `bash -c` in `/workspace` | yes |\n| `read_file(filepath, start_line, end_line)` | returns a range of lines | yes |\n| `edit_file(filepath, old_string, new_string, allow_multiple)` | replaces a fragment | yes |\n| `write_file(filepath, content)` | creates or overwrites a file, creating folders | yes |\n| `submit_patch()` | freezes the diff as the answer | **no** |\n| `get_status()` | remaining time, tool calls and turns | **no** |\n| `search_similar_code(query, k)` | nearest symbols by embedding similarity | yes |\n| `get_code_neighbors(node, edge_type, max_neighbors)` | callers and callees of a symbol in the code graph | yes |\n| `get_code_subgraph(nodes)` | the graph connecting a set of symbols | yes |\n\n<span style=\"font-size:12px\">[Model Selection rules; HARNESS_README §6; `swegemma/tools/*.py`]</span>\n\n**`run_command`.** Output (stdout and stderr) is cut at 5,000 characters. A command that runs longer than 300 seconds is stopped and returns a `TimeoutExceeded` error, but the session continues. A non-zero exit code comes back as `{\"status\": \"error\", \"error_type\": \"CommandError\", \"details\": {\"stdout\": …, \"stderr\": …, \"exit_code\": 1}}`, so the model sees the error text. The sandbox has `git`, `grep`, `find`, `sed`, `awk` and Python 3.13; it does not have `rg` or `tree`.\n\n**`read_file`.** Returns at most 150 lines and 10,000 characters. The response includes `total_lines` and `is_truncated`, so the model knows whether there is more. Paths are relative to `/workspace`.\n\n**`edit_file`.** Looks for `old_string` in three ways, in this order:\n\n1. *exact:* the fragment appears character for character (Windows line endings are normalised first);\n2. *flexible:* the lines match after stripping leading and trailing spaces, and the new code is re-indented to fit;\n3. *regex:* tokens are matched with flexible whitespace around brackets, colons and operators.\n\nIf the fragment matches more than once and `allow_multiple` is false, the edit is refused. The table below shows what happens in common cases.\n\n| `old_string` passed by the model | Result |\n|:--|:--|\n| copied exactly, indentation included | applied |\n| indentation lost, fragment still unique | applied as a substring match, and the new code lands **without** indentation: the file may no longer compile |\n| a block with different indentation | applied by the flexible tier, re-indented correctly |\n| too short, found several times | refused as ambiguous |\n| contains line numbers copied from `read_file` output | not found |\n\nHence the rule: after every edit run `python -m py_compile <file>`.\n\n**`write_file` and paths outside the workspace.** The README says a leading `/` is stripped from paths. In the code (`swegemma 0.2.7`, `tools/workspace.py`) a path such as `/tmp/repro.py` is **rejected** with “Path traversal detected”. File tools only work inside `/workspace`. Scratch scripts belong in `/tmp`, so create them through `run_command`:\n\n```bash\ncat > /tmp/repro.py <<'EOF'\nfrom rich.console import Console\nConsole().print(\"\")\nEOF\npython /tmp/repro.py\n```\n\n**`submit_patch`.** Runs `git add -N .` (so new files are included) and `git diff HEAD`, stores the diff and marks the task as submitted. The loop ends after the current turn. It is free and can be called even when the tool budget is used up.\n\n**Graph tools.** They query a pre-built call graph and 256-dimensional embeddings of every function and class. The query for `search_similar_code` is matched against **symbol names** (for example `APIKeyHeader`), so a natural-language sentence returns noise. Chapter 13 shows what the graph covers and what it misses.\n\n## 11. What your notebook must do to be scored\n\nYour notebook runs twice: once when you save it, and again inside the scoring pipeline. A few facts about the second run matter:\n\n- The hidden `tasks.jsonl` has no `patch` or `test_patch` fields. Code that reads them without checking crashes the re-run.\n- Choose the output path by checking the folder (`Path(\"/kaggle/working\").exists()`). Environment variables can differ between a normal run and the scoring re-run.\n- Every scoring notebook so far leaves `submission.zip` in `/kaggle/working`, with `agent.yaml` at the archive root and no directory entries inside the zip. The best one also leaves an unpacked copy in `/kaggle/working/submission_bundle/`. The scorer searches `/kaggle/working` recursively for `agent.yaml`, so the unpacked copy does no harm.\n- Keep packaging independent of any data analysis, so a failing chart cannot stop the archive from being written.\n- The organisers’ compiler is available in the dataset [metric/gemma-4-developer-agent-wheelhouse](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/metric/gemma-4-developer-agent-wheelhouse). Compiling your archive with it before submitting catches YAML errors, bad `!include` paths, wrong model names and unknown tools, which saves submissions.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Part III. The research behind coding agents\n\n## 12. The papers, one by one\n\nEach paper below is explained in the same order: the problem it tackles, how the method works, what it achieved, and what it means for an agent in this competition. They are arranged so that each one builds on the previous.\n\n### 12.1 SWE-bench (2023): how to measure a coding agent at all\n\n**The problem.** Before 2023, code benchmarks asked models to write one short function from a description (for example HumanEval). Real software work is different: you change an existing, large codebase, and your change has to fit everything around it. There was no benchmark for that.\n\n**The method.** Jimenez and colleagues at Princeton built one from real history. They collected about 90,000 pull requests from 12 popular Python repositories and kept those that close a GitHub issue and add or change tests. Then they ran every candidate: the new tests must fail on the code before the fix and pass after it. What remained were 2,294 tasks. Each task gives the model the issue text and the repository before the fix; the model must produce a patch; the patch is judged by running the tests. <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.06770\">arXiv:2310.06770</a>, ICLR 2024]</span>\n\n\n<p><img src=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2310.06770v3/teaser.svg\" width=\"100%\"></p>\n<p><i>Figure 1 of the SWE-bench paper. An issue and a codebase go into the language model, which generates a pull request (a patch). The patch is checked by unit tests: tests marked with a cross in the “Pre PR” column failed before the fix and must pass after it; tests with a light tick already passed and must keep passing.</i></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2310.06770v3/collection-pipeline.svg\" width=\"100%\"></p>\n<p><i>Figure 2 of the SWE-bench paper: the three filters that turn raw pull requests into tasks. This competition’s organisers describe the same pipeline for their data (Chapter 13).</i></p>\n\nThe paper introduced two kinds of tests, and both decide pass or fail in this competition:\n\n- **FAIL_TO_PASS tests** fail before the fix and must pass after it. They prove the issue is resolved. SWE-bench has 9.1 of them per task on average.\n- **PASS_TO_PASS tests** already passed before the fix and must still pass. They prove nothing else broke. The median is 51 per task.\n\n**The result.** The best model of the time, Claude 2, given files chosen by the BM25 search algorithm (Chapter 14.3), resolved 1.96% of tasks. Given the files that the real fix touched (an “oracle”), it reached 4.8%.\n\n**What it means for you.** Finding where to edit is the hardest part, and even the right file inside a large context confuses a model. Every later system in this chapter is, in some way, an answer to that finding.\n\n### 12.2 SWE-bench Verified (2024): removing tasks that measure noise\n\n**The problem.** Some SWE-bench tasks cannot be solved from their description, or their tests reject correct solutions (for example a test checks an exact error message the issue never mentions). A benchmark full of such tasks underestimates every agent.\n\n**The method.** OpenAI ran a review campaign with professional developers who annotated SWE-bench samples for two defects: an under-specified problem statement, and tests that may unfairly reject a valid solution. Samples with problems were removed, and 500 verified tasks were kept. <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[<a href=\"https://openai.com/index/introducing-swe-bench-verified/\">OpenAI</a>]</span>","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(12, 3.2))\nvals = [(\"flagged: tests may reject a valid solution\", 61.1, ORANGE), (\"flagged: under-specified problem statement\", 38.3, YELLOW),\n        (\"filtered out in total\", 68.3, RED)]\nb = ax.barh([v[0] for v in vals][::-1], [v[1] for v in vals][::-1], color=[v[2] for v in vals][::-1], height=.55)\nfor bb, v in zip(b, [v[1] for v in vals][::-1]):\n    ax.text(v + 1, bb.get_y() + .27, f\"{v:.1f}%\", va=\"center\", fontsize=9.5, color=INK2)\nax.set_xlim(0, 80); ax.set_xlabel(\"% of SWE-bench test samples annotated\"); ax.grid(axis=\"y\", visible=False)\nax.set_title(\"SWE-bench Verified: what human annotators found (500 samples kept)\")\nplt.tight_layout(); plt.show()","metadata":{"_kg_hide-input":true,"jupyter":{"source_hidden":true},"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-25T19:25:29.346394Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-25T19:25:29.346639Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-25T19:25:29.462392Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-25T19:25:29.3466Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-25T19:25:29.461543Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**How to read the chart.** Each bar is the share of annotated SWE-bench samples that received a flag, as reported by OpenAI. A sample could get both flags, so the first two bars add up to more than the third.\n\n**What it means for you.** Benchmarks built from real history contain a layer of noise no agent can overcome. This competition’s organisers dealt with it differently: they ran every hidden task through a strong closed model and kept only tasks it solved or nearly solved (Chapter 13). So every hidden task is known to be solvable in principle.\n\n### 12.3 ReAct (2022): reasoning and acting in turns\n\n**The problem.** Language models reason well in text but invent facts they cannot check. Models that only act (call tools) without reasoning plan badly.\n\n**The method.** Yao and colleagues let the model alternate: a *thought* (free text about what to do), an *action* (a tool call), an *observation* (the tool’s result), then the next thought. Each thought is grounded in real observations. <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.03629\">arXiv:2210.03629</a>, ICLR 2023]</span>\n\n\n<p><img src=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2210.03629v3/teaser-new.svg\" width=\"85%\"></p>\n<p><i>Figure 1 of the ReAct paper. On a question-answering task (top) and a household task (bottom), answering directly, reasoning only (CoT) and acting only all fail; ReAct, which interleaves thoughts with actions and observations, succeeds.</i></p>\n\n**The result.** Interleaving beat both pure reasoning and pure acting on question answering, fact checking and interactive tasks.\n\n**What it means for you.** The agent loop in this competition is a ReAct loop. One twist: the harness switches the model’s hidden reasoning off by default (Chapter 7, “the thinking switch”), so the “thought” exists only as the text the model writes next to its tool call. Long text without a tool call counts as an empty turn, and three in a row end the task.\n\n### 12.4 SWE-agent (2024): the interface the model works through matters\n\n**The problem.** Early agents gave the model a raw Linux shell. People do not write code through a raw shell either; they use editors and IDEs that show the right amount of information and catch mistakes early.\n\n**The method.** Yang and colleagues designed an **Agent-Computer Interface (ACI)**: a small set of commands built for a language model. A file viewer shows 100 lines at a time with line numbers. Search commands return a short list of matches and ask for a narrower query when there are too many. An edit command replaces a range of lines and runs a linter; if the edit introduces a syntax error, it is rejected and the model is shown why. Old observations are collapsed so the history stays short. <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.15793\">arXiv:2405.15793</a>, NeurIPS 2024]</span>\n\n\n<p><img src=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.15793v3/swe_agent_overview.png\" width=\"100%\"></p>\n<p><i>Figure 1 of the SWE-agent paper. The LM agent talks to the computer (terminal and file system) through the Agent-Computer Interface: LM-friendly commands going out, LM-friendly feedback coming back.</i></p>\n\n**The result.** With GPT-4 Turbo, SWE-agent resolved 12.47% of the full SWE-bench (286 of 2,294) and 18.0% of SWE-bench Lite, against 3.8% for the best non-interactive method. The ablations are the most useful part for this competition:\n\n| What was changed | SWE-bench Lite | Counterpart here |\n|:--|:--|:--|\n| file viewer of 100 lines (baseline) | 18.0% | `read_file` returns at most 150 lines |\n| viewer of 30 lines / whole file | 14.3% / 12.7% | read ranges, not whole files |\n| edit command without the linter | 15.0% | `edit_file` has no linter, so run `py_compile` yourself |\n| no edit command at all (shell only) | 10.3% | `edit_file` exists; use it rather than `sed` |\n| full history instead of collapsing old observations | 15.0% | ADK compaction plays this role |\n\nOne more measurement: 90.5% of first edits eventually succeed, but after one failed edit the chance drops to 57.2%. A failed edit starts a chain of further mistakes.\n\n\n<p><img src=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.15793v3/edit_comparison.png\" width=\"100%\"></p>\n<p><i>Figure 6 of the SWE-agent paper. Left: without an edit command the model floods its context with cat output and gets no feedback from sed. Middle: an edit command without a linter lets a syntax error through. Right: with the linter, the broken edit is rejected with an explanation and the model fixes it.</i></p>\n\n**What it means for you.** The tools you are given are fixed, but you decide how they are used: read narrow ranges, keep command output short, compile after every edit, and after two failed edits re-read the exact lines and try a smaller fragment. The paper measured the effect of each of these habits.\n\n### 12.5 OpenHands (2024): the event stream behind every agent\n\n**The problem.** Every research group was building its own agent plumbing: sandboxes, action formats, histories. That made results hard to reproduce and compare.\n\n**The method.** OpenHands (formerly OpenDevin) is an open platform in which everything the agent does is an *event*: an action (run a command, edit a file, browse a page) or an observation (the result). The agent reads the event history and produces the next action; a runtime inside a Docker sandbox executes it. <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.16741\">arXiv:2407.16741</a>, ICLR 2025]</span>\n\n\n<p><img src=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2407.16741v3/oh_arch.png\" width=\"100%\"></p>\n<p><i>The OpenHands architecture. The agent turns the event history into the next action; the runtime (a Docker sandbox with a shell, a Python kernel and a browser) turns each action into an observation; all actions and observations are recorded in the event stream.</i></p>\n\n**What it means for you.** The competition harness has the same anatomy: ADK keeps the event history, the harness runtime executes tool calls inside a Docker sandbox, and compaction trims the history. Knowing that the history *is* the model’s memory explains why every tool result you allow into it has a cost.\n\n### 12.6 Agentless (2024): a fixed pipeline instead of a free agent\n\n**The problem.** Agents that decide every step themselves are expensive and hard to debug, and they sometimes wander.\n\n**The method.** Xia and colleagues at UIUC dropped the free-form agent and used a fixed three-phase pipeline. <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.01489\">arXiv:2407.01489</a>]</span>\n\n1. **Hierarchical localisation.** First the model picks suspicious files from the repository tree (combined with embedding-based retrieval). Then, for those files, it sees a “skeleton” (class and function signatures without bodies) and picks the relevant classes and functions. Finally it picks the exact lines to edit.\n2. **Repair.** The model generates several candidate patches in a search-and-replace format: one greedy, the rest sampled at a higher temperature.\n3. **Validation.** The model writes tests that reproduce the issue; candidates are run against these and against the repository’s existing tests, and the final patch is chosen by majority vote among the survivors.\n\n\n<p><img src=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2407.01489v2/overview.svg\" width=\"100%\"></p>\n<p><i>Figure 1 of the Agentless paper. Blue: localisation from the repository structure to files, then classes and functions, then edit locations. Purple: generation of several candidate patches. Green: reproduction tests, filtering and ranking, and the submitted patch.</i></p>\n\n**The result.** 32.0% on SWE-bench Lite (96 of 300) at an average cost of 0.70 USD per task, the best open-source result at the time. The authors also found that 4.3% of Lite tasks contain the solution in the description and 10% lack the information needed to solve them.\n\n**What it means for you.** You cannot ship a Python pipeline, but you can reproduce its structure: a read-only analyzer that localises top-down (files, then functions, then lines), a coder that reproduces the issue before editing, and a reviewer that checks the result. The idea of several candidate patches is limited here by time (Chapter 14.6).\n\n### 12.7 AutoCodeRover (2024): searching by program structure\n\n**The problem.** Text search finds strings, but code has structure: classes contain methods, methods call other methods.\n\n**The method.** AutoCodeRover gives the model search APIs that work on the syntax tree, such as `search_class`, `search_method_in_class` and `search_code_in_file`. The model calls them until it judges the context sufficient, then writes a patch; if the patch does not apply, it retries. When tests exist, *spectrum-based fault localisation* ranks methods by how often they run in failing versus passing tests. <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.05427\">arXiv:2404.05427</a>, ISSTA 2024]</span>\n\n\n<p><img src=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2404.05427v3/overall-workflow.png\" width=\"100%\"></p>\n<p><i>The AutoCodeRover workflow. Context retrieval: the model calls structural search APIs and analyses the results until the context is sufficient, which yields buggy locations. Patch generation: the model writes a patch and retries while it cannot be applied.</i></p>\n\n**The result.** 19% of SWE-bench Lite at 0.43 USD per task.\n\n**What it means for you.** Structural search is exactly what a skill can provide (Example D in Chapter 6): a script that parses the repository and returns classes and methods matching the issue, async functions included.\n\n### 12.8 RepoGraph (2025): a graph of the whole repository\n\n**The problem.** Search tools return isolated snippets; the model does not see how a function relates to its callers and dependencies.\n\n**The method.** RepoGraph parses the repository into a graph at the level of lines: nodes are definitions and references of functions and classes, edges record calls and containment. An agent gets a tool, `search_repograph`, that returns the neighbourhood of a symbol (its “ego-graph”) in a flattened, readable form. <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.14684\">arXiv:2410.14684</a>, ICLR 2025]</span>\n\n\n<p><img src=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2410.14684v2/main.png\" width=\"100%\"></p>\n<p><i>The RepoGraph overview. (a) Building the graph: code lines become definition and reference nodes connected by invoke and contain edges. (b) In a procedural pipeline, the graph search is inserted into each step. (c) In an agent, it becomes one more action.</i></p>\n\n**The result.** Added as a plug-in, it improved all four systems it was tested with on SWE-bench Lite.\n\n**What it means for you.** The organisers supply a graph of this kind for every task, with three tools to query it (Chapter 10). Chapter 13 shows its gaps (no async functions, one edge type). Building a better graph is a Paper Track topic of its own.\n\n### 12.9 Lost in the Middle (2023): why a full context is not a useful context\n\n**The problem.** Models advertise long context windows. Do they actually use all of it?\n\n**The method.** Liu and colleagues gave models 20 documents, one of which contained the answer, and moved that document from the first position to the last.\n\n**The result.** Accuracy follows a U-shape: high when the answer is at the start or the end, lowest in the middle, sometimes below the accuracy with no documents at all. <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03172\">arXiv:2307.03172</a>, TACL 2024]</span>\n\n\n<p><img src=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2307.03172v3/figure1.svg\" width=\"55%\"></p>\n<p><i>Figure 1 of “Lost in the Middle”. Accuracy of gpt-3.5-turbo as a function of the position of the document that holds the answer among 20 retrieved documents. The dashed line is the accuracy without any documents.</i></p>\n\n**What it means for you.** Every file the coder reads lands in the middle of its history, where the model attends worst. A sub-agent that reads in its own context and returns a short report places the useful information at the end of the coder’s history. This is the scientific case for the coder-plus-analyzer design.\n\n### 12.10 vLLM and PagedAttention (2023): why the context stops at 32K\n\n**The problem.** To generate text quickly, a model keeps the *keys and values* of attention for every token already processed (the KV cache). That cache grows with the context length and quickly fills GPU memory.\n\n**The method.** vLLM stores the KV cache in fixed-size blocks, the way an operating system stores memory in pages, and keeps a table of which blocks belong to which request. Memory is no longer wasted on fragmentation, so more requests fit on the same GPUs. <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.06180\">arXiv:2309.06180</a>, SOSP 2023]</span>\n\n\n<p><img src=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2309.06180v1/system-overview.png\" width=\"65%\"></p>\n<p><i>The vLLM system: a scheduler, a KV cache manager with block tables, and workers that each hold one shard of the model. In this competition there are four workers, one per L4 GPU.</i></p>\n\n**What it means for you.** Gemma 4 31B supports 256K tokens of context <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[<a href=\"https://huggingface.co/google/gemma-4-31B-it\">model card</a>]</span>, but on four 24 GB cards the weights take about 16–18 GB and the rest has to hold the KV cache and working buffers, so the harness sets the context to 32,768 tokens <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[HARNESS_README §3.1]</span>. That limit is a property of the hardware you are given, and every design decision about reading files and keeping history follows from it.\n\n### 12.11 LoRA (2021): fine-tuning a big model cheaply\n\n**The problem.** Fine-tuning all 31 billion weights needs several times the model’s size in GPU memory and produces a full copy of the model for every variant.\n\n**The method.** LoRA freezes the original weight matrix $W$ and learns a correction $\\Delta W = BA$, where $B$ is $d \\times r$ and $A$ is $r \\times k$ with a small rank $r$ (4 to 128). Only $A$ and $B$ are trained. At the start $B = 0$, so the model begins exactly as the original. <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.09685\">arXiv:2106.09685</a>]</span>\n\n\n<p><img src=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2106.09685v2/figure1.svg\" width=\"40%\"></p>\n<p><i>Figure 1 of the LoRA paper. The input passes through the frozen pretrained weights and, in parallel, through two narrow matrices A (initialised randomly) and B (initialised to zero); the two outputs are added.</i></p>\n\n**The result.** Quality comparable to full fine-tuning with a tiny fraction of trainable parameters, and adapters small enough to swap per task.\n\n**What it means for you.** Up to 8 adapters of rank up to 128 fit in the 3 GiB limit, and each agent can use its own. One detail explains a starter-kit surprise: the adapters in `sample_submission` have every `lora_B` equal to zero, so by the formula above $BA = 0$ and they change nothing. They demonstrate the file format and contain no training. Chapter 14.8 computes adapter sizes; Chapter 16 covers the open question of whether the scoring server loads adapters at all.\n\n### 12.12 SWE-Gym (2024): learning from the agent’s own successful runs\n\n**The problem.** To train a model to act as an agent, you need examples of good agent behaviour on real tasks with executable environments, and they were scarce.\n\n**The method.** Pan, Wang, Neubig and colleagues built 2,438 Python tasks, each with an executable environment and tests. They ran an agent many times on them and kept only the runs that solved the task (*rejection sampling*), then fine-tuned models on those successful trajectories. They also trained a *verifier*, a model that scores a finished trajectory, so that among several attempts the most promising one can be picked. <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.21139\">arXiv:2412.21139</a>, ICML 2025]</span>\n\n\n<p><img src=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2412.21139v2/openhands-fig1-scaling.svg\" width=\"60%\"></p>\n<p><i>Figure 1 of the SWE-Gym paper. Top: the share of SWE-bench Verified solved grows with the number of training trajectories (about 11% at ~120 trajectories, about 21% at ~490). Bottom: running the agent several times and letting the verifier choose raises the share further, from about 20% with one run to about 32% with 16.</i></p>\n\n**The result.** Up to 19 percentage points of absolute improvement from fine-tuning, and 32.0% on SWE-bench Verified with fine-tuned agents plus the verifier.\n\n**What it means for you.** This is the most direct recipe for the “post-train Gemma 4” part of the competition: collect trajectories in an exact copy of the harness, keep the successful ones, fine-tune a LoRA adapter on them. The 129 public tasks are too few and too close to FastAPI and Rich to train on; they are better used to measure.\n\n### 12.13 SWE-smith (2025): manufacturing training tasks\n\n**The problem.** Real issues with tests are limited. Training needs tens of thousands of tasks.\n\n**The method.** Yang, Lieret, Jimenez and colleagues turn any repository with tests into a task factory. First an agent sets up an execution environment for the repository. Then tasks are synthesised in four ways: a model rewrites a function to introduce a bug, procedural modifications break code mechanically, real pull requests are “mirrored” by undoing them, and several bugs are combined. A task is kept when it breaks existing tests. Strong models then solve the tasks, and their trajectories train a smaller model. <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.21798\">arXiv:2504.21798</a>]</span>\n\n\n<p><img src=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2504.21798v2/overview.png\" width=\"75%\"></p>\n<p><i>The SWE-smith overview: (1) turn a GitHub repository into an execution environment, (2) synthesise task instances with four methods, (3) collect expert trajectories on them and train a student model (SWE-agent-LM).</i></p>\n\n**The result.** 50,000 tasks from 128 repositories; SWE-agent-LM-32B trained on them reached 40.2% on SWE-bench Verified.\n\n**What it means for you.** If you train, generate tasks from repositories *other* than FastAPI, Rich, Requests and HTTPX, because the hidden tasks come from private repositories and you want the agent to generalise.\n\n### 12.14 SWE-RL and GRPO (2025): reinforcement learning with a simple reward\n\n**The problem.** Fine-tuning on examples teaches the model to imitate. Reinforcement learning (RL) lets it improve from its own attempts, but needs a reward signal, and running full test suites for every attempt is slow.\n\n**The method.** SWE-RL (Meta) uses a cheap rule-based reward: the textual similarity between the model’s patch and the real patch from the pull request, and −1 if the answer is malformed. Training uses **GRPO** (Group Relative Policy Optimization), introduced in the DeepSeekMath paper. For each task the model produces a group of answers; each answer’s *advantage* is its reward compared with the group’s average, divided by the group’s spread:\n\n$$A_i = \\frac{r_i - \\operatorname{mean}(r_1,\\dots,r_G)}{\\operatorname{std}(r_1,\\dots,r_G)}$$\n\nAnswers better than their siblings are reinforced, worse ones discouraged. Unlike the older PPO algorithm, no separate *value model* has to be trained to estimate how good an answer is, which saves the memory and compute such a model would need. <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.18449\">arXiv:2502.18449</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.03300\">arXiv:2402.03300</a>]</span>\n\n\n<p><img src=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2502.18449v2/DevLlama-swerl.svg\" width=\"100%\"></p>\n<p><i>The SWE-RL method: pull requests from GitHub become a seed RL dataset; the model reads the issue and code and writes reasoning plus a patch; the reward compares the predicted patch with the oracle patch (−1 for a wrong format); GRPO updates the weights.</i></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"https://arxiv.org/html/2402.03300v3/GRPO.svg\" width=\"100%\"></p>\n<p><i>PPO versus GRPO, from the DeepSeekMath paper. PPO trains a value model alongside the policy to compute advantages. GRPO samples a group of outputs for the same question and computes each output’s advantage from the group’s rewards, with no value model.</i></p>\n\n**The result.** Llama3-SWE-RL-70B reached 41.0% on SWE-bench Verified. The RL-trained model also improved on tasks outside software engineering (maths, general reasoning), while plain supervised fine-tuning made those worse.\n\n**What it means for you.** RL is the most ambitious route and needs serious compute. The reward can be as simple as patch similarity, or as faithful as running the tests in a copy of the harness.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Part IV. The data\n\n## 13. What the Data tab contains, and what it tells an agent designer\n\n### The files\n\n| File or folder | What is inside | What it is for |\n|:--|:--|:--|\n| `tasks.jsonl` | 129 public development tasks. Fields: `instance_id`, `repo`, `base_commit`, `problem_statement` (the issue text), `hints_text` (empty in all 129), `patch` (the real fix), `test_patch` (the grading tests), `created_at` | studying tasks, local validation |\n| `snapshots/*.tgz` | one archive per task: the repository at `base_commit`, about 21.5 GB in total; the `.git` folder has no later commits | running an agent locally |\n| `graphs/*.json` | a call graph per repository state: nodes are Python functions and classes with their full source, edges are calls | backs `get_code_neighbors` and `get_code_subgraph` |\n| `embeddings/*.npz` | a 256-number vector for every graph node | backs `search_similar_code` |\n| `wheels/` | 124 offline Python packages | installing projects without internet |\n| `docker/` | the sandbox image recipe (Python 3.13, git, pytest) and two compatibility shims for old test suites | reproducing the sandbox |\n| `sample_submission/` | the organisers’ starter agent | a format example |\n| `HARNESS_README.md` | 46 KB of harness documentation | the reference manual |\n\n<span style=\"font-size:12px\">[<a href=\"https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/gemma-4-developer-agent/data\">Data</a>]</span>\n\n### How the tasks were made\n\nThe organisers built the public and hidden sets with one pipeline <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[Data, “Dataset Generation & Verification Pipeline”]</span>:\n\n1. **Mining.** Commits and pull requests that change both functional code and its tests.\n2. **Linking.** Each change is matched with its original description: an issue, a discussion or a bug report.\n3. **Filtering.** Mechanical refactors, documentation-only changes, large automated changes and tasks without reproducible acceptance criteria are removed.\n4. **Execution checks.** Without the fix, the new tests must fail. With the fix, the full test suite must pass.\n5. **Hidden set only:** a stronger closed model must solve the task or stop within a single test of solving it.\n\nThe hidden set has about 120 tasks from **private** repositories. Two consequences follow. Every hidden task is known to be solvable. And the model cannot have seen these repositories during its training, while it may well have seen FastAPI and Rich; local results on public tasks can therefore flatter an agent.\n\n### The public tasks at a glance","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"if not T.empty:\n    fig, ax = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(12, 3.6), gridspec_kw={\"width_ratios\": [1, 1.2]})\n    vc = T[\"repo\"].value_counts()\n    b = ax[0].barh(vc.index[::-1], vc.values[::-1], color=[RC.get(r, INK2) for r in vc.index[::-1]], height=.55)\n    for bb, v in zip(b, vc.values[::-1]):\n        ax[0].text(v + 1, bb.get_y() + .27, f\"{v} ({v/len(T):.0%})\", va=\"center\", fontsize=9, color=INK2)\n    ax[0].set_xlim(0, vc.max() * 1.3); ax[0].grid(axis=\"y\", visible=False); ax[0].set_title(\"Public tasks by repository\")\n    ps = T[\"problem_statement\"]\n    feat = pd.Series({\"PR template (<!--, checklists)\": ps.str.contains(r\"<!--|## (?:Checklist|Type of changes|Description)|- \\[[ x]\\]\").mean(),\n                      \"code block\": ps.str.contains(\"```\").mean(), \"a .py file path\": ps.str.contains(r\"[\\w/]+\\.py\\b\").mean(),\n                      \"a traceback\": ps.str.contains(\"Traceback\").mean()}).sort_values()\n    b = ax[1].barh(feat.index, feat.values * 100, color=BLUE, height=.55)\n    for bb, v in zip(b, feat.values):\n        ax[1].text(v * 100 + .6, bb.get_y() + .27, f\"{v:.0%}\", va=\"center\", fontsize=9, color=INK2)\n    ax[1].set_xlim(0, 40); ax[1].grid(axis=\"y\", visible=False); ax[1].set_xlabel(\"% of tasks\"); ax[1].set_title(\"What the issue text contains\")\n    plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()","metadata":{"_kg_hide-input":true,"jupyter":{"source_hidden":true},"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-25T19:25:29.463497Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-25T19:25:29.464063Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-25T19:25:29.659139Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-25T19:25:29.464041Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-25T19:25:29.65854Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**How to read the charts.** Left: how many public tasks come from each repository. Right: the share of task descriptions that contain a pull-request template (checklists and HTML comments left from GitHub’s PR form), a code block, a path to a `.py` file, or a Python traceback.\n\n**What they show.** FastAPI and Rich account for 89% of the public tasks. Descriptions rarely point to a file (10%) and almost never include a traceback (1%). One in five is a pull-request description rather than a bug report, with the template text still in it.\n\n**Why it matters for your agent.** The hidden tasks come from other repositories, so tuning for FastAPI internals will not transfer; the agent’s method will. The agent must be told explicitly to ignore template text, and to treat a task as “implement what this describes”, which often means a new feature rather than a bug fix. It cannot count on a file name or stack trace to tell it where to look.\n\nOther facts from the same data: the median description is 418 characters; by repository the median length is 83 words for FastAPI, 13 for Rich and 59 for Requests. Some tasks are one line long, for example “Notes: Add support for Exception.__notes__”.\n\n### How big a correct fix is","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"if not T.empty:\n    fig, ax = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(12, 3.6))\n    x = np.sort(T[\"lines\"].values); y = np.arange(1, len(x) + 1) / len(x) * 100\n    ax[0].step(x, y, where=\"post\", color=ORANGE, lw=2); ax[0].set_xscale(\"log\")\n    for k in (5, 12, 50):\n        p = (T[\"lines\"] <= k).mean() * 100\n        ax[0].plot([k], [p], \"o\", color=INK, ms=5); ax[0].text(k * 1.15, p - 6, f\"{p:.0f}% ≤ {k} lines\", fontsize=9)\n    ax[0].set_xlabel(\"changed lines in the reference fix (log scale)\"); ax[0].set_ylabel(\"% of fixes at or below\")\n    ax[0].set_title(\"How big a correct answer is\")\n    nf = T[\"nf\"].clip(upper=5).value_counts().sort_index()\n    b = ax[1].bar([str(i) if i < 5 else \"5+\" for i in nf.index], nf.values, color=ORANGE, width=.6)\n    for bb in b:\n        ax[1].text(bb.get_x() + .3, bb.get_height() + 1, int(bb.get_height()), ha=\"center\", fontsize=9, color=INK2)\n    ax[1].grid(axis=\"x\", visible=False); ax[1].set_xlabel(\"files in the fix\"); ax[1].set_title(f\"{(T['nf'] == 1).mean():.0%} of fixes touch one file\")\n    plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()","metadata":{"_kg_hide-input":true,"jupyter":{"source_hidden":true},"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-25T19:25:29.65993Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-25T19:25:29.660173Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-25T19:25:30.086382Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-25T19:25:29.660148Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-25T19:25:30.085663Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**How to read the charts.** Left: a cumulative curve. For any size on the horizontal axis (changed lines, log scale), the curve gives the share of reference fixes that are that size or smaller. The marked points read, for example, “51% of fixes change 12 lines or fewer”. Right: how many files each reference fix touches.\n\n**What they show.** Correct answers are small. Half change at most 12 lines, three quarters at most 50, and 71% touch a single file. A long tail of large changes exists (up to several thousand lines in one task), but it is rare.\n\n**Why it matters for your agent.** A patch that rewrites a lot of code is probably wrong, and it risks breaking PASS_TO_PASS tests. The system prompt should push for the smallest change at the root cause.\n\nThree more facts from the reference fixes:\n\n- **About 9% of fixes create a new file**, most of them FastAPI documentation examples under `docs_src/` that the new tests import. **12% edit `docs_src/`.** An agent told never to create files cannot solve these tasks.\n- **No reference fix edits a test file.** Tests arrive only at grading time, in `test_patch`.\n- **In 74% of tasks the test changes are larger than the fix.** Typically one or two new test functions are added next to existing tests of the same module. Reading those existing tests is the quickest way for the agent to learn what “correct” looks like.\n\n### How far the issue text is from the fix","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"GENERIC = {\"__init__\", \"main\", \"utils\", \"types\", \"base\", \"core\", \"models\", \"params\", \"_compat\", \"compat\"}\ndef gap(r):\n    t = r.problem_statement.lower(); src = [f for f in r.fix if f[\"path\"].endswith(\".py\")]\n    fn = {m.group(1) for f in src for h in f[\"h\"] for m in [re.search(r\"@@.*?@@\\s*(?:async\\s+)?(?:def|class)\\s+(\\w+)\", h)] if m} - GENERIC\n    st = {pathlib.Path(f[\"path\"]).stem for f in src} - GENERIC\n    if any(re.search(rf\"\\b{re.escape(a.lower())}\\b\", t) for a in fn): return \"names the function\"\n    if any(re.search(rf\"\\b{re.escape(a.lower())}\\b\", t) for a in st): return \"names only the module\"\n    return \"names neither\"\nif not T.empty:\n    T[\"gap\"] = T.apply(gap, axis=1); order = [\"names the function\", \"names only the module\", \"names neither\"]\n    tab = pd.crosstab(T[\"repo\"], T[\"gap\"], normalize=\"index\").reindex(index=[r for r in REPOS if r in set(T[\"repo\"])], columns=order).fillna(0) * 100\n    fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(12, 3.0)); left = np.zeros(len(tab))\n    for col, c in zip(order, [AQUA, YELLOW, MAG]):\n        b = ax.barh([r.split(\"/\")[1] for r in tab.index], tab[col], left=left, color=c, height=.55, label=col, edgecolor=\"white\", linewidth=2)\n        for bb, v, l in zip(b, tab[col], left):\n            if v >= 8: ax.text(l + v / 2, bb.get_y() + .27, f\"{v:.0f}%\", ha=\"center\", va=\"center\", fontsize=9.5, fontweight=\"bold\")\n        left += tab[col].values\n    ax.set_xlim(0, 100); ax.grid(axis=\"y\", visible=False); ax.set_xlabel(\"% of tasks\")\n    ax.legend(ncol=3, loc=\"upper center\", bbox_to_anchor=(.5, -.3)); ax.set_title(\"How close the issue text is to the fix site\")\n    plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()\n    print(\"all tasks:\", {k: f\"{v:.0%}\" for k, v in T[\"gap\"].value_counts(normalize=True).items()})","metadata":{"_kg_hide-input":true,"jupyter":{"source_hidden":true},"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-25T19:25:30.087239Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-25T19:25:30.087465Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-25T19:25:30.266464Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-25T19:25:30.087446Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-25T19:25:30.26578Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**How to read the chart.** The headers of a unified diff name the function that each changed block belongs to (`@@ … def some_function(`). For every task we check whether the issue text mentions that function by name (green), mentions only the name of the file’s module (yellow), or mentions neither (pink). Each bar is one repository.\n\n**What it shows.** Across all public tasks, 21% of issue texts name the function that needs the fix, 20% name only the module, and **59% name neither**.\n\n**Why it matters for your agent.** In one task out of five, a single `git grep` for a name from the issue leads straight to the fix. In the majority of tasks the agent has to find the place by behaviour, by error text or through the tests. Localisation is the main difficulty here, exactly as SWE-bench found (Chapter 12.1). This is where a good analyzer or a search skill earns its keep.\n\n### The code graph and embeddings: what they can and cannot see\n\nThe organisers supply a graph and embeddings for every task. A participant audited them against the repositories <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[<a href=\"https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/gemma-4-developer-agent/discussion/742911\">forum 742911</a>]</span>, and the harness code adds more detail:\n\n- **Half the files arrive empty.** Each task file is a hard link to a file named after its commit. Zip archives cannot store hard links, so 129 of 256 graph files and 129 of 256 embedding files are empty in the download. The harness always registers the three graph tools, but describes them in the task prompt only when both files are larger than 100 bytes.\n- **Async functions are missing.** Of 305 `async def` functions in the files the reference fixes touch, none appears in the graph, while 4,739 of 4,740 regular functions do. A large part of FastAPI’s request handling is asynchronous, so the graph cannot reach it.\n- **One edge type only.** All edges are `calls`. There are no import, inheritance or containment edges, and no module nodes.\n- **Embeddings barely separate code.** Two *random* nodes have a cosine similarity of about 0.84 on average (Chapter 14.4 explains why). A similarity of 0.99 between a query and a result therefore says little.\n\n**Why it matters for your agent.** Treat the graph tools as an occasional hint. Base the search on `git grep` and `read_file`, which see all code including async functions.\n\n# Part V. The maths\n\nEach topic below starts with the intuition, then gives the formula, then a worked example with this competition’s numbers.\n\n## 14. The mathematics of the competition\n\n### 14.1 How noisy the score is\n\n**Intuition.** Your agent does not have a fixed score. On any given set of tasks it gets lucky on some and unlucky on others. With only about 60 tasks on the public leaderboard, luck moves the score a lot.\n\n**Formula.** Let the agent solve each task with probability $p$, independently, and let there be $n$ tasks. The number solved follows a binomial distribution, and the score is its mean:\n\n$$X \\sim \\operatorname{Binomial}(n, p), \\qquad \\text{score} = \\frac{X}{n}, \\qquad \\operatorname{SE}(\\text{score}) = \\sqrt{\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}}$$\n\n**Worked example.** With $n = 60$ and $p = 0.12$: $\\operatorname{SE} = \\sqrt{0.12 \\cdot 0.88 / 60} \\approx 0.042$. The exact 95% range of the binomial distribution runs from 0.05 to 0.20. One task is worth $1/60 \\approx 0.017$.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"n = 60; ps_ = np.linspace(0.01, 0.35, 100); lo = binom.ppf(0.025, n, ps_) / n; hi = binom.ppf(0.975, n, ps_) / n\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(12, 3.8))\nax[0].fill_between(ps_, lo, hi, color=BLUE, alpha=.18, label=\"95% range of the observed score\")\nax[0].plot(ps_, ps_, color=BLUE, lw=2, label=\"true share of solvable tasks\")\nax[0].axvline(.12, color=INK2, ls=\"--\", lw=1); ax[0].text(.125, .31, \"true 0.12 → on the leaderboard\\nanything from 0.05 to 0.20\", fontsize=9)\nax[0].set_xlabel(\"true rate p\"); ax[0].set_ylabel(\"score on 60 tasks\"); ax[0].legend(loc=\"upper left\")\nax[0].set_title(\"Public leaderboard: ~60 tasks, one task = 0.017\")\nk = np.arange(0, 21)\nax[1].bar(k, binom.pmf(k, n, .12), color=BLUE, width=.7, label=\"agent A, p = 0.12\")\nax[1].bar(k + .35, binom.pmf(k, n, .15), color=ORANGE, width=.35, label=\"agent B, p = 0.15\")\nax[1].set_xlabel(\"tasks solved out of 60\"); ax[1].set_ylabel(\"probability\"); ax[1].legend()\np_win = sum(binom.pmf(a, n, .12) * binom.pmf(b, n, .15) for a in range(n + 1) for b in range(a + 1, n + 1))\nax[1].set_title(f\"B is stronger, yet beats A with probability {p_win:.2f} only\")\nplt.tight_layout(); plt.show()","metadata":{"_kg_hide-input":true,"jupyter":{"source_hidden":true},"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-25T19:25:30.26741Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-25T19:25:30.267613Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-25T19:25:31.030605Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-25T19:25:30.267597Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-25T19:25:31.029901Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**How to read the charts.** Left: for every true rate $p$ on the horizontal axis, the shaded band shows where the observed leaderboard score falls 95% of the time on 60 tasks. Right: the probability of solving exactly $k$ of 60 tasks for two agents, A with $p = 0.12$ and B with $p = 0.15$.\n\n**What they show.** An agent that truly solves 12% can appear anywhere from 0.05 to 0.20. The two distributions on the right overlap heavily: on a given set of 60 tasks, the better agent B finishes ahead of A only 64% of the time.\n\n**Why it matters for you.**\n\n- A difference of 0.02–0.03 between two of your submissions is noise. The standard error of the difference between two independent submissions is $\\sqrt{2 \\cdot 0.12 \\cdot 0.88 / 60} \\approx 0.059$.\n- The public leaderboard predicts the private one poorly: they are different halves of the hidden set, each with this much noise.\n- Measure locally when you can. To get the standard error down to 0.01 at $p = 0.12$ you would need $n = p(1-p)/0.01^2 \\approx 1{,}056$ tasks. On the 129 public tasks it is about 0.029, already better than a leaderboard submission.\n- With two final submissions allowed, one safe and one riskier is a sensible split.\n\n### 14.2 Where an agent loses its chance\n\n**Intuition.** Solving a task is a chain: find the place, make the right change, have it apply and break nothing, submit in time. A chain is as strong as its weakest link.\n\n**Formula.** With $L$ = found the right place, $E$ = made a correct edit, $V$ = the edit applies and passes all tests, $S$ = the patch was submitted within the limits:\n\n$$P(\\text{solved}) = P(L)\\cdot P(E\\mid L)\\cdot P(V\\mid L,E)\\cdot P(S\\mid L,E,V)$$\n\n**Worked example.** Suppose $P(L)=0.4$, $P(E\\mid L)=0.5$, $P(V\\mid L,E)=0.8$, $P(S\\mid\\ldots)=0.9$. Then $P(\\text{solved}) = 0.144$. Better search raising $P(L)$ to 0.6 gives 0.216, an extra 7 tasks per 100. Making the agent always submit ($P(S)=1$) gives 0.16. Because the terms multiply, improving the weakest one pays most, and localisation is usually the weakest.\n\n### 14.3 Finding the place: TF-IDF, BM25 and Hit@k\n\n**Intuition.** Before any language model is involved, you can rank the repository’s files by how many rare words they share with the issue. Words that appear in every file (like `self`) say little; a rare identifier that appears in the issue and in one file says a lot.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"levels = [(\"all tracked files in the repository\", 1.00), (\".py source files (tests excluded)\", .70), (\"files the agent reads (top ~5)\", .38),\n          (\"the function that needs the fix\", .20), (\"the 3–12 lines that change\", .10)]\nfig, ax = canvas(13, 3.8, (0, 13), (0, len(levels) + .4), \"Localisation narrows the search step by step\")\nfor i, (label, w) in enumerate(levels):\n    y = len(levels) - i - .5; width = 10 * w\n    ax.add_patch(FancyBboxPatch((6 - width / 2, y - .38), width, .76, boxstyle=\"round,pad=0.01,rounding_size=0.08\",\n                                fc=[GRID, \"#dfe9f7\", \"#c6daf3\", \"#9ec5f4\", BLUE][i], ec=\"white\"))\n    if width > 4:\n        ax.text(6, y, label, ha=\"center\", va=\"center\", fontsize=10, color=INK)\n    else:                                           # narrow levels: label to the right of the bar\n        ax.text(6 + width / 2 + .2, y, label, ha=\"left\", va=\"center\", fontsize=10, color=INK, fontweight=\"bold\" if i == 4 else \"normal\")\nplt.show()","metadata":{"_kg_hide-input":true,"jupyter":{"source_hidden":true},"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-25T19:25:31.0315Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-25T19:25:31.031715Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-25T19:25:31.112506Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-25T19:25:31.031688Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-25T19:25:31.111943Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**How to read the funnel.** Localisation narrows the search at each level: from all files, to Python source files, to the handful the agent will actually read, to the function, to the few lines that change. The widths are illustrative. Every level can lose the right answer, which is why the first levels matter most.\n\n**Formula.** TF-IDF gives a term $t$ in a document $d$ a weight that grows with how often the term appears in the document and falls with how many of the $N$ documents contain it:\n\n$$\\operatorname{tfidf}(t,d)=\\operatorname{tf}(t,d)\\cdot\\log\\frac{N}{\\operatorname{df}(t)}$$\n\nBM25, the ranking function used by the SWE-bench authors, adds two corrections: repeated occurrences count less and less (saturation, controlled by $k_1$), and long documents are penalised (controlled by $b$):\n\n$$\\operatorname{BM25}(q,d)=\\sum_{t\\in q}\\operatorname{IDF}(t)\\cdot\\frac{\\operatorname{tf}(t,d)\\,(k_1+1)}{\\operatorname{tf}(t,d)+k_1\\left(1-b+b\\,\\frac{|d|}{\\text{avgdl}}\\right)},\\qquad k_1\\approx1.2,\\ b\\approx0.75$$\n\nFor code, tokenisation matters: `get_request_handler` should also count as `get`, `request`, `handler`, and `CamelCase` as `camel`, `case`.\n\n**How search quality is measured.** *Hit@k* is the share of tasks where at least one file of the real fix is among the top $k$ results. If the agent reads the top $k$ files, $P(L) \\approx \\text{Hit@}k$ at the file level. This can be measured on the public tasks without running a model at all.\n\n### 14.4 Why embedding similarity is almost always high\n\n**Intuition.** If all vectors point in roughly the same direction, the angle between any two of them is small, whatever they represent.\n\n**Formula.** Cosine similarity measures the angle between two vectors:\n\n$$\\cos(u,v)=\\frac{u\\cdot v}{\\lVert u\\rVert\\,\\lVert v\\rVert}$$\n\nWrite every vector as a shared mean part plus its own part: $u = m + a$, $v = m + b$. When $\\lVert m\\rVert$ is much larger than $\\lVert a\\rVert$ and $\\lVert b\\rVert$, the cosine is close to 1 no matter what $a$ and $b$ are. This property is called *anisotropy*. Ethayarajh (2019) measured it in contextual word embeddings: randomly chosen words in GPT-2’s upper layers have an average cosine similarity close to 1.\n\n\n<p><img src=\"https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1909.00512/assets/mean_cosine_similarity_across_words.png\" width=\"80%\"></p>\n<p><i>From Ethayarajh (2019), “How Contextual are Contextualized Word Representations?”: average cosine similarity between randomly sampled words, per layer, for ELMo, BERT and GPT-2. In GPT-2 even random words are highly similar, and more so in upper layers.</i></p>\n\n**Worked example.** In this competition’s embeddings, two random nodes have a median cosine of about 0.84, so the scale of meaningful differences is squeezed into 0.84–1.0. The standard fix is to subtract the mean vector of all nodes and normalise again before comparing. The `search_similar_code` tool does not do this.\n\n### 14.5 The context budget\n\n**Intuition.** Everything the model is shown on a call competes for the same 32,768 tokens.\n\n**Formula.**\n\n$$T_{\\text{system}}+T_{\\text{tools}}+T_{\\text{task}}+\\sum T_{\\text{history}}+T_{\\text{output}}\\le 32\\,768$$\n\n**Worked example** for the coder of the best public solution, at about 3.6 characters per token: system prompt 700–1,200 tokens; seven tool descriptions about 900; task text with the repository tree 1,900–4,700; reserve for the reply (`max_output_tokens`) 8,192. That leaves about 18,000–21,000 tokens for the history. A single `read_file` can return 10,000 characters, about 2,800 tokens, so roughly seven full reads fill the history. Chapter 9 shows how compaction then shortens it.\n\n### 14.6 The time budget\n\n**Intuition.** The model writes text at a limited speed, and every task shares one 12-hour budget.\n\n**Formula.**\n\n$$\\text{tokens the model can write per task} \\approx \\text{speed} \\times (\\text{time per task} - \\text{setup} - \\text{command time})$$\n\n$$\\frac{12\\ \\text{h}}{120\\ \\text{tasks}} = 6\\ \\text{min};\\qquad 12\\text{–}31\\ \\tfrac{\\text{tokens}}{\\text{s}} \\times 360\\ \\text{s} \\approx 4{,}300\\text{–}11{,}200\\ \\text{tokens}$$\n\nThe speeds come from a participant’s measurements of vLLM 0.19.1 on 4× L4: a median of 12.2 tokens per second in a conservative serving mode and 31.4 with CUDA graph compilation. <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[notebook “Gemma and the Shape of Doubt”, versions 8 and 10]</span> The scoring server’s exact configuration is not published.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(12, 3.4)); mins = np.linspace(1, 10, 100)\nfor tps, c in ((12, ORANGE), (31, BLUE)):\n    ax.plot(mins, mins * 60 * tps / 1000, color=c, lw=2, label=f\"{tps} tok/s (participant’s measurement on 4×L4)\")\nax.axvline(6, color=INK2, ls=\"--\", lw=1); ax.text(6.1, 16, \"12 h / 120 tasks = 6 min\\nper task, setup included\", fontsize=9)\nax.set_xlabel(\"minutes per task\"); ax.set_ylabel(\"thousand generated tokens\"); ax.legend(loc=\"upper left\")\nax.set_title(\"How much text the model can write per task\"); plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()","metadata":{"_kg_hide-input":true,"jupyter":{"source_hidden":true},"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-25T19:25:31.113302Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-25T19:25:31.113524Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-25T19:25:31.258064Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-25T19:25:31.113506Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-25T19:25:31.257318Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**How to read the chart.** For a given number of minutes per task (horizontal axis), how many thousand tokens the model can generate at each measured speed. The dashed line is the average time available per task.\n\n**What it shows.** In six minutes the model writes between about 4,300 and 11,200 tokens in total, across all its turns, before counting setup and the time commands take to run.\n\n**Why it matters for your agent.** Long reasoning is expensive: one 4,000-token burst of thinking can consume anywhere from a third to all of a task’s average budget. Short turns that go straight to a tool call leave room for more attempts. Setting `max_time_minutes` in `eval_config.yaml` protects the whole submission from the 12-hour limit.\n\n### 14.7 Temperature, and why the best value depends on the number of attempts\n\n**Intuition.** Temperature decides how adventurous the model is when picking each token. Adventurous is good if you get many tries; careful is good if only one try counts.\n\n**Formula.** The model produces a score $z_w$ for every candidate token $w$; temperature $T$ rescales the scores before they are turned into probabilities:\n\n$$P(w)=\\frac{\\exp(z_w/T)}{\\sum_{v}\\exp(z_v/T)}$$\n\nAs $T \\to 0$ the most likely token is always chosen; at $T = 1$ the model samples from its natural distribution. `top_k` and `top_p` then cut off the unlikely tail.\n\n**The evidence.** The Codex paper measured how temperature affects the chance of solving programming problems when you may try $k$ times. <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.03374\">Chen et al., 2021</a>]</span>\n\n\n<p><img src=\"https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2107.03374/assets/figs/ft-pass-vs-temp.png\" width=\"38%\"></p>\n<p><i>From the Codex paper (Chen et al., 2021). Top: pass@k as a function of the number of samples k, one line per temperature. Bottom: the best temperature for each k. With a single sample the best temperature is 0.2; higher temperatures win only when many samples are allowed.</i></p>\n\n**What it means for you.** In this competition one patch per task is scored, which is the $k = 1$ case, where a low temperature such as 0.2 does best in the Codex measurements. Google recommends $T = 1.0$, top-p 0.95 and top-k 64 for Gemma 4 in general use <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[<a href=\"https://huggingface.co/google/gemma-4-31B-it\">model card</a>]</span>. An agent is not a single sample, though: it makes dozens of choices per task, and a low temperature can make it repeat the same failing command. Which setting wins in this harness has not been measured publicly, so change it only as a single-variable experiment.\n\n### 14.8 How big a LoRA adapter is\n\n**Formula.** For a weight matrix of size $d_{\\text{out}} \\times d_{\\text{in}}$, a rank-$r$ LoRA adds $r\\,(d_{\\text{in}} + d_{\\text{out}})$ trainable numbers.\n\n**Worked example.** The starter-kit adapters show that Gemma 4 31B’s query projection `q_proj` maps 5,376 inputs to 8,192 outputs:\n\n$$r=16:\\quad 16\\cdot(5376+8192)=217{,}088\\ \\text{parameters}\\approx 0.42\\ \\text{MB in bf16, per layer}$$\n\nThe README estimates a rank-16 adapter on all linear layers at 110–220 MB, so up to 8 adapters fit in 3 GiB; the server allows up to 8 adapters of rank up to 128. <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[HARNESS_README §3.4]</span> Size is not the constraint for LoRA here. Whether the scoring server loads adapters is (Chapter 16).\n\n### 14.9 pass@k and choosing the best of several attempts\n\n**Formula.** If one run solves a task with probability $p$, at least one of $k$ independent runs succeeds with probability $1-(1-p)^k$. From $n$ runs with $c$ successes, the unbiased estimate used in the Codex paper is:\n\n$$\\text{pass@}k = 1-\\frac{\\binom{n-c}{k}}{\\binom{n}{k}}$$\n\n**What it means for you.** Only one patch per task is scored, so several attempts help only if the agent can tell which attempt is right, for example by running its reproduction script and keeping the version that passes. SWE-Gym showed that a good verifier turns more attempts into steadily more solved tasks (Chapter 12.12). Here every extra attempt costs time from the same 6-minute average.\n\n# Part VI. Practice\n\n## 15. What is known about results so far\n\n| Submission | Public score | Design |\n|:--|:--|:--|\n| “GEMMA: EDA, Baseline for a start” (romanrozen) | **0.12** | coder + read-only analyzer (AgentTool), temperature 0.2, top-k 40, 8,192 output tokens, thinking off, no `eval_config.yaml`, no adapters |\n| “Black Cat SWE Agent” (lucifer19) | 0.06 | a single agent with all 9 tools, temperature 0.10–0.15, thinking off |\n| “Complete EDA & ADK Starter Kit” (nursrijan) | 0.05 | coder + analyzer <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[<a href=\"https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/gemma-4-developer-agent/discussion/743140\">forum 743140</a>]</span> |\n| Starter kit with three fixes, 5 minutes per task (Oleksii Zhukov) | 0.00 | no adapters, 5-minute and 50-call limits; the whole run took about 80 minutes |\n| Starter kit as shipped | error | “Your notebook hit an unhandled error while rerunning your code”; an organiser is preparing a fix <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[<a href=\"https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/gemma-4-developer-agent/discussion/743213\">forum 743213</a>]</span> |\n\n**Local runs on 4× L4.** The notebook “Gemma and the Shape of Doubt” served the model in a Kaggle notebook and ran the harness on public tasks:\n\n- *Version 7* (thinking on, 5 minutes per task): 0 of 4. All four runs hit the time limit, no library code was changed, and three fallback patches contained only reproduction scripts.\n- *Version 8* (thinking off, shorter answers): 2 of 4.\n- *Version 10* (temperature 1.0, compiled serving): 2 of 4, plus 3 of 12 on a wider sample.\n- *Failure patterns:* the same failed edit repeated until the patch was empty; one probe repeated 39 times; 41 of 45 tool calls spent searching for a symbol that does not exist; test files edited instead of code; context overflow; the installed copy of a package imported instead of the checkout.\n- *Local evaluation traps:* some FastAPI tests need `inline_snapshot`, which is not among the public wheels, and Requests tests hang on DNS lookups in the isolated network, so a local failure can be an environment problem.\n\nThese samples are 4–16 tasks, far too few for precise numbers (Chapter 14.1). Qualitatively they agree: agents lose tasks to process (time, repetition, empty patches) more often than to misunderstanding the code.\n\n## 16. Traps: why submissions fail or score zero\n\n| Trap | What happens | How to avoid it |\n|:--|:--|:--|\n| copying the starter kit’s `eval_config.yaml` | 1 minute and 10 tool calls per task: nothing gets fixed | write your own limits around a 6-minute average |\n| no per-task limit at all | long tasks eat the 12 hours; currently the whole submission then fails | set `max_time_minutes` |\n| the starter kit’s adapters | zero weights, and the model is addressed as `openai/main_lora`, which the server must know | leave adapters out until the organisers confirm they load |\n| `thinking_level` in the config | adds `reasoning_effort`, which LiteLLM may reject | switch thinking with `include_thoughts` only |\n| curly braces in a prompt | ADK treats `{word}` as a state variable and raises on unknown ones | no braces except `{problem_description}` |\n| a long reply carrying a big edit | the reply is cut at `max_output_tokens`, the tool call never closes, the turn is lost | small edits, short reasoning |\n| scratch files in `/workspace` | they become part of the patch | scratch files in `/tmp` via `run_command`; `git status --short` before submitting |\n| running the whole test suite | minutes per run, the 300-second limit, a wasted budget | one file or one test: `python -m pytest tests/test_x.py -x -q -k name` |\n| relying on graph tools | data can be missing, async code never appears | search with `git grep` and `read_file` |\n| editing after `submit_patch` | the edit is not scored | make `submit_patch` the last action |\n| extra inputs attached to the notebook | not seen in any scoring notebook | attach only the competition data |\n\n**On “Kaggle Error”.** On day three several submissions, including the official starter kit, failed with generic errors. The organisers acknowledged the starter-kit failure (caused by its adapters) and are preparing a fix. Other failures show no detailed message on the participant side. What reliably works so far is the packaging of the scoring notebooks described in Chapter 11.\n\n## 17. Strategies, from cheap and safe to expensive and risky\n\nEach lever names the factor from Chapter 14.2 it improves.\n\n### Level 1. The protocol in the system prompt (improves $P(S)$ and $P(V)$)\n\nThese rules cost nothing and address the failure patterns in Chapter 15:\n\n- Require a tool call in every reply until the patch is submitted; three replies without one end the task.\n- Create reproduction scripts in `/tmp` with a heredoc in `run_command`.\n- Run `python -m py_compile` after every edit. After two failed edits, print the exact lines with `sed -n` and retry with a 2–3 line fragment.\n- Never repeat a command unchanged. If a search finds nothing, change the term: a shorter name, the class instead of the method, the error text.\n- If the behaviour contradicts the code you read, check which copy is imported: `python -c \"import pkg; print(pkg.__file__)\"` must point inside `/workspace`.\n- Ignore pull-request template text; allow new files when the task needs them.\n- Before submitting, run `git status --short` and `git diff` and remove junk.\n- Run tests the way the grader does: `PYTHONSAFEPATH=1 python3 -m pytest <file> -p no:anyio -q`.\n\n### Level 2. Agent architecture (improves $P(L)$ and protects the context)\n\n- **Coder + analyzer** (Example B in Chapter 6): the design of the best public solution, supported by “Lost in the Middle” (Chapter 12.9).\n- **Give the analyzer the full task** through `{problem_description}` in its prompt, so nothing is lost when the coder paraphrases.\n- **Top-down localisation** in the analyzer, as in Agentless (Chapter 12.6): files, then functions, then lines.\n- **A reviewer** before submission (Example C), because a partial fix scores zero.\n\n### Level 3. Your own tools through skills (improves $P(L)$)\n\nA skill (Example D) is the only way to add code of your own. Strong candidates: a BM25 locator over the repository that also indexes async functions; a finder for the existing tests of a module; a safe-edit script that checks uniqueness and compiles the file. Scripts share the task’s time, so they must finish in seconds. No public submission with a score has used a skill yet.\n\n### Level 4. Sampling, thinking and budgets (improves $P(S)$ and $P(E)$)\n\n- Set `max_time_minutes` so that about 120 tasks fit in 12 hours.\n- Use thinking only where reasoning pays for its time, for example in the analyzer, whose thoughts never reach the coder’s context.\n- Compare temperatures one submission at a time (Chapter 14.7).\n\n### Level 5. Post-training (improves every factor at once)\n\nThis is what the organisers most want to see, and the most demanding route.\n\n1. **Tasks.** Build them from repositories other than the four public ones, the way SWE-smith does (Chapter 12.13), because the hidden tasks come from private repositories.\n2. **Trajectories.** Run an agent in an exact copy of the harness, keep only successful runs, and fine-tune on them (SWE-Gym, Chapter 12.12). Whether trajectories from closed models may be used is an open question on the forum <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[<a href=\"https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/gemma-4-developer-agent/discussion/742807\">forum 742807</a>]</span>.\n3. **Reinforcement learning.** A patch-similarity reward or a test-execution reward with GRPO (Chapter 12.14).\n4. **Packaging.** A LoRA adapter under `adapters/`, rank up to 128, within 3 GiB.\n\nA forum post reported that the pinned vLLM 0.19.1 refuses to start with LoRA enabled for this model. Until the organisers confirm that adapters load during scoring, this route may not be usable. <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[<a href=\"https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/gemma-4-developer-agent/discussion/743213\">forum 743213</a>]</span>\n\n### Experiment discipline\n\n- **One change per submission.** With about ±0.04 of noise, two changes at once make it impossible to tell which one worked.\n- **Measure locally.** Kaggle notebooks offer a 4× L4 accelerator. Serving the model there and running `swegemma eval` on 20–129 public tasks is several times more precise than one leaderboard submission.\n- **Classify failures:** wrong fix, patch did not apply, out of time, broken environment. Only the first needs a better model or better search; the others are fixed by the protocol.\n\n## 18. The Paper Track\n\nA separate paper track with 35,000 USD in prizes runs alongside. Papers are due November 12, 2026; the best will be presented at a Google-hosted event such as a NeurIPS expo workshop. <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[<a href=\"https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/gemma-4-developer-agent/overview/prizes\">Prizes</a>, <a href=\"https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/gemma-4-developer-agent-paper\">paper track</a>]</span> Topics:\n\n- **Tuning & Optimization:** parameter-efficient fine-tuning and reinforcement learning for SWE agents.\n- **Code Comprehension:** building, parsing and embedding code graphs.\n- **Tasks & Benchmarks:** new tasks, datasets and resources about code structure.\n- **Graph Reasoning:** LLM reasoning over large graphs.\n\nUsing the released graphs and embeddings is encouraged. Their gaps (Chapter 13) are a ready research question; one participant has already published groundwork for a graph that covers async functions, decorators and FastAPI dependency injection.\n\n## 19. Rules that are easy to break by accident\n\n- **One Kaggle account per person**, and no submissions from several accounts. <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[Rules, preamble]</span>\n- **No private sharing of code outside a team.** Share publicly on Kaggle, or form a team of up to 5; teams can merge until November 25. <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[Rules 2.1]</span>\n- **External data and models** are allowed if they are publicly available and reasonably cheap for all participants. <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[Rules 2.6]</span>\n- **Competition data** may not be shared with people who have not accepted the rules. <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[Rules 2.4]</span>\n- **Winners** hand over training and inference code with a description of the environment, and release the solution under an open licence. <span style=\"font-size:12px\">[Rules 2.5, 2.8]</span>\n\n# Part VII. For advanced readers\n\n## 20. Seventeen details you only see in the source code\n\nEach item gives the context first, then the detail, then what to do about it. Chapter numbers point to the full explanation.\n\n**Model and settings**\n\n1. *Context:* the YAML lets you set a thinking budget. *Detail:* `thinking_budget` is validated but never sent to the model; `include_thoughts: false` means no reasoning at all, and `include_thoughts: true` means reasoning capped only by `max_output_tokens`. *Action:* decide thinking with `include_thoughts` alone and size `max_output_tokens` accordingly. (Chapter 7)\n2. *Context:* seeds usually make runs reproducible. *Detail:* `seed` is dropped before the request reaches vLLM. *Action:* expect two identical submissions to score differently. (Chapter 7)\n3. *Context:* adapters are attached per agent. *Detail:* an agent with `adapter: name` sends its requests to the model id `openai/name`, which the server must have registered; the starter adapters are all-zero anyway. *Action:* leave adapters out until the organisers confirm support. (Chapters 7, 12.11)\n4. *Context:* sub-agents start with a fresh context. *Detail:* `AgentTool` copies the parent’s session state, so `{problem_description}` works in a sub-agent’s prompt. *Action:* give the analyzer and reviewer the original issue directly. (Chapter 7)\n\n**The agent loop**\n\n5. *Context:* the harness nudges a model that stops calling tools. *Detail:* three turns in a row without a tool call end the task. *Action:* require a tool call in every reply. (Chapter 9)\n6. *Context:* `submit_patch` looks like a checkpoint. *Detail:* the diff is frozen at the call and the loop ends after that turn; without the call the working tree is taken anyway. *Action:* verify first, submit last, and keep `/workspace` clean. (Chapter 9)\n7. *Context:* the README says leading slashes are stripped. *Detail:* the file tools reject `/tmp/...` paths. *Action:* create scratch files with `run_command`. (Chapter 10)\n8. *Context:* agents often start with `ls`. *Detail:* the task message already contains the first 150 lines of `find . -maxdepth 3`. *Action:* skip the initial listing. (Chapter 9)\n9. *Context:* long histories are compacted. *Detail:* every 15 events the older history becomes a summary; exact text is lost. *Action:* re-read the lines before calling `edit_file`. (Chapter 9)\n10. *Context:* `edit_file` tries three matching strategies. *Detail:* the first is a plain substring match, so a fragment without its indentation still matches and the new code is inserted unindented. *Action:* compile after every edit. (Chapter 10)\n\n**Verification**\n\n11. *Context:* agents usually run `pytest` to check their work. *Detail:* the grader runs `PYTHONSAFEPATH=1 python3 -m pytest … -p no:anyio`, which keeps the current directory off `sys.path`. *Action:* test with the same command. (Chapter 8)\n12. *Context:* 12 hours sounds like plenty. *Detail:* tasks run one at a time with no default per-task limit, and passing 12 hours currently fails the whole submission. *Action:* set `max_time_minutes`. (Chapters 6, 14.6)\n13. *Context:* your notebook is re-run for scoring. *Detail:* the hidden `tasks.jsonl` has no `patch` or `test_patch`. *Action:* guard any code that reads them. (Chapter 11)\n\n**Data**\n\n14. *Context:* local results on public tasks are tempting to trust. *Detail:* hidden tasks come from private repositories and were filtered for solvability by a strong model; public FastAPI and Rich code may be familiar to the model from training. *Action:* read local numbers as optimistic. (Chapter 13)\n15. *Context:* agents often grep for names from the issue. *Detail:* 59% of issues name neither the function nor the module that changes. *Action:* invest in localisation. (Chapter 13)\n16. *Context:* the graph tools promise structural navigation. *Detail:* no async functions, one edge type, anisotropic embeddings. *Action:* use them as hints only. (Chapters 13, 14.4)\n\n**The leaderboard**\n\n17. *Context:* a higher leaderboard score looks like a better agent. *Detail:* at 12% vs 15% on 60 tasks, the better agent leads only 64% of the time. *Action:* change one thing per submission and measure locally. (Chapter 14.1)\n\n## 21. Sources\n\n**Competition.** Pages: [Overview](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/gemma-4-developer-agent/overview), [Evaluation](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/gemma-4-developer-agent/overview/evaluation), [Timeline](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/gemma-4-developer-agent/overview/timeline), [Prizes](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/gemma-4-developer-agent/overview/prizes), [Model Selection, Budget, and Harness Rules](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/gemma-4-developer-agent/overview/model-selection-budget-and-harness-rules), [Rules](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/gemma-4-developer-agent/rules), [Data](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/gemma-4-developer-agent/data). `HARNESS_README.md` on the Data tab. Harness source: [metric/gemma-4-developer-agent-wheelhouse](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/metric/gemma-4-developer-agent-wheelhouse) (swegemma 0.2.7, adk-submission 0.2.11, adk-eval-core 0.1.0, google-adk 1.36.1). Forum threads [743007](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/gemma-4-developer-agent/discussion/743007), [743063](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/gemma-4-developer-agent/discussion/743063), [742911](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/gemma-4-developer-agent/discussion/742911), [743213](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/gemma-4-developer-agent/discussion/743213), [742882](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/gemma-4-developer-agent/discussion/742882), [742807](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/gemma-4-developer-agent/discussion/742807), [743140](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/gemma-4-developer-agent/discussion/743140). Public notebooks: “GEMMA: EDA, Baseline for a start” (romanrozen), “Gemma 4 harness guide + first submission” (Oleksii Zhukov), “Gemma and the Shape of Doubt” (prvsiyan), “Black Cat SWE Agent” (lucifer19), “Complete EDA & ADK Starter Kit” (nursrijan), the Paper Track companion notebook on AsyncPolyGraph.\n\n**Research.** Jimenez et al., SWE-bench, ICLR 2024 ([arXiv:2310.06770](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.06770)). OpenAI, [Introducing SWE-bench Verified](https://openai.com/index/introducing-swe-bench-verified/), 2024. Yao et al., ReAct, ICLR 2023 ([arXiv:2210.03629](https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.03629)). Yang et al., SWE-agent, NeurIPS 2024 ([arXiv:2405.15793](https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.15793)). Wang et al., OpenHands, ICLR 2025 ([arXiv:2407.16741](https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.16741)). Xia et al., Agentless, 2024 ([arXiv:2407.01489](https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.01489)). Zhang et al., AutoCodeRover, ISSTA 2024 ([arXiv:2404.05427](https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.05427)). Ouyang et al., RepoGraph, ICLR 2025 ([arXiv:2410.14684](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.14684)). Liu et al., Lost in the Middle, TACL 2024 ([arXiv:2307.03172](https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03172)). Kwon et al., PagedAttention / vLLM, SOSP 2023 ([arXiv:2309.06180](https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.06180)). Hu et al., LoRA, 2021 ([arXiv:2106.09685](https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.09685)). Pan et al., SWE-Gym, ICML 2025 ([arXiv:2412.21139](https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.21139)). Yang et al., SWE-smith, 2025 ([arXiv:2504.21798](https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.21798)). Wei et al., SWE-RL, 2025 ([arXiv:2502.18449](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.18449)). Shao et al., DeepSeekMath (GRPO), 2024 ([arXiv:2402.03300](https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.03300)). Chen et al., Codex / pass@k, 2021 ([arXiv:2107.03374](https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.03374)). Ethayarajh, How Contextual are Contextualized Word Representations?, EMNLP 2019 ([arXiv:1909.00512](https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.00512)). Gemma Team, Gemma 4 Technical Report, 2026 ([arXiv:2607.02770](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.02770)); model card [google/gemma-4-31B-it](https://huggingface.co/google/gemma-4-31B-it).\n\n**Industry.** GitHub, [GitHub Copilot: Meet the new coding agent](https://github.blog/news-insights/product-news/github-copilot-meet-the-new-coding-agent/). Google, [Jules is out of public beta](https://blog.google/technology/google-labs/jules-now-available/). Cognition, [SWE-bench technical report](https://cognition.com/blog/swe-bench-technical-report).\n\n*Figures from research papers load from arXiv and ar5iv and are credited in their captions. The organisers have promised scoring fixes and answers to open questions (LoRA, distillation, the 12-hour limit), so parts of this guide may go out of date.*","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Glossary: the words you need before starting\n\n| Term | Plain meaning | In this competition |\n|:--|:--|:--|\n| **LLM** (large language model) | a neural network that continues text: given everything written so far, it predicts the next piece | Gemma 4 31B, the only model allowed |\n| **Token** | the unit of text the model reads and writes, roughly ¾ of an English word or 3–4 characters of code | all limits are counted in tokens |\n| **Context window** | the maximum amount of text (in tokens) the model can take into account at once: instructions, history and its own answer together | 32,768 tokens here |\n| **Prompt / system prompt** | the text given to the model. The system prompt is the permanent instruction that sets its role and rules | you write it in `prompts/system.md` |\n| **Agent** | an LLM running in a loop: it decides on an action, a program executes it, the result is shown to the model, and it decides again | what you submit is the configuration of such an agent |\n| **Tool / tool call** | a function the model may ask to run, for example “read this file”. The model writes a structured request, the harness executes it and returns the result | 9 tools are provided |\n| **Harness** | the organisers’ program that runs your agent, gives it tools, enforces budgets and scores the result | libraries `swegemma`, `adk-submission`, `adk-eval-core` |\n| **Sandbox / container** | an isolated copy of a computer (a Docker container) where the agent works, so it cannot damage anything or reach the internet | one fresh container per task, no network |\n| **Repository, commit** | a project’s code under version control (git); a commit is one saved state of it | each task gives a repository at a fixed commit |\n| **Issue** | a bug report or feature request written by a person on GitHub | the task text the agent receives |\n| **Patch / diff** | a description of changes to files, line by line: which lines are removed and which are added | the agent’s answer is a diff |\n| **Unit test, pytest** | a small program that checks one behaviour of the code; pytest runs them and exits with code 0 if all pass | hidden tests decide pass or fail |\n| **SWE-bench** | the standard research benchmark of real GitHub issues, checked by tests | this competition follows its design |\n| **Quantization** | storing model weights with fewer bits to save memory | the model uses 4-bit weights |\n| **LoRA** | a cheap way to fine-tune a big model by training a small add-on | optional adapters in the submission |\n| **ADK** | Google’s Agent Development Kit, a library for building LLM agents | your YAML is compiled into ADK agents |\n| **vLLM** | a fast server that runs the model and answers requests | serves Gemma on 4 GPUs |\n| **YAML** | a simple text format for configuration files | `agent.yaml` and friends |","metadata":{}}]}
--
/workspace/gemma4/refs/notebooks/gemma-4-starter-inside-the-harness-eda/gemma-4-starter-inside-the-harness-eda.ipynb:1:{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"display_name":"Python 3","language":"python","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.12"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"none","dataSources":[{"sourceId":149921,"sourceType":"competition"}],"isGpuEnabled":false,"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook"}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(120deg,#1A73E8 0%,#6A3DE8 50%,#D93025 100%);padding:30px 32px;border-radius:16px;color:#fff\">\n  <div style=\"font-size:13px;letter-spacing:2px;opacity:.85;color:#fff\">GOOGLE · THE GEMMA 4 DEVELOPER AGENT COMPETITION</div>\n  <h1 style=\"color:#fff;margin:6px 0 4px 0;font-size:34px\">🤖 Universal Starter: EDA → Localization → ADK Agent → <code style=\"background:rgba(255,255,255,.18);color:#fff;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:6px\">submission.zip</code></h1>\n  <p style=\"font-size:16px;margin:10px 0 0 0;color:#fff;opacity:.95\">One config cell · Run-All safe · every harness fact verified against the competition files at runtime</p>\n</div>\n\n<br>\n\n**The task in one sentence:** build a software-engineering agent on **Gemma 4** that reads a real GitHub issue, edits a Python repository inside a sandbox, and produces a patch that makes hidden tests pass. Submissions are **declarative agent bundles** (YAML + prompts, optionally adapters) rather than arbitrary code.\n\n### ✨ What makes this starter different\n\n| | Principle | Why it matters |\n|:-:|:--|:--|\n| 🔎 | **Zero hard-coded harness facts** | Tool names, model alias, context limit and bundle schema are *detected* from `HARNESS_README.md` and `sample_submission`, with the evidence shown. If the organizers change something, the notebook adapts. |\n| 🛡️ | **Run-All safe** | Every section degrades gracefully. Unknown data formats produce a warning, not a crash. |\n| 📐 | **Correct EDA** | A real unified-diff parser (hunk-count aware, works with or without `diff --git` headers). All takeaways are *computed*, not guessed. |\n| 🎯 | **Localization baseline** | A no-LLM TF-IDF retriever measures how hard it is to find the file to fix. That is the #1 failure mode of SWE agents. |\n| 🧰 | **Reusable tooling** | `edit_file` simulator with syntax checks, patch-sanity checker, tag-tolerant YAML validator, deterministic zip packaging. |\n| 🚫 | **No leakage** | Reference patches are only used for analysis and are never written to any output. |","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### 🗺️ Contents\n1. [⚙️ Config](#config) : the only cell you need to edit\n2. [📦 Data discovery](#discovery) : locate the data, inventory it, read the harness README\n3. [🧰 Tool verification](#tools) : which agent tools really exist\n4. [📊 Task EDA](#eda) : repositories, patch anatomy, difficulty, timeline\n5. [🧭 Localization signals](#signals) : what the issue text tells us about *where* to edit\n6. [🕸️ Code intelligence](#graphs) : call graphs & embeddings explorer\n7. [🎯 Localization baseline](#baseline) : lexical retrieval Hit@k\n8. [✂️ Edit mechanics & patch safety](#edit) : avoid the classic `edit_file` pitfalls\n9. [🧠 Prompts & context budget](#prompts)\n10. [🏗️ Agent bundle builder](#bundle) : single agent or analyzer + coder\n11. [🛡️ Validator & packaging](#validate) : `submission.zip`\n12. [🚀 Playbook](#playbook) : what to try next, ranked\n\n> **Quick start:** attach the competition data → (optionally) edit `CFG` → **Run All** → download `submission.zip` from the output panel and submit it following the competition's submission instructions.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"<a id=\"config\"></a>\n# ⚙️ 1. Config\nEverything tunable lives here. `None` means *auto-detect from the competition files*.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"CFG = {\n    # ── agent ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    \"architecture\": \"analyzer+coder\",   # \"single\" | \"analyzer+coder\"\n    \"bundle_base\": \"starter\",           # \"starter\" = build from this notebook's templates\n                                        # \"sample\"  = copy sample_submission and only swap in our prompt\n    \"model_alias\": None,                # None → detected from sample_submission\n    \"fallback_model_alias\": \"gemma-4-31b-it-qat-w4a16-ct\",\n    \"temperature\": 0.2,\n    \"top_p\": 0.95,\n    \"top_k\": 40,\n    \"max_output_tokens\": 8192,\n    \"thinking_budget\": 4096,\n    # ── environment ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    \"context_limit\": None,              # None → detected from HARNESS_README.md\n    \"fallback_context_limit\": 32768,\n    # ── analysis ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    \"localization_max_tasks\": 200,\n    \"graph_scan_max_tasks\": 60,\n    \"time_budget_s\": 240,               # soft cap per heavy analysis loop\n    \"seed\": 42,\n    # ── output ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    \"bundle_dir\": \"submission_bundle\",\n    \"zip_name\": \"submission.zip\",\n}","metadata":{},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"<a id=\"discovery\"></a>\n# 📦 2. Setup & data discovery","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import os, re, sys, json, gzip, time, html, pickle, shutil, tarfile, zipfile, hashlib, textwrap, warnings\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom collections import Counter, defaultdict\nfrom contextlib import contextmanager\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport seaborn as sns\nimport networkx as nx\nimport yaml\nfrom IPython.display import display, HTML, Markdown\n\nfrom pandas.io.formats.style import Styler\nif not hasattr(Styler, \"map\"):          # pandas < 2.1 compatibility\n    Styler.map = Styler.applymap\n\nwarnings.filterwarnings(\"ignore\")\nnp.random.seed(CFG[\"seed\"])\npd.set_option(\"display.max_colwidth\", 90)\n\n# ── visual identity ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nPAL = [\"#4285F4\", \"#EA4335\", \"#FBBC04\", \"#34A853\", \"#A142F4\", \"#24C1E0\", \"#F538A0\", \"#5F6368\"]\nsns.set_theme(style=\"whitegrid\", palette=PAL)\nplt.rcParams.update({\n    \"figure.dpi\": 110, \"axes.titleweight\": \"bold\", \"axes.titlesize\": 12, \"axes.labelsize\": 10,\n    \"axes.spines.top\": False, \"axes.spines.right\": False, \"font.family\": \"DejaVu Sans\",\n    \"figure.titleweight\": \"bold\", \"figure.titlesize\": 15,\n})\n\ndef esc(s):\n    return html.escape(str(s))\n\ndef callout(msg, kind=\"info\"):\n    bg, bd, icon = {\"info\": (\"#E8F0FE\", \"#4285F4\", \"💡\"), \"ok\": (\"#E6F4EA\", \"#34A853\", \"✅\"),\n                    \"warn\": (\"#FEF7E0\", \"#F9AB00\", \"⚠️\"), \"err\": (\"#FCE8E6\", \"#EA4335\", \"⛔\")}[kind]\n    display(HTML(f'<div style=\"background:{bg};border-left:5px solid {bd};padding:10px 14px;'\n                 f'border-radius:6px;margin:6px 0;color:#202124\">{icon} {msg}</div>'))\n\ndef kpis(items):\n    cards = \"\".join(\n        f'<div style=\"flex:1;min-width:130px;background:#fff;border:1px solid #DADCE0;border-radius:12px;'\n        f'padding:10px 14px;margin:4px;box-shadow:0 1px 2px rgba(60,64,67,.15)\">'\n        f'<div style=\"font-size:12px;color:#5F6368\">{esc(label)}</div>'\n        f'<div style=\"font-size:22px;font-weight:700;color:#1A73E8\">{esc(value)}</div></div>'\n        for label, value in items)\n    display(HTML(f'<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;margin:4px 0 10px 0\">{cards}</div>'))\n\ndef details(summary, body_html):\n    display(HTML(f'<details style=\"margin:4px 0\"><summary style=\"cursor:pointer;font-weight:600\">{esc(summary)}</summary>{body_html}</details>'))\n\ndef pre(text, max_lines=80):\n    lines = str(text).splitlines()\n    more = f\"\\n… ({len(lines) - max_lines} more lines)\" if len(lines) > max_lines else \"\"\n    return ('<pre style=\"font-size:12px;background:#F8F9FA;color:#202124;padding:10px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto\">'\n            + esc(\"\\n\".join(lines[:max_lines]) + more) + \"</pre>\")\n\ndef human_bytes(n):\n    for unit in [\"B\", \"KB\", \"MB\", \"GB\", \"TB\"]:\n        if n < 1024:\n            return f\"{n:,.1f} {unit}\" if unit != \"B\" else f\"{n} B\"\n        n /= 1024\n    return f\"{n:.1f} PB\"\n\n@contextmanager\ndef safe(name):\n    \"\"\"Run a section; on failure show a warning instead of crashing Run All.\"\"\"\n    try:\n        yield\n    except Exception as e:\n        callout(f\"<b>{esc(name)}</b> skipped: <code>{esc(type(e).__name__)}: {esc(e)}</code>\", \"warn\")\n\nWORK = Path(\"/kaggle/working\") if Path(\"/kaggle/working\").exists() else Path(\".\").resolve()\nprint(f\"Python {sys.version.split()[0]} · pandas {pd.__version__} · networkx {nx.__version__} · working dir: {WORK}\")","metadata":{},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### 🔎 Locating the competition data\nNo hard-coded personal paths: we check the standard Kaggle mount points, then search `/kaggle/input` for `tasks.jsonl`. You can also set `GEMMA_AGENT_DATA=/path/to/data` to run this notebook locally.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def limited_walk(root, max_depth):\n    root = Path(root)\n    if not root.exists():\n        return\n    stack = [(root, 0)]\n    while stack:\n        d, depth = stack.pop()\n        try:\n            entries = sorted(os.scandir(d), key=lambda e: e.name)\n        except (PermissionError, FileNotFoundError, NotADirectoryError):\n            continue\n        for e in entries:\n            yield Path(e.path)\n            if e.is_dir(follow_symlinks=False) and depth < max_depth:\n                stack.append((Path(e.path), depth + 1))\n\ndef find_data_dir():\n    candidates = [Path(os.environ[\"GEMMA_AGENT_DATA\"])] if os.environ.get(\"GEMMA_AGENT_DATA\") else []\n    candidates += [Path(\"/kaggle/input/competitions/gemma-4-developer-agent\"),\n                   Path(\"/kaggle/input/gemma-4-developer-agent\"),\n                   Path(\"competition_data\"), Path(\"../competition_data\")]\n    for c in candidates:\n        if (c / \"tasks.jsonl\").exists():\n            return c\n    for root in [Path(\"/kaggle/input\"), Path(\".\")]:\n        for p in limited_walk(root, 3):\n            if p.name == \"tasks.jsonl\":\n                return p.parent\n    return None\n\nDATA_DIR = find_data_dir()\nDEMO_MODE = DATA_DIR is None\nif DEMO_MODE:\n    callout(\"Competition data not found → running in <b>demo mode</b> on a tiny synthetic sample. \"\n            \"Attach the competition dataset (<i>Add Input → Competitions</i>) for real results.\", \"warn\")\nelse:\n    callout(f\"Data directory: <code>{esc(DATA_DIR)}</code>\", \"ok\")","metadata":{},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def dir_stats(path, max_files=200_000):\n    n, size, exts, truncated = 0, 0, Counter(), False\n    for dp, _, fns in os.walk(path):\n        for f in fns:\n            n += 1\n            try:\n                size += os.path.getsize(os.path.join(dp, f))\n            except OSError:\n                pass\n            name = f.lower()\n            ext = \".tar.gz\" if name.endswith(\".tar.gz\") else (Path(f).suffix.lower() or \"(none)\")\n            exts[ext] += 1\n            if n >= max_files:\n                truncated = True\n                break\n        if truncated:\n            break\n    return n, size, exts, truncated\n\nINVENTORY = pd.DataFrame()\nif not DEMO_MODE:\n    rows = []\n    for e in sorted(DATA_DIR.iterdir()):\n        if e.is_dir():\n            n, s, exts, tr = dir_stats(e)\n            kind = \"📁\"\n        else:\n            n, s, exts, tr = 1, e.stat().st_size, Counter({e.suffix or \"(none)\": 1}), False\n            kind = \"📄\"\n        rows.append({\"\": kind, \"entry\": e.name, \"files\": f\"{n:,}{'+' if tr else ''}\", \"size\": human_bytes(s),\n                     \"top extensions\": \", \".join(f\"{k} ×{v}\" for k, v in exts.most_common(4))})\n    INVENTORY = pd.DataFrame(rows)\n    display(INVENTORY.style.hide(axis=\"index\").set_properties(**{\"text-align\": \"left\"}))","metadata":{},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### 📜 The harness README is the source of truth\nWe extract key facts **with the line they came from**, so you can check every claim at a glance. The full README is rendered below (collapsed).","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"README_PATH = None\nif not DEMO_MODE:\n    README_PATH = next((p for p in [DATA_DIR / \"HARNESS_README.md\", *sorted(DATA_DIR.glob(\"*README*\"))] if p.exists()), None)\nREADME = README_PATH.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"replace\") if README_PATH else \"\"\n\ndef readme_lines(pattern, max_hits=3):\n    hits = []\n    for line in README.splitlines():\n        if re.search(pattern, line, flags=re.I) and line.strip():\n            hits.append(textwrap.shorten(line.strip(), 170))\n            if len(hits) >= max_hits:\n                break\n    return hits\n\ndef detect_context_limit(text):\n    \"\"\"Look for max_model_len / context-window numbers; return (value, evidence line).\"\"\"\n    pats = [r\"max[_ -]?model[_ -]?len\\D{0,20}?(\\d{1,3}(?:[,_]\\d{3})+|\\d{4,7})\",\n            r\"context(?:[ -]window|[ -]length|[ -]limit|[ -]ceiling)?\\D{0,40}?(\\d{1,3}(?:[,_]\\d{3})+|\\d{4,7})\\s*(?:tokens)?\"]\n    for pat in pats:\n        for line in text.splitlines():\n            m = re.search(pat, line, flags=re.I)\n            if m:\n                v = int(re.sub(r\"[,_]\", \"\", m.group(1)))\n                if 2048 <= v <= 2_000_000:\n                    return v, line.strip()\n    return None, None\n\nctx_value, ctx_evidence = detect_context_limit(README)\nCONTEXT_LIMIT = CFG[\"context_limit\"] or ctx_value or CFG[\"fallback_context_limit\"]\nctx_source = \"CFG override\" if CFG[\"context_limit\"] else (\"HARNESS_README.md\" if ctx_value else \"fallback (not found in README)\")\n\nfacts = []\nfor topic, pat in [(\"Context / tokens\", r\"max_model_len|context|token\"), (\"Turn / call budget\", r\"turn|budget|max[_ ]?calls?\"),\n                   (\"Time limits\", r\"timeout|wall|second|minute\"), (\"Hardware\", r\"\\bGPU|L4|A100|H100|TPU|vLLM\"),\n                   (\"Submission rules\", r\"submission|archive|\\.zip|extension|adapter|LoRA\"),\n                   (\"Scoring\", r\"score|metric|pass|resolved|pytest\")]:\n    for line in readme_lines(pat):\n        facts.append({\"topic\": topic, \"evidence (README line)\": line})\n\nif README:\n    callout(f\"Context limit used by this notebook: <b>{CONTEXT_LIMIT:,} tokens</b> (source: {esc(ctx_source)})\"\n            + (f\"<br><small>evidence: <code>{esc(ctx_evidence)}</code></small>\" if ctx_evidence else \"\"), \"info\")\n    if facts:\n        display(pd.DataFrame(facts).style.hide(axis=\"index\").set_properties(**{\"text-align\": \"left\"}))\n    details(f\"📖 Show full {README_PATH.name} ({len(README.splitlines())} lines)\", pre(README, max_lines=400))\nelse:\n    callout(f\"No harness README found. Using fallback context limit {CONTEXT_LIMIT:,}.\", \"warn\")","metadata":{},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"<a id=\"tools\"></a>\n# 🧰 3. Tool verification\nCommunity notebooks list up to nine sandbox tools. Rather than trusting any list (including this one), we **grep the harness files** for each tool name. The agent bundle built later only uses tools that were actually found, so a typo or a renamed tool cannot silently break your submission.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"EXPECTED_TOOLS = {\n    \"run_command\": \"execution\", \"submit_patch\": \"execution\", \"get_status\": \"execution\",\n    \"read_file\": \"filesystem\", \"edit_file\": \"filesystem\", \"write_file\": \"filesystem\",\n    \"search_similar_code\": \"code intelligence\", \"get_code_neighbors\": \"code intelligence\",\n    \"get_code_subgraph\": \"code intelligence\",\n}\nTEXT_EXTS = {\".py\", \".md\", \".txt\", \".yaml\", \".yml\", \".json\", \".toml\", \".cfg\", \".sh\", \".ini\"}\n\ndef text_corpus(dirs, max_file_bytes=2_000_000, max_files=4000):\n    chunks, n = [], 0\n    for d in dirs:\n        if not d or not Path(d).exists():\n            continue\n        for p in limited_walk(d, 6):\n            if p.is_file() and p.suffix.lower() in TEXT_EXTS:\n                try:\n                    if p.stat().st_size <= max_file_bytes:\n                        chunks.append(p.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"ignore\"))\n                        n += 1\n                except OSError:\n                    pass\n            if n >= max_files:\n                break\n    return \"\\n\".join(chunks)\n\nHARNESS_DIRS = [] if DEMO_MODE else [DATA_DIR / d for d in (\"sandbox\", \"docker\")]\nSAMPLE_DIRS = [] if DEMO_MODE else [p for p in DATA_DIR.iterdir() if \"sample\" in p.name.lower() and p.is_dir()]\ncorpus_harness = text_corpus(HARNESS_DIRS)\ncorpus_sample = text_corpus(SAMPLE_DIRS)\n\nrows = []\nfor tool, cat in EXPECTED_TOOLS.items():\n    pat = re.compile(r\"\\b\" + re.escape(tool) + r\"\\b\")\n    r, h, s = bool(pat.search(README)), bool(pat.search(corpus_harness)), bool(pat.search(corpus_sample))\n    rows.append({\"tool\": tool, \"category\": cat, \"README\": \"✅\" if r else \"—\", \"harness sources\": \"✅\" if h else \"—\",\n                 \"sample submission\": \"✅\" if s else \"—\", \"verified\": r or h or s})\nTOOLS_DF = pd.DataFrame(rows)\n\nhave_evidence = bool(README or corpus_harness or corpus_sample)\nAVAILABLE_TOOLS = [t for t in EXPECTED_TOOLS if TOOLS_DF.set_index(\"tool\").loc[t, \"verified\"]] if have_evidence else list(EXPECTED_TOOLS)\n\ndef _style_verified(v):\n    return \"background-color:#E6F4EA;color:#137333;font-weight:600\" if v is True else (\n           \"background-color:#FCE8E6;color:#C5221F;font-weight:600\" if v is False else \"\")\ndisplay(TOOLS_DF.style.hide(axis=\"index\").map(_style_verified, subset=[\"verified\"]))\n\nother = sorted(set(re.findall(r\"`([a-z_][a-z0-9_]{2,})\\(\", README)) - set(EXPECTED_TOOLS))\nif other:\n    callout(\"Other callables mentioned in the README (review, some may be tools): \"\n            + \", \".join(f\"<code>{esc(o)}</code>\" for o in other), \"info\")\nif not have_evidence:\n    callout(\"No harness files to verify against → assuming the default tool list.\", \"warn\")\nmissing = [t for t in EXPECTED_TOOLS if t not in AVAILABLE_TOOLS]\nif missing and have_evidence:\n    callout(\"Not found anywhere, excluded from the bundle: \" + \", \".join(f\"<code>{m}</code>\" for m in missing), \"warn\")\nprint(\"AVAILABLE_TOOLS =\", AVAILABLE_TOOLS)","metadata":{},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"<a id=\"eda\"></a>\n# 📊 4. Task EDA\n### Loading `tasks.jsonl` and its schema","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"DEMO_TASKS = [\n    {\"instance_id\": \"demo_1\", \"repo\": \"demo/pkg\", \"base_commit\": \"0\" * 40,\n     \"problem_statement\": \"`parse_query` crashes on empty input.\\n\\nTraceback (most recent call last):\\nValueError\",\n     \"hints_text\": \"\", \"created_at\": \"2026-05-01T00:00:00Z\",\n     \"patch\": \"--- a/pkg/query.py\\n+++ b/pkg/query.py\\n@@ -1,3 +1,5 @@ def parse_query(s):\\n     x = 1\\n+    if not s:\\n+        return {}\\n     y = 2\\n     z = 3\\n\",\n     \"test_patch\": \"--- a/tests/test_query.py\\n+++ b/tests/test_query.py\\n@@ -1,1 +1,3 @@\\n import pkg\\n+def test_empty():\\n+    assert pkg.parse_query('') == {}\\n\"},\n    {\"instance_id\": \"demo_2\", \"repo\": \"demo/pkg\", \"base_commit\": \"1\" * 40,\n     \"problem_statement\": \"Headers with underscores are silently accepted.\", \"hints_text\": \"see headers.py\",\n     \"created_at\": \"2026-06-01T00:00:00Z\",\n     \"patch\": \"diff --git a/pkg/headers.py b/pkg/headers.py\\n--- a/pkg/headers.py\\n+++ b/pkg/headers.py\\n@@ -10,2 +10,2 @@ class Headers:\\n-    allow = True\\n+    allow = False\\n     pass\\n\",\n     \"test_patch\": \"\"},\n]\n\nif not DEMO_MODE:\n    df = pd.read_json(DATA_DIR / \"tasks.jsonl\", lines=True, dtype=False)\nelse:\n    df = pd.DataFrame(DEMO_TASKS)\n\n# normalise the columns this notebook relies on\nif \"instance_id\" not in df.columns:\n    df[\"instance_id\"] = df[\"id\"] if \"id\" in df.columns else [f\"task_{i}\" for i in range(len(df))]\nfor col in [\"problem_statement\", \"hints_text\", \"patch\", \"test_patch\", \"base_commit\"]:\n    if col not in df.columns:\n        df[col] = \"\"\n    df[col] = df[col].fillna(\"\").astype(str)\nif \"repo\" not in df.columns:\n    df[\"repo\"] = df[\"instance_id\"].str.rsplit(\"_\", n=1).str[0]\ndf[\"created_at\"] = pd.to_datetime(df.get(\"created_at\"), errors=\"coerce\", utc=True)\n\ndef schema_table(frame):\n    rows = []\n    for c in frame.columns:\n        s = frame[c]\n        filled = s.notna() & s.astype(str).str.strip().ne(\"\") & s.astype(str).ne(\"NaT\")\n        example = next((v for v in s if isinstance(v, str) and v.strip()), s.iloc[0] if len(s) else \"\")\n        rows.append({\"column\": c, \"dtype\": str(s.dtype), \"filled %\": round(100 * filled.mean(), 1),\n                     \"unique\": s.astype(str).nunique(),\n                     \"example\": textwrap.shorten(str(example).replace(\"\\n\", \" ⏎ \"), 80)})\n    return pd.DataFrame(rows)\n\nprint(f\"{len(df)} tasks · {df['repo'].nunique()} repositories · {len(df.columns)} columns\")\ndisplay(schema_table(df).style.hide(axis=\"index\")\n        .bar(subset=[\"filled %\"], color=\"#AECBFA\", vmin=0, vmax=100).format({\"filled %\": \"{:.0f}%\"})\n        .set_properties(**{\"text-align\": \"left\"}))","metadata":{},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### 🔬 A real unified-diff parser\nThe reference patches use plain `--- a/` / `+++ b/` headers, **often without** a `diff --git` line. Counting `diff --git` lines therefore reports \"1 file\" for every task. The parser below tracks hunk line counts, so it handles both styles and also catches malformed patches. That last part is useful later for checking your agent's output.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"HUNK_RE = re.compile(r\"^@@ -(\\d+)(?:,(\\d+))? \\+(\\d+)(?:,(\\d+))? @@(.*)$\")\nSYMBOL_RE = re.compile(r\"(?:async\\s+)?(?:def|class)\\s+([A-Za-z_]\\w*)\")\n\ndef _clean_path(p):\n    p = p.split(\"\\t\")[0].strip()\n    return p[2:] if p.startswith((\"a/\", \"b/\")) else p\n\ndef parse_patch(text):\n    \"\"\"Return {'files': [...], 'malformed': int, 'unterminated': bool}. Each file: path, added, deleted, hunks, symbols, status.\"\"\"\n    files, cur, old_path = [], None, None\n    rem_old = rem_new = 0\n    malformed = 0\n\n    def new_file(path):\n        f = {\"path\": path, \"added\": 0, \"deleted\": 0, \"hunks\": 0, \"symbols\": set(), \"status\": \"modified\", \"_hdr\": False}\n        files.append(f)\n        return f\n\n    for line in (text or \"\").splitlines():\n        if rem_old > 0 or rem_new > 0:                      # inside a hunk\n            tag = line[:1]\n            if tag == \"+\":\n                cur[\"added\"] += 1; rem_new -= 1\n                m = SYMBOL_RE.match(line[1:].strip());  m and cur[\"symbols\"].add(m.group(1))\n                continue\n            if tag == \"-\":\n                cur[\"deleted\"] += 1; rem_old -= 1\n                m = SYMBOL_RE.match(line[1:].strip());  m and cur[\"symbols\"].add(m.group(1))\n                continue\n            if tag == \" \" or line == \"\":\n                rem_old -= 1; rem_new -= 1\n                continue\n            if tag == \"\\\\\":\n                continue\n            malformed += 1\n            rem_old = rem_new = 0                            # fall through: treat as header\n        if line.startswith(\"\\\\\"):\n            continue\n        if line.startswith(\"diff --git \"):\n            m = re.match(r\"diff --git a/(\\S+) b/(\\S+)\", line)\n            cur = new_file(m.group(2) if m else line[11:].strip())\n            continue\n        if line.startswith(\"--- \"):\n            old_path = _clean_path(line[4:])\n            continue\n        if line.startswith(\"+++ \"):\n            new_path = _clean_path(line[4:])\n            if cur is None or cur[\"_hdr\"]:\n                cur = new_file(new_path)\n            cur[\"_hdr\"] = True\n            if new_path == \"/dev/null\":\n                cur[\"path\"], cur[\"status\"] = old_path, \"deleted\"\n            else:\n                cur[\"path\"] = new_path\n                if old_path == \"/dev/null\":\n                    cur[\"status\"] = \"added\"\n            continue\n        m = HUNK_RE.match(line)\n        if m and cur is not None:\n            rem_old = int(m.group(2)) if m.group(2) is not None else 1\n            rem_new = int(m.group(4)) if m.group(4) is not None else 1\n            cur[\"hunks\"] += 1\n            sm = SYMBOL_RE.search(m.group(5) or \"\")\n            if sm:\n                cur[\"symbols\"].add(sm.group(1))\n    for f in files:\n        f.pop(\"_hdr\", None)\n        f[\"symbols\"] = sorted(f[\"symbols\"])\n    return {\"files\": files, \"malformed\": malformed, \"unterminated\": rem_old > 0 or rem_new > 0}\n\ndef is_test_path(p):\n    p = p.lower()\n    return bool(re.search(r\"(^|/)(tests?|testing)/\", p) or re.search(r\"(^|/)test_[^/]*\\.py$\", p) or p.endswith(\"_test.py\") or p.endswith(\"conftest.py\"))\n\n# ── self-test: both header styles, symbols, hunk accounting ───────────────────────\n_t1 = parse_patch(\"--- a/x.py\\n+++ b/x.py\\n@@ -1,2 +1,3 @@ def foo():\\n a\\n+b\\n c\\n--- a/y.py\\n+++ b/y.py\\n@@ -5 +5 @@\\n-q\\n+r\\n\")\n_t2 = parse_patch(\"diff --git a/n.py b/n.py\\nnew file mode 100644\\n--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/n.py\\n@@ -0,0 +1,2 @@\\n+class Bar:\\n+    pass\\n\")\nassert [f[\"path\"] for f in _t1[\"files\"]] == [\"x.py\", \"y.py\"] and _t1[\"files\"][0][\"symbols\"] == [\"foo\"]\nassert _t1[\"files\"][1][\"added\"] == 1 and not _t1[\"unterminated\"] and _t1[\"malformed\"] == 0\nassert _t2[\"files\"][0][\"status\"] == \"added\" and _t2[\"files\"][0][\"symbols\"] == [\"Bar\"]\nprint(\"parse_patch self-test passed ✅\")","metadata":{},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def task_features(row):\n    p = parse_patch(row[\"patch\"])\n    src = [f for f in p[\"files\"] if not is_test_path(f[\"path\"])]\n    tp = parse_patch(row[\"test_patch\"])\n    new_tests = re.findall(r\"^\\+\\s*(?:async\\s+)?def\\s+(test_\\w+)\", row[\"test_patch\"], flags=re.M)\n    added = sum(f[\"added\"] for f in p[\"files\"]); deleted = sum(f[\"deleted\"] for f in p[\"files\"])\n    return pd.Series({\n        \"gold_files\": [f[\"path\"] for f in src],\n        \"gold_symbols\": sorted({s for f in src for s in f[\"symbols\"]}),\n        \"n_files\": len(p[\"files\"]), \"n_src_files\": len(src),\n        \"n_hunks\": sum(f[\"hunks\"] for f in p[\"files\"]),\n        \"added\": added, \"deleted\": deleted, \"churn\": added + deleted,\n        \"adds_new_file\": any(f[\"status\"] == \"added\" for f in p[\"files\"]),\n        \"n_test_files\": len(tp[\"files\"]), \"n_new_tests\": len(new_tests),\n        \"patch_ok\": (not p[\"malformed\"]) and (not p[\"unterminated\"]) and bool(p[\"files\"]),\n    })\n\nfeat = df.apply(task_features, axis=1)\ndf = pd.concat([df.drop(columns=[c for c in feat.columns if c in df.columns]), feat], axis=1)\n\ndf[\"problem_words\"] = df[\"problem_statement\"].str.split().str.len().fillna(0).astype(int)\ndf[\"has_hints\"] = df[\"hints_text\"].str.strip().ne(\"\")\nBINS, LABELS = [-1, 5, 20, 80, 300, np.inf], [\"XS (≤5)\", \"S (≤20)\", \"M (≤80)\", \"L (≤300)\", \"XL (>300)\"]\ndf[\"size_bucket\"] = pd.cut(df[\"churn\"], bins=BINS, labels=LABELS)\n\nfor col in [\"FAIL_TO_PASS\", \"PASS_TO_PASS\"]:           # present in some SWE-bench style dumps\n    if col in df.columns:\n        df[f\"n_{col.lower()}\"] = df[col].apply(lambda v: len(json.loads(v)) if isinstance(v, str) and v.startswith(\"[\") else (len(v) if isinstance(v, list) else np.nan))\n\nspan = (f\"{df['created_at'].min():%Y-%m} → {df['created_at'].max():%Y-%m}\" if df[\"created_at\"].notna().any() else \"n/a\")\nkpis([(\"Tasks\", f\"{len(df):,}\"), (\"Repositories\", df[\"repo\"].nunique()),\n      (\"Median churn (lines)\", int(df[\"churn\"].median())), (\"Single-file fixes\", f\"{(df['n_src_files'] == 1).mean():.0%}\"),\n      (\"With hints\", f\"{df['has_hints'].mean():.0%}\"), (\"Adds new tests\", f\"{(df['n_new_tests'] > 0).mean():.0%}\"),\n      (\"Well-formed patches\", f\"{df['patch_ok'].mean():.0%}\"), (\"Created\", span)])","metadata":{},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### 🎨 The benchmark at a glance","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"with safe(\"EDA dashboard\"):\n    repo_order = df[\"repo\"].value_counts().index.tolist()\n    rc = {r: PAL[i % len(PAL)] for i, r in enumerate(repo_order)}\n    fig, ax = plt.subplots(2, 3, figsize=(18, 9.5))\n    fig.suptitle(\"Gemma 4 Developer Agent: benchmark anatomy\", y=1.0)\n\n    # (a) tasks per repo\n    vc = df[\"repo\"].value_counts()\n    a = ax[0, 0]; a.barh(vc.index[::-1], vc.values[::-1], color=[rc[r] for r in vc.index[::-1]])\n    for i, v in enumerate(vc.values[::-1]):\n        a.text(v, i, f\" {v}\", va=\"center\", fontsize=9)\n    a.set_title(\"Tasks per repository\"); a.set_xlabel(\"tasks\")\n\n    # (b) difficulty buckets by repo\n    a = ax[0, 1]\n    ct = pd.crosstab(df[\"repo\"], df[\"size_bucket\"]).reindex(index=repo_order[::-1], columns=LABELS, fill_value=0)\n    left = np.zeros(len(ct))\n    for j, lab in enumerate(LABELS):\n        a.barh(ct.index, ct[lab], left=left, color=sns.color_palette(\"viridis\", len(LABELS))[j], label=lab)\n        left += ct[lab].values\n    a.set_title(\"Patch size buckets (churn = added + deleted)\"); a.legend(fontsize=8, ncol=2, loc=\"lower right\")\n\n    # (c) churn distribution (log)\n    a = ax[0, 2]\n    bins = np.logspace(0, np.log10(max(2, df[\"churn\"].max() + 1)), 30)\n    a.hist(df[\"churn\"].clip(lower=1), bins=bins, color=PAL[0], alpha=.85, edgecolor=\"white\")\n    a.set_xscale(\"log\"); a.xaxis.set_major_formatter(plt.matplotlib.ticker.FuncFormatter(lambda v, _: f\"{v:,.0f}\"))\n    a.axvline(df[\"churn\"].median(), color=PAL[1], ls=\"--\", lw=2, label=f\"median = {int(df['churn'].median())}\")\n    a.set_title(\"Reference patch churn (log scale)\"); a.set_xlabel(\"lines changed\"); a.legend()\n\n    # (d) files & hunks\n    a = ax[1, 0]\n    fcount = df[\"n_src_files\"].clip(upper=5).value_counts().sort_index()\n    hcount = df[\"n_hunks\"].clip(upper=8).value_counts().sort_index()\n    x = np.arange(1, 9)\n    a.bar(x - .2, [fcount.get(i, 0) for i in x], width=.4, label=\"source files\", color=PAL[3])\n    a.bar(x + .2, [hcount.get(i, 0) for i in x], width=.4, label=\"hunks\", color=PAL[4])\n    a.set_xticks(x, [str(i) if i < 8 else \"8+\" for i in x]); a.set_title(\"How spread out is a fix?\")\n    a.set_xlabel(\"count per task (files capped at 5+, hunks at 8+)\"); a.legend()\n\n    # (e) problem statement length by repo\n    a = ax[1, 1]\n    sns.boxplot(data=df.assign(problem_words=df[\"problem_words\"].clip(lower=1)), y=\"repo\", x=\"problem_words\",\n                hue=\"repo\", order=repo_order, hue_order=repo_order, palette=rc, legend=False, ax=a, fliersize=2)\n    a.set_xscale(\"log\"); a.set_title(\"Issue length (words, log)\"); a.set_ylabel(\"\"); a.set_xlabel(\"words\")\n    a.xaxis.set_major_formatter(plt.matplotlib.ticker.FuncFormatter(lambda v, _: f\"{v:,.0f}\"))\n    a.xaxis.set_minor_formatter(plt.matplotlib.ticker.NullFormatter())\n\n    # (f) timeline\n    a = ax[1, 2]\n    if df[\"created_at\"].notna().any():\n        tl = (df.dropna(subset=[\"created_at\"]).assign(month=lambda d: d[\"created_at\"].dt.strftime(\"%Y-%m\"))\n                .pivot_table(index=\"month\", columns=\"repo\", values=\"instance_id\", aggfunc=\"count\", fill_value=0))\n        tl = tl.reindex(columns=[r for r in repo_order if r in tl.columns])\n        tl.plot(kind=\"bar\", stacked=True, ax=a, color=[rc[r] for r in tl.columns], width=.85, legend=False)\n        a.set_title(\"When were the tasks created?\"); a.set_xlabel(\"\"); a.tick_params(axis=\"x\", rotation=45)\n    else:\n        a.text(.5, .5, \"no created_at column\", ha=\"center\"); a.axis(\"off\")\n    handles = [plt.Rectangle((0, 0), 1, 1, color=rc[r]) for r in repo_order]\n    fig.legend(handles, repo_order, loc=\"lower center\", ncol=min(6, len(repo_order)), bbox_to_anchor=(.5, -.03), frameon=False)\n    plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()","metadata":{},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"with safe(\"EDA takeaways\"):\n    single = (df[\"n_src_files\"] == 1).mean(); small = (df[\"churn\"] <= 20).mean()\n    q90 = int(df[\"churn\"].quantile(.9)); multi_hunk = (df[\"n_hunks\"] > 1).mean()\n    top_repo = df[\"repo\"].value_counts()\n    txt = f\"\"\"\n#### 📌 Computed takeaways\n- **{single:.0%}** of fixes touch exactly one source file, and **{multi_hunk:.0%}** need more than one hunk. So the agent must be able to make *several coordinated edits*, not just one.\n- **{small:.0%}** of reference patches change ≤ 20 lines, while the 90th percentile is **{q90} lines**. There is a long tail of larger feature-style changes.\n- **{(df['n_new_tests'] > 0).mean():.0%}** of tasks add new test functions. Those hidden tests check behaviour the issue describes, so reproduce it yourself before you fix it.\n- The biggest repository (`{top_repo.index[0]}`) accounts for **{top_repo.iloc[0] / len(df):.0%}** of tasks. Repo-specific prompt hints (layout, test commands) can pay off.\n- **{df['adds_new_file'].mean():.0%}** of fixes create a new file, so `write_file` matters, but rarely.\n\"\"\"\n    display(Markdown(txt))","metadata":{},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"<a id=\"signals\"></a>\n# 🧭 5. Localization signals\nAn agent that edits the wrong file cannot pass. How often does the issue text itself *point at* the fix? We check, per task, whether the problem statement mentions a gold file (path or module name), a gold symbol (function/class changed by the reference patch), contains a traceback, or includes a code block.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"GENERIC_STEMS = {\"__init__\", \"utils\", \"util\", \"main\", \"base\", \"core\", \"types\", \"models\", \"helpers\", \"common\", \"compat\"}\n\ndef mentions(text, needles):\n    t = text.lower()\n    return any(re.search(r\"(?<![\\w/])\" + re.escape(n.lower()) + r\"(?![\\w])\", t) for n in needles if len(n) >= 3)\n\ndef signal_row(r):\n    text = r[\"problem_statement\"] + \"\\n\" + r[\"hints_text\"]\n    paths = r[\"gold_files\"]\n    stems = [Path(p).stem for p in paths if Path(p).stem not in GENERIC_STEMS and len(Path(p).stem) >= 4]\n    return pd.Series({\n        \"mentions_file_path\": mentions(text, paths),\n        \"mentions_module\": mentions(text, stems),\n        \"mentions_symbol\": mentions(text, r[\"gold_symbols\"]),\n        \"has_traceback\": \"Traceback (most recent call last)\" in text,\n        \"has_code_block\": \"```\" in text or \"\\n    \" in r[\"problem_statement\"],\n        \"has_hints\": r[\"has_hints\"],\n    })\n\nSIGNALS = df.apply(signal_row, axis=1)\ndf = pd.concat([df.drop(columns=[c for c in SIGNALS.columns if c in df.columns and c != \"has_hints\"]),\n                SIGNALS.drop(columns=[\"has_hints\"])], axis=1)\ndf[\"any_location_hint\"] = df[[\"mentions_file_path\", \"mentions_module\", \"mentions_symbol\", \"has_traceback\"]].any(axis=1)\n\nwith safe(\"signals chart\"):\n    sig_cols = [\"mentions_file_path\", \"mentions_module\", \"mentions_symbol\", \"has_traceback\", \"has_code_block\", \"has_hints\", \"any_location_hint\"]\n    by_repo = df.groupby(\"repo\")[sig_cols].mean().T * 100\n    by_repo[\"ALL\"] = df[sig_cols].mean() * 100\n    fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(11, 4.6))\n    sns.heatmap(by_repo, annot=True, fmt=\".0f\", cmap=\"Blues\", cbar_kws={\"label\": \"% of tasks\"}, ax=ax, vmin=0, vmax=100)\n    ax.set_title(\"What does the issue text reveal about the fix location?  (% of tasks)\")\n    ax.set_ylabel(\"\"); ax.set_xlabel(\"\")\n    plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()\n    no_hint = 1 - df[\"any_location_hint\"].mean()\n    callout(f\"<b>{no_hint:.0%}</b> of tasks give <i>no direct location hint</i> (no file, module, symbol or traceback). \"\n            \"For these, the agent has to search. That is where graph and embedding tools or a grep-first strategy earn their keep.\", \"info\")","metadata":{},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"with safe(\"hot files\"):\n    hot = (df[[\"repo\", \"gold_files\"]].explode(\"gold_files\").dropna()\n             .groupby([\"repo\", \"gold_files\"]).size().rename(\"tasks\").reset_index()\n             .sort_values(\"tasks\", ascending=False).head(15))\n    hot[\"share of repo\"] = hot.apply(lambda r: r[\"tasks\"] / (df[\"repo\"] == r[\"repo\"]).sum(), axis=1)\n    display(Markdown(\"#### 🔥 Hot files: where reference fixes land most often\"))\n    display(hot.rename(columns={\"gold_files\": \"file\"}).style.hide(axis=\"index\")\n            .bar(subset=[\"tasks\"], color=\"#FAD2CF\").format({\"share of repo\": \"{:.0%}\"}))","metadata":{},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### 🃏 Task card viewer\nCall `show_task(\"<instance_id>\")` on any task to see the issue, the stats and a colour-coded reference diff. Use it to build intuition and to debug your agent's trajectories.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def render_diff(diff, max_lines=70):\n    out = []\n    for i, line in enumerate((diff or \"\").splitlines()):\n        if i >= max_lines:\n            out.append('<span style=\"color:#5F6368\">… truncated …</span>'); break\n        if line.startswith(\"+\") and not line.startswith(\"+++\"):\n            style = \"color:#137333;background:#E6F4EA\"\n        elif line.startswith(\"-\") and not line.startswith(\"---\"):\n            style = \"color:#C5221F;background:#FCE8E6\"\n        elif line.startswith(\"@@\"):\n            style = \"color:#1A73E8;font-weight:600\"\n        elif line.startswith((\"---\", \"+++\", \"diff \")):\n            style = \"color:#5F6368;font-weight:600\"\n        else:\n            style = \"color:#202124\"\n        out.append(f'<span style=\"{style}\">{esc(line) or \"&nbsp;\"}</span>')\n    return ('<pre style=\"font-size:12px;line-height:1.4;background:#F8F9FA;padding:10px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;margin:0\">'\n            + \"\\n\".join(out) + \"</pre>\")\n\ndef badge(text, color=\"#1A73E8\"):\n    return f'<span style=\"background:{color};color:#fff;border-radius:10px;padding:2px 9px;margin-right:5px;font-size:12px\">{esc(text)}</span>'\n\ndef show_task(instance_id, max_problem_chars=1800):\n    r = df.loc[df[\"instance_id\"] == instance_id]\n    if r.empty:\n        callout(f\"Unknown instance_id <code>{esc(instance_id)}</code>\", \"err\"); return\n    r = r.iloc[0]\n    problem = r[\"problem_statement\"]\n    problem = problem[:max_problem_chars] + (\"\\n…\" if len(problem) > max_problem_chars else \"\")\n    flags = \"\".join(badge(k.replace(\"_\", \" \"), \"#34A853\") for k in\n                    [\"mentions_file_path\", \"mentions_symbol\", \"has_traceback\", \"has_hints\"] if bool(r.get(k)))\n    display(HTML(f\"\"\"\n    <div style=\"border:1px solid #DADCE0;border-radius:14px;padding:16px 18px;color:#202124;background:#fff\">\n      <div style=\"font-size:20px;font-weight:700\">{esc(r['instance_id'])}\n        <span style=\"font-size:13px;color:#5F6368;font-weight:400\">· {esc(r['repo'])} @ {esc(str(r['base_commit'])[:10])}</span></div>\n      <div style=\"margin:8px 0\">{badge(f\"{r['n_src_files']} file(s)\")}{badge(f\"{r['n_hunks']} hunk(s)\", \"#A142F4\")}\n        {badge(f\"+{r['added']} / -{r['deleted']}\", \"#EA4335\")}{badge(str(r['size_bucket']), \"#5F6368\")}{flags}</div>\n      <div style=\"font-size:12px;color:#5F6368\">gold files: <code>{esc(', '.join(r['gold_files']))}</code>\n        · symbols: <code>{esc(', '.join(r['gold_symbols']) or '—')}</code></div>\n      <h4 style=\"margin:12px 0 4px 0\">📝 Issue</h4>{pre(problem, 40)}\n      <h4 style=\"margin:12px 0 4px 0\">🩹 Reference patch</h4>{render_diff(r['patch'])}\n      <details style=\"margin-top:8px\"><summary style=\"cursor:pointer;font-weight:600\">🧪 Test patch ({r['n_new_tests']} new test functions)</summary>{render_diff(r['test_patch'], 50)}</details>\n    </div>\"\"\"))\n\nmedian_task = df.iloc[(df[\"churn\"] - df[\"churn\"].median()).abs().argsort().iloc[0]][\"instance_id\"]\nshow_task(median_task)","metadata":{},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"<a id=\"graphs\"></a>\n# 🕸️ 6. Code intelligence: call graphs & embeddings\nThe dataset ships pre-computed code graphs and symbol embeddings. These are what the graph and search tools query. The file format is **not assumed**: the loaders below accept node-link JSON (`nodes` + `edges`/`links`), GraphML, pickled NetworkX graphs, `.npz`/`.npy` matrices and gzip variants, and they report what they actually found.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"_LISTING = {}\ndef list_files(subdir, max_depth=3):\n    if subdir not in _LISTING:\n        base = (DATA_DIR / subdir) if DATA_DIR else None\n        _LISTING[subdir] = [p for p in limited_walk(base, max_depth) if p.is_file()] if base and base.exists() else []\n    return _LISTING[subdir]\n\ndef find_artifact(subdir, row):\n    \"\"\"Best file in `subdir` for a task: match instance_id, then base_commit, then repo name.\"\"\"\n    files = list_files(subdir)\n    if not files:\n        return None\n    repo = str(row.get(\"repo\", \"\"))\n    keys = [str(row[\"instance_id\"]), str(row.get(\"base_commit\", \"\"))[:12], str(row.get(\"base_commit\", \"\"))[:7],\n            repo.replace(\"/\", \"__\"), repo.split(\"/\")[-1]]\n    for k in [k for k in keys if k and len(k) >= 4]:\n        hits = [p for p in files if k in str(p.relative_to(DATA_DIR / subdir))]\n        if hits:\n            return sorted(hits, key=lambda p: len(str(p)))[0]\n    return None\n\ndef load_any(path):\n    path = Path(path)\n    name = path.name.lower()\n    opener = gzip.open if name.endswith(\".gz\") else open\n    stem = name[:-3] if name.endswith(\".gz\") else name\n    if stem.endswith(\".json\"):\n        with opener(path, \"rt\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n            return json.load(f)\n    if stem.endswith(\".jsonl\"):\n        with opener(path, \"rt\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n            return [json.loads(l) for l in f if l.strip()]\n    if stem.endswith(\".graphml\"):\n        return nx.read_graphml(path)\n    if stem.endswith((\".gpickle\", \".pkl\", \".pickle\")):\n        with opener(path, \"rb\") as f:        # competition-provided file; do not unpickle untrusted data\n            return pickle.load(f)\n    if stem.endswith(\".npz\"):\n        return dict(np.load(path, allow_pickle=True))\n    if stem.endswith(\".npy\"):\n        return np.load(path, allow_pickle=True)\n    if stem.endswith(\".parquet\"):\n        return pd.read_parquet(path)\n    raise ValueError(f\"unsupported format: {path.name}\")\n\n_SRC_KEYS, _DST_KEYS = (\"source\", \"src\", \"from\", \"caller\", \"u\"), (\"target\", \"dst\", \"to\", \"callee\", \"v\")\n_TYPE_KEYS = (\"type\", \"kind\", \"relation\", \"label\", \"edge_type\", \"rel\")\n\ndef to_graph(obj):\n    if isinstance(obj, nx.Graph):\n        return nx.DiGraph(obj)\n    if isinstance(obj, dict):\n        for k in (\"graph\", \"data\", \"code_graph\"):\n            if isinstance(obj.get(k), dict) and \"nodes\" in obj[k]:\n                obj = obj[k]\n        nodes, edges = obj.get(\"nodes\"), obj.get(\"edges\", obj.get(\"links\"))\n        if nodes is None or edges is None:\n            return None\n        G = nx.DiGraph()\n        items = nodes.items() if isinstance(nodes, dict) else ((None, n) for n in nodes)\n        for key, n in items:\n            if isinstance(n, dict):\n                nid = key if key is not None else next((n[k] for k in (\"id\", \"name\", \"qualname\", \"qualified_name\") if k in n), None)\n                G.add_node(nid, **{k: v for k, v in n.items() if isinstance(v, (str, int, float, bool)) and k != \"id\"})\n            else:\n                G.add_node(key if key is not None else n)\n        for e in edges:\n            if isinstance(e, dict):\n                s = next((e[k] for k in _SRC_KEYS if k in e), None); t = next((e[k] for k in _DST_KEYS if k in e), None)\n                typ = next((e[k] for k in _TYPE_KEYS if k in e), \"edge\")\n            elif isinstance(e, (list, tuple)) and len(e) >= 2:\n                s, t = e[0], e[1]; typ = e[2] if len(e) > 2 and isinstance(e[2], str) else \"edge\"\n            else:\n                continue\n            if s is not None and t is not None:\n                G.add_edge(s, t, type=str(typ))\n        return G\n    return None\n\ndef node_tail(n):\n    return re.split(r\"[.:/#]\", str(n))[-1]\n\ndef node_label(n):\n    parts = [p for p in re.split(r\"[.:/#]\", str(n)) if p]\n    return \".\".join(parts[-2:]) if len(parts) > 1 else str(n)\n\ndef symbol_nodes(G, symbols):\n    symbols = set(symbols)\n    return [n for n in G.nodes if node_tail(n) in symbols or G.nodes[n].get(\"name\") in symbols]\n\nprint(\"graph files:\", len(list_files(\"graphs\")), \"· embedding files:\", len(list_files(\"embeddings\")),\n      \"· snapshot entries:\", len(list((DATA_DIR / \"snapshots\").iterdir())) if DATA_DIR and (DATA_DIR / \"snapshots\").is_dir() else 0)","metadata":{},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"_GRAPH_CACHE = {}\ndef graph_for(row):\n    p = find_artifact(\"graphs\", row)\n    if p is None:\n        return None, None\n    if p not in _GRAPH_CACHE:\n        _GRAPH_CACHE[p] = to_graph(load_any(p))\n        if len(_GRAPH_CACHE) > 8:                 # keep memory bounded\n            _GRAPH_CACHE.pop(next(iter(_GRAPH_CACHE)))\n    return _GRAPH_CACHE[p], p\n\nwith safe(\"graph coverage scan\"):\n    t0, recs = time.time(), []\n    for _, r in df.head(CFG[\"graph_scan_max_tasks\"]).iterrows():\n        if time.time() - t0 > CFG[\"time_budget_s\"]:\n            break\n        G, p = graph_for(r)\n        if G is None:\n            continue\n        recs.append({\"instance_id\": r[\"instance_id\"], \"nodes\": G.number_of_nodes(), \"edges\": G.number_of_edges(),\n                     \"gold_symbol_in_graph\": bool(r[\"gold_symbols\"]) and bool(symbol_nodes(G, r[\"gold_symbols\"])),\n                     \"has_gold_symbols\": bool(r[\"gold_symbols\"])})\n    GRAPH_SCAN = pd.DataFrame(recs)\n    if GRAPH_SCAN.empty:\n        callout(\"No parseable code graphs found. Check the format in the inventory above and extend <code>to_graph</code>.\", \"warn\")\n    else:\n        sub = GRAPH_SCAN[GRAPH_SCAN[\"has_gold_symbols\"]]\n        kpis([(\"Tasks scanned\", len(GRAPH_SCAN)), (\"Median nodes\", int(GRAPH_SCAN[\"nodes\"].median())),\n              (\"Median edges\", int(GRAPH_SCAN[\"edges\"].median())),\n              (\"Gold symbol is a graph node\", f\"{sub['gold_symbol_in_graph'].mean():.0%}\" if len(sub) else \"n/a\")])","metadata":{},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def plot_fix_neighbourhood(instance_id=None, max_nodes=40):\n    rows = df[df[\"gold_symbols\"].str.len() > 0]\n    row = rows[rows[\"instance_id\"] == instance_id].iloc[0] if instance_id else None\n    if row is None:\n        for _, r in rows.iterrows():                       # first task whose gold symbol is in its graph\n            G, _ = graph_for(r)\n            if G is not None and symbol_nodes(G, r[\"gold_symbols\"]):\n                row = r; break\n    if row is None:\n        callout(\"No task with a gold symbol present in its graph.\", \"warn\"); return\n    G, path = graph_for(row)\n    centers = symbol_nodes(G, row[\"gold_symbols\"])\n    center = max(centers, key=G.degree)\n    und = G.to_undirected(as_view=True)\n    ego = set(nx.ego_graph(und, center, radius=1).nodes)\n    if len(ego) < 8:\n        ego |= set(nx.ego_graph(und, center, radius=2).nodes)\n    ego = sorted(ego, key=lambda n: (n != center, -G.degree(n)))[:max_nodes]\n    H = G.subgraph(ego)\n\n    etypes = sorted({d.get(\"type\", \"edge\") for _, _, d in H.edges(data=True)})\n    ecol = {t: PAL[(i + 4) % len(PAL)] for i, t in enumerate(etypes)}\n    pos = nx.spring_layout(H, seed=CFG[\"seed\"], k=1.2 / np.sqrt(max(len(H), 1)))\n    fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(13, 8))\n    for t in etypes:\n        el = [(u, v) for u, v, d in H.edges(data=True) if d.get(\"type\", \"edge\") == t]\n        nx.draw_networkx_edges(H, pos, edgelist=el, edge_color=ecol[t], arrows=True, arrowsize=12, width=1.4, alpha=.75, ax=ax,\n                               connectionstyle=\"arc3,rad=0.08\")\n    others = [n for n in H if n != center]\n    nx.draw_networkx_nodes(H, pos, nodelist=others, node_size=[250 + 60 * H.degree(n) for n in others],\n                           node_color=PAL[0], alpha=.85, ax=ax)\n    nx.draw_networkx_nodes(H, pos, nodelist=[center], node_size=1600, node_color=PAL[1], edgecolors=\"#202124\", linewidths=2, ax=ax)\n    nx.draw_networkx_labels(H, pos, labels={n: node_label(n) for n in H}, font_size=8, font_weight=\"bold\", ax=ax)\n    ax.legend(handles=[plt.Line2D([0], [0], color=ecol[t], lw=3, label=t) for t in etypes]\n              + [plt.Line2D([0], [0], marker=\"o\", color=\"w\", markerfacecolor=PAL[1], markersize=13, label=\"symbol changed by the fix\")],\n              loc=\"upper left\", frameon=True)\n    ax.set_title(f\"Where the fix lives: neighbourhood of `{node_tail(center)}` · {row['instance_id']}  \"\n                 f\"({G.number_of_nodes():,} nodes / {G.number_of_edges():,} edges in full graph)\")\n    ax.axis(\"off\"); plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()\n    top = sorted(G.nodes, key=lambda n: G.in_degree(n), reverse=True)[:8]\n    display(pd.DataFrame({\"most-referenced symbol\": [node_tail(n) for n in top], \"in-degree\": [G.in_degree(n) for n in top],\n                          \"full id\": [str(n) for n in top]}).style.hide(axis=\"index\"))\n\nwith safe(\"fix neighbourhood plot\"):\n    plot_fix_neighbourhood()","metadata":{},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def unpack_embeddings(obj):\n    \"\"\"Return (ids | None, matrix, summary DataFrame) from npz dict / array / DataFrame.\"\"\"\n    arrays = obj if isinstance(obj, dict) else {\"array\": obj}\n    if isinstance(obj, pd.DataFrame):\n        num = obj.select_dtypes(\"number\")\n        arrays = {\"matrix\": num.values, **{c: obj[c].values for c in obj.columns if c not in num.columns}}\n    summary = pd.DataFrame([{\"key\": k, \"shape\": tuple(np.shape(v)), \"dtype\": str(np.asarray(v).dtype)} for k, v in arrays.items()])\n    mats = {k: np.asarray(v) for k, v in arrays.items() if np.asarray(v).ndim == 2 and np.asarray(v).dtype.kind == \"f\"}\n    if not mats:\n        return None, None, summary\n    vk = max(mats, key=lambda k: mats[k].shape[0])\n    V = mats[vk].astype(np.float32)\n    ids = next((np.asarray(v).astype(str) for k, v in arrays.items()\n                if k != vk and np.asarray(v).ndim == 1 and len(v) == len(V) and np.asarray(v).dtype.kind in \"USO\"), None)\n    return ids, V, summary\n\ndef module_of(sym):\n    s = str(sym)\n    if \":\" in s:\n        return s.split(\":\")[0]\n    return s.rsplit(\".\", 1)[0] if \".\" in s else \"(root)\"\n\nwith safe(\"embedding explorer\"):\n    row = next((r for _, r in df.iterrows() if find_artifact(\"embeddings\", r) is not None and r[\"gold_symbols\"]), None)\n    if row is None:\n        callout(\"No embedding files found for any task.\", \"warn\")\n    else:\n        epath = find_artifact(\"embeddings\", row)\n        ids, V, summ = unpack_embeddings(load_any(epath))\n        display(Markdown(f\"**{epath.relative_to(DATA_DIR)}** contains:\")); display(summ.style.hide(axis=\"index\"))\n        if V is None:\n            callout(\"No float matrix found in this file.\", \"warn\")\n        else:\n            n = len(V)\n            idx = np.random.default_rng(CFG[\"seed\"]).choice(n, size=min(n, 6000), replace=False)\n            Xc = V[idx] - V[idx].mean(0)\n            _, _, vt = np.linalg.svd(Xc, full_matrices=False)\n            P = Xc @ vt[:2].T\n            kpis([(\"Vectors\", f\"{n:,}\"), (\"Dimension\", V.shape[1]), (\"Mean L2 norm\", f\"{np.linalg.norm(V, axis=1).mean():.2f}\")])\n            fig, ax = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(16, 6), gridspec_kw={\"width_ratios\": [1.5, 1]})\n            if ids is not None:\n                mods = pd.Series([module_of(ids[i]) for i in idx])\n                top_mods = mods.value_counts().index[:7].tolist()\n                for j, m in enumerate(top_mods):\n                    mask = (mods == m).values\n                    ax[0].scatter(P[mask, 0], P[mask, 1], s=12, alpha=.7, color=PAL[j], label=textwrap.shorten(m, 40))\n                rest = ~mods.isin(top_mods).values\n                ax[0].scatter(P[rest, 0], P[rest, 1], s=8, alpha=.3, color=\"#BDC1C6\", label=\"other\")\n                gold_pos = [k for k, i in enumerate(idx) if node_tail(ids[i]) in set(row[\"gold_symbols\"])]\n                for k in gold_pos:\n                    ax[0].scatter(P[k, 0], P[k, 1], s=300, marker=\"*\", color=PAL[1], edgecolors=\"#202124\", zorder=5)\n                    ax[0].annotate(node_tail(ids[idx[k]]), P[k], xytext=(6, 6), textcoords=\"offset points\", fontweight=\"bold\")\n                ax[0].legend(fontsize=8, markerscale=2, loc=\"best\")\n            else:\n                ax[0].scatter(P[:, 0], P[:, 1], s=10, alpha=.6)\n            ax[0].set_title(f\"Symbol embeddings (PCA) · {row['instance_id']} · ★ = changed by the fix\")\n            norms = np.linalg.norm(V, axis=1)\n            ax[1].hist(norms, bins=40, color=PAL[3], edgecolor=\"white\"); ax[1].set_title(\"L2 norm distribution\")\n            plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()\n\n            if ids is not None:\n                gold = [i for i, s in enumerate(ids) if node_tail(s) in set(row[\"gold_symbols\"])]\n                if gold:\n                    Vn = V / (np.linalg.norm(V, axis=1, keepdims=True) + 1e-9)\n                    sims = Vn @ Vn[gold[0]]\n                    order = [i for i in np.argsort(-sims) if i != gold[0]][:8]\n                    display(Markdown(f\"**Nearest neighbours of `{ids[gold[0]]}`**: roughly what similarity search would surface around the fix:\"))\n                    display(pd.DataFrame({\"symbol\": ids[order], \"cosine\": sims[order].round(3)}).style.hide(axis=\"index\")\n                            .bar(subset=[\"cosine\"], color=\"#AECBFA\").format({\"cosine\": \"{:.3f}\"}))","metadata":{},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"<a id=\"baseline\"></a>\n# 🎯 7. Localization baseline: can plain retrieval find the file?\nBefore spending GPU time on an agent, measure the difficulty of step 1. For each task we index every non-test `.py` file of the repository snapshot with **TF-IDF over code-aware tokens** (`get_request_handler` → `get request handler`, `CamelCase` → `camel case`) and rank files against the issue text.\n\n- **TF-IDF**: pure lexical similarity.\n- **TF-IDF + mention boost**: extra score when the issue explicitly names the file/module or a symbol *defined* in the file. This is what a disciplined `grep` step gives an agent.\n\n**Hit@k** = share of tasks where a gold source file is in the top-k. A low Hit@5 means the agent needs real exploration (graph tools, sub-agent), not just reading the top result.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import TfidfVectorizer\n\n_ID_RE = re.compile(r\"[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]+\")\n_PART_RE = re.compile(r\"[A-Z]?[a-z]+|[A-Z]+(?![a-z])|\\d+\")\ndef code_tokens(text):\n    out = []\n    for tok in _ID_RE.findall(text):\n        out.append(tok.lower())\n        parts = [p.lower() for p in _PART_RE.findall(tok) if len(p) > 1]\n        if len(parts) > 1:\n            out.extend(parts)\n    return out\n\ndef strip_common_prefix(files):\n    keys = list(files)\n    if len(keys) > 1:\n        first = {k.split(\"/\", 1)[0] for k in keys}\n        if len(first) == 1 and all(\"/\" in k for k in keys):\n            return strip_common_prefix({k.split(\"/\", 1)[1]: v for k, v in files.items()})\n    return files\n\ndef read_py_files(entry, max_files=8000, max_bytes=400_000):\n    files = {}\n    if entry.is_dir():\n        for p in entry.rglob(\"*.py\"):\n            try:\n                if p.stat().st_size <= max_bytes:\n                    files[str(p.relative_to(entry))] = p.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"ignore\")\n            except OSError:\n                pass\n            if len(files) >= max_files:\n                break\n    elif zipfile.is_zipfile(entry):\n        with zipfile.ZipFile(entry) as z:\n            for i in z.infolist():\n                if i.filename.endswith(\".py\") and i.file_size <= max_bytes and len(files) < max_files:\n                    files[i.filename] = z.read(i).decode(\"utf-8\", \"ignore\")\n    elif tarfile.is_tarfile(entry):\n        with tarfile.open(entry) as t:\n            for m in t:\n                if m.isfile() and m.name.endswith(\".py\") and m.size <= max_bytes and len(files) < max_files:\n                    files[m.name] = t.extractfile(m).read().decode(\"utf-8\", \"ignore\")\n    return strip_common_prefix(files)\n\ndef resolve_snapshot(row):\n    base = DATA_DIR / \"snapshots\" if DATA_DIR else None\n    if not base or not base.exists():\n        return None\n    entries = [p for p in limited_walk(base, 1)]\n    repo = str(row[\"repo\"])\n    for key in [row[\"instance_id\"], str(row[\"base_commit\"])[:12], str(row[\"base_commit\"])[:7], repo.replace(\"/\", \"__\"), repo.split(\"/\")[-1]]:\n        if key and len(key) >= 4:\n            hits = [p for p in entries if key in p.name]\n            if hits:\n                return sorted(hits, key=lambda p: (len(p.relative_to(base).parts), len(p.name)))[0]\n    return None\n\n_DEF_RE = re.compile(r\"^\\s*(?:async\\s+)?(?:def|class)\\s+([A-Za-z_]\\w*)\", re.M)\n_INDEX_CACHE = {}\ndef build_index(entry):\n    if entry not in _INDEX_CACHE:\n        files = {k: v for k, v in read_py_files(entry).items() if not is_test_path(k)}\n        paths = sorted(files)\n        if not paths:\n            _INDEX_CACHE[entry] = None\n        else:\n            docs = [(\" \".join([p.replace(\"/\", \" \")] * 3)) + \"\\n\" + files[p][:200_000] for p in paths]\n            vec = TfidfVectorizer(tokenizer=code_tokens, lowercase=False, token_pattern=None, sublinear_tf=True, max_features=300_000)\n            X = vec.fit_transform(docs)\n            defs = [set(_DEF_RE.findall(files[p])) for p in paths]\n            _INDEX_CACHE[entry] = (paths, vec, X, defs)\n        if len(_INDEX_CACHE) > 6:\n            _INDEX_CACHE.pop(next(iter(_INDEX_CACHE)))\n    return _INDEX_CACHE[entry]\n\ndef rank_files(index, query, boost=False):\n    paths, vec, X, defs = index\n    scores = (X @ vec.transform([query]).T).toarray().ravel()\n    if boost:\n        q = query.lower()\n        q_ids = {t for t in _ID_RE.findall(query) if len(t) >= 4 and (\"_\" in t or any(c.isupper() for c in t[1:]))}\n        for i, p in enumerate(paths):\n            stem = Path(p).stem\n            if p.lower() in q or (stem not in GENERIC_STEMS and len(stem) >= 4 and re.search(r\"\\b\" + re.escape(stem.lower()) + r\"\\b\", q)):\n                scores[i] += 1.0\n            scores[i] += min(1.0, 0.35 * len(q_ids & defs[i]))\n    return [paths[i] for i in np.argsort(-scores)]\n\ndef gold_rank(ranked, gold):\n    for k, p in enumerate(ranked, 1):\n        if any(p == g or p.endswith(\"/\" + g) or g.endswith(\"/\" + p) for g in gold):\n            return k\n    return None\n\nwith safe(\"localization baseline\"):\n    t0, recs = time.time(), []\n    for _, r in df.head(CFG[\"localization_max_tasks\"]).iterrows():\n        if time.time() - t0 > CFG[\"time_budget_s\"] or not r[\"gold_files\"]:\n            continue\n        entry = resolve_snapshot(r)\n        index = build_index(entry) if entry is not None else None\n        if index is None:\n            continue\n        query = r[\"problem_statement\"] + \"\\n\" + r[\"hints_text\"]\n        recs.append({\"instance_id\": r[\"instance_id\"], \"repo\": r[\"repo\"], \"n_files\": len(index[0]),\n                     \"rank_tfidf\": gold_rank(rank_files(index, query), r[\"gold_files\"]),\n                     \"rank_boost\": gold_rank(rank_files(index, query, boost=True), r[\"gold_files\"])})\n    LOC = pd.DataFrame(recs)\n    if LOC.empty:\n        callout(\"Could not index any repository snapshot (unknown layout?). Inspect <code>snapshots/</code> in the inventory \"\n                \"and adapt <code>resolve_snapshot</code> / <code>read_py_files</code>.\", \"warn\")\n    else:\n        ks = [1, 3, 5, 10, 20]\n        hit = lambda col, k: (LOC[col].fillna(1e9) <= k).mean()\n        fig, ax = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(16, 5.2))\n        for col, lab, c in [(\"rank_tfidf\", \"TF-IDF\", PAL[0]), (\"rank_boost\", \"TF-IDF + mention boost\", PAL[1])]:\n            ax[0].plot(ks, [hit(col, k) * 100 for k in ks], marker=\"o\", lw=2.5, color=c, label=lab)\n            for k in ks:\n                ax[0].annotate(f\"{hit(col, k):.0%}\", (k, hit(col, k) * 100), xytext=(0, 7), textcoords=\"offset points\", ha=\"center\", fontsize=8, color=c)\n        ax[0].set_xscale(\"log\"); ax[0].set_xticks(ks, [str(k) for k in ks]); ax[0].set_ylim(0, 112)\n        ax[0].set_xlabel(\"k (files shown to the agent)\"); ax[0].set_ylabel(\"Hit@k (%)\"); ax[0].legend()\n        ax[0].set_title(f\"How often is the gold file in the top-k?  ({len(LOC)} tasks)\")\n        per = LOC.groupby(\"repo\").apply(lambda g: pd.Series({\"Hit@1\": (g[\"rank_boost\"].fillna(1e9) <= 1).mean(),\n                                                            \"Hit@5\": (g[\"rank_boost\"].fillna(1e9) <= 5).mean(),\n                                                            \"Hit@10\": (g[\"rank_boost\"].fillna(1e9) <= 10).mean()})) * 100\n        per.plot(kind=\"barh\", ax=ax[1], color=[PAL[3], PAL[2], PAL[4]], width=.8)\n        ax[1].set_xlim(0, 100); ax[1].set_xlabel(\"%\"); ax[1].set_ylabel(\"\"); ax[1].set_title(\"Boosted retriever per repository\")\n        plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()\n        kpis([(\"Tasks evaluated\", len(LOC)), (\"Median repo size (py files)\", int(LOC[\"n_files\"].median())),\n              (\"Hit@1 (boost)\", f\"{hit('rank_boost', 1):.0%}\"), (\"Hit@5 (boost)\", f\"{hit('rank_boost', 5):.0%}\"),\n              (\"MRR (boost)\", f\"{(1 / LOC['rank_boost'].dropna()).sum() / len(LOC):.2f}\")])\n        gain = hit(\"rank_boost\", 5) - hit(\"rank_tfidf\", 5)\n        callout(f\"Explicit mentions add <b>{gain:+.0%}</b> Hit@5 on top of pure TF-IDF. \"\n                \"The system prompt below turns this into a rule: <i>grep for every identifier in the issue before reading files.</i>\", \"info\")","metadata":{},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"<a id=\"edit\"></a>\n# ✂️ 8. Edit mechanics & patch safety\n### A string-replacement `edit_file`, simulated\nAgents lose a lot of turns to failed or *silently broken* edits. The simulator below re-implements the common three-tier strategy (**exact → whitespace-flexible → token regex**) and, crucially, **checks that the result still parses**. It is a local model for building intuition, and the real harness implementation is authoritative.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import ast\n\ndef simulate_edit(content, old, new):\n    \"\"\"Return (new_content | None, tier, error | None).\"\"\"\n    c = content.count(old) if old else 0\n    if c == 1:\n        return content.replace(old, new), \"exact\", None\n    if c > 1:\n        return None, \"exact\", f\"old_string matches {c} times, so it must be unique\"\n    lines = content.split(\"\\n\")\n    target = [l.strip() for l in old.strip(\"\\n\").split(\"\\n\")]\n    if any(target):\n        hits = [i for i in range(len(lines) - len(target) + 1)\n                if all(lines[i + j].strip() == target[j] for j in range(len(target)))]\n        if len(hits) == 1:\n            i = hits[0]\n            indent = lines[i][: len(lines[i]) - len(lines[i].lstrip())]\n            block = [(indent + l) if l.strip() else \"\" for l in textwrap.dedent(new.strip(\"\\n\")).split(\"\\n\")]\n            return \"\\n\".join(lines[:i] + block + lines[i + len(target):]), \"flexible\", None\n        if len(hits) > 1:\n            return None, \"flexible\", f\"{len(hits)} whitespace-insensitive matches\"\n    toks = old.split()\n    if toks:\n        ms = list(re.finditer(r\"\\s+\".join(map(re.escape, toks)), content))\n        if len(ms) == 1:\n            return content[: ms[0].start()] + new + content[ms[0].end():], \"regex\", None\n        if len(ms) > 1:\n            return None, \"regex\", f\"{len(ms)} regex matches\"\n    return None, \"—\", \"old_string not found\"\n\ndef compiles(src):\n    try:\n        ast.parse(src); return True, \"\"\n    except SyntaxError as e:\n        return False, f\"{type(e).__name__}: {e.msg} (line {e.lineno})\"\n\nORIGINAL = \"\"\"def calculate_total(items):\n    total = 0\n    for item in items:\n        total += item.price\n    return total\n\"\"\"\nCASES = [\n    (\"Exact match, indentation included\", \"        total += item.price\",\n     \"        if item.is_valid:\\n            total += item.price\"),\n    (\"Exact match, indentation missing (classic pitfall)\", \"total += item.price\",\n     \"if item.is_valid:\\n    total += item.price\"),\n    (\"Wrong indentation in old_string → flexible tier\", \"for item in items:\\n    total += item.price\",\n     \"for item in items:\\n    if item.is_valid:\\n        total += item.price\"),\n    (\"Collapsed whitespace → regex tier\", \"total  +=   item.price\", \"total += item.price * item.qty\"),\n    (\"Ambiguous old_string\", \"total\", \"grand_total\"),\n]\nrows, outputs = [], {}\nfor name, old, new in CASES:\n    out, tier, err = simulate_edit(ORIGINAL, old, new)\n    ok, why = compiles(out) if out is not None else (None, \"\")\n    outputs[name] = out\n    rows.append({\"case\": name, \"tier\": tier, \"applied\": \"✅\" if out is not None else \"❌\",\n                 \"still parses\": \"✅\" if ok else (\"💥 \" + why if ok is False else \"—\"), \"error\": err or \"\"})\ndisplay(pd.DataFrame(rows).style.hide(axis=\"index\").set_properties(**{\"text-align\": \"left\"}))\ndetails(\"👀 See the silently broken result of the classic pitfall\", pre(outputs[CASES[1][0]]))\ncallout(\"<b>Lesson baked into the prompt below:</b> copy <code>old_string</code> verbatim <i>including leading whitespace</i>, \"\n        \"keep it short but unique, and run <code>python -m py_compile &lt;file&gt;</code> after every edit. \"\n        \"An edit that is applied but no longer parses is invisible until the hidden tests fail.\", \"info\")","metadata":{},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### 🧪 Patch sanity checker\nRun this on every patch your agent produces during local experiments. On the reference patches it doubles as a check of the parser itself.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"PROTECTED = (\"conftest.py\", \"pytest.ini\", \"tox.ini\", \"noxfile.py\", \"setup.cfg\", \"pyproject.toml\", \".github/\")\nSCRATCH_RE = re.compile(r\"(^|/)(repro|reproduce|scratch|debug|tmp|temp)[^/]*\\.py$\", re.I)\n\ndef check_patch(diff, max_churn=400):\n    \"\"\"Return a list of (severity, message). severity ∈ {'error', 'warn'}.\"\"\"\n    if not diff or not diff.strip():\n        return [(\"error\", \"empty patch\")]\n    info, issues = parse_patch(diff), []\n    if not info[\"files\"]:\n        issues.append((\"error\", \"no file headers found\"))\n    if info[\"malformed\"] or info[\"unterminated\"]:\n        issues.append((\"error\", \"hunk line counts do not match the @@ headers (corrupted diff)\"))\n    churn = 0\n    for f in info[\"files\"]:\n        churn += f[\"added\"] + f[\"deleted\"]\n        if is_test_path(f[\"path\"]):\n            issues.append((\"warn\", f\"modifies a test file: {f['path']}\"))\n        if any(f[\"path\"].endswith(p) or p in f[\"path\"] for p in PROTECTED):\n            issues.append((\"warn\", f\"touches config/harness file: {f['path']}\"))\n        if f[\"status\"] == \"added\" and SCRATCH_RE.search(f[\"path\"]):\n            issues.append((\"error\", f\"looks like a leftover scratch file: {f['path']}\"))\n    if churn > max_churn:\n        issues.append((\"warn\", f\"very large patch ({churn} changed lines)\"))\n    return issues\n\ngold_issues = Counter(msg.split(\":\")[0] for d in df[\"patch\"] for _, msg in check_patch(d))\nclean = sum(1 for d in df[\"patch\"] if not check_patch(d))\nkpis([(\"Reference patches checked\", len(df)), (\"Clean\", f\"{clean / max(len(df), 1):.0%}\")])\nif gold_issues:\n    display(pd.DataFrame(gold_issues.most_common(), columns=[\"finding on reference patches\", \"count\"]).style.hide(axis=\"index\"))\n\ndemo_bad = \"--- a/pkg/core.py\\n+++ b/pkg/core.py\\n@@ -1 +1 @@\\n-x = 1\\n+x = 2\\n--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/repro_issue.py\\n@@ -0,0 +1,1 @@\\n+print('debug')\\n\"\nprint(\"Example agent patch →\", check_patch(demo_bad))","metadata":{},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"<a id=\"prompts\"></a>\n# 🧠 9. Prompts & context budget\nThe prompts are **generated from `AVAILABLE_TOOLS`**, so they never reference a tool that does not exist. They encode the lessons from the sections above: grep-first localization, reproduce before fixing, whitespace-exact edits, compile checks, budget discipline, and a clean diff.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def build_system_prompt(architecture, tools=None):\n    tools = set(tools if tools is not None else AVAILABLE_TOOLS)\n    T = lambda name: name in tools\n    loc = []\n    if architecture == \"analyzer+coder\":\n        loc.append(\"Call the `code_analyzer` tool with the full issue text first. It returns LOCATION / ROOT CAUSE / FIX PLAN. Verify its claim by reading those exact lines before editing.\")\n    if T(\"run_command\"):\n        loc.append(\"Extract every identifier, error message and file name from the issue and search for them: `grep -rn \\\"<identifier>\\\" --include=*.py . | head -30`.\")\n    if T(\"search_similar_code\") and architecture == \"single\":\n        loc.append(\"When the issue describes behaviour without naming code, use `search_similar_code` with a short natural-language query.\")\n    if T(\"get_code_neighbors\") and architecture == \"single\":\n        loc.append(\"Use `get_code_neighbors` to follow callers/callees from a candidate function to the place where behaviour diverges.\")\n    how = ([\"`read_file` with a line range\"] if T(\"read_file\") else []) + ([\"`sed -n 'START,ENDp' FILE`\"] if T(\"run_command\") else [])\n    loc.append(\"Read only the lines you need\" + (f\" ({' or '.join(how)}).\" if how else \".\"))\n    loc_txt = \"\\n\".join(f\"   - {l}\" for l in loc)\n    edit_how = (\"edit source files with `edit_file`. Copy `old_string` verbatim from the file, *including leading indentation*, \"\n                \"and strip any line-number prefixes. Keep `old_string` short but unique.\"\n                if T(\"edit_file\") else\n                \"edit source files with a small Python snippet run via the shell (read the file, `str.replace` one unique, \"\n                \"whitespace-exact snippet, write it back) and assert that the snippet occurs exactly once.\")\n    status = (\"- Call `get_status` every ~8 tool calls. When less than 25% of turns or time remain, stop exploring and go straight to Fix → Verify → Submit.\\n\"\n              if T(\"get_status\") else \"- You have a limited number of turns: stop exploring after ~60% of your effort and move to Fix → Verify → Submit.\\n\")\n    return f\"\"\"You are an autonomous senior Python engineer working inside a sandboxed checkout of a real open-source repository at /workspace.\nGoal: resolve the issue in the user message with the smallest correct patch, then call `submit_patch`.\n\n## Hard rules\n- Never edit, add or delete tests, `conftest.py`, `pytest.ini`, CI or packaging files. Hidden tests are applied after you finish.\n- Keep public APIs backward compatible unless the issue explicitly asks for a change.\n- Scratch files go to /tmp only. Anything left in /workspace becomes part of your patch.\n- The environment is pre-built: do not try to install packages.\n- Always finish by calling `submit_patch`. A careful best-effort fix beats no patch.\n\n## Workflow\n1. **Understand**: state the expected vs. actual behaviour to yourself in one or two sentences.\n2. **Localize**:\n{loc_txt}\n3. **Reproduce**: write a minimal script to /tmp/repro.py that shows the bug and run it with `python /tmp/repro.py`.\n4. **Fix**: {edit_how} One logical change per edit. Fix the root cause, not the symptom, and also handle the edge cases the issue mentions.\n5. **Verify**: run `python -m py_compile <file>` after every edit, rerun /tmp/repro.py, then run the closest existing tests: `python -m pytest <tests/path> -x -q` (narrow with `-k`).\n6. **Submit**: run `git status` and `git diff`, make sure only intended source changes remain, then call `submit_patch`.\n\n## Budget discipline\n{status}- Keep outputs short: pipe through `head`, use `grep -n`, `pytest -q`. Never print whole large files.\n- If an edit fails twice, re-read the exact lines and retry with a smaller unique snippet.\n\n## Quality bar\n- Match the surrounding code style, type hints and naming.\n- Prefer a small, targeted change over a refactor. Touch other files only when the fix requires it.\n\"\"\"\n\ndef build_analyzer_prompt(available=None):\n    available = set(available if available is not None else AVAILABLE_TOOLS)\n    T = lambda name: name in available\n    tools = []\n    if T(\"run_command\"):\n        tools.append(\"`run_command` for READ-ONLY commands only: `grep -rn`, `ls`, `sed -n`, `git log -p -S`\")\n    if T(\"search_similar_code\"):\n        tools.append(\"`search_similar_code` for concepts the issue describes without naming code\")\n    if T(\"get_code_neighbors\"):\n        tools.append(\"`get_code_neighbors` to walk callers and callees\")\n    if T(\"get_code_subgraph\"):\n        tools.append(\"`get_code_subgraph` to see how a few candidate symbols connect\")\n    if T(\"read_file\"):\n        tools.append(\"`read_file` with tight line ranges to confirm\")\n    tools_txt = \"\\n\".join(f\"- {t}\" for t in tools)\n    return f\"\"\"You are `code_analyzer`, a read-only code navigation specialist. You never modify files.\nGiven an issue, find exactly where it must be fixed.\n\n## Tools\n{tools_txt}\n\n## Method\n1. Extract identifiers from the issue: function/class names, error messages, file paths, options.\n2. Search for each one, then follow the call chain until you reach the line where behaviour diverges from what the issue expects.\n3. Confirm by reading the actual code. Never guess line numbers.\n\n## Answer format (at most 250 words, nothing else)\nLOCATION: <path>:<start>-<end> (<function or class>)\nROOT CAUSE: <one or two sentences>\nFIX PLAN: <concrete change>\nRELATED: <other call sites or files needing the same change, or \"none\">\nTESTS: <existing test files that exercise this code>\nCONFIDENCE: high | medium | low\n\"\"\"\n\nARCH = CFG[\"architecture\"] if CFG[\"architecture\"] in (\"single\", \"analyzer+coder\") else \"analyzer+coder\"\nSYSTEM_PROMPT = build_system_prompt(ARCH)\nANALYZER_PROMPT = build_analyzer_prompt() if ARCH == \"analyzer+coder\" else \"\"\n\n# ── our prompts (the only change to this notebook) ──\nSYSTEM_PROMPT = r\"\"\"You are an autonomous senior Python engineer working inside a sandboxed checkout of a real open-source repository at /workspace.\nGoal: resolve the issue in the user message with the smallest correct patch, then call `submit_patch`.\n\n## How the session works\n- Every reply must contain a tool call until you have called `submit_patch`. Three replies in a row without a tool call end the session, and whatever is in the working tree is graded as it is.\n- Nobody answers questions. Decide and act.\n- After `submit_patch` the patch is frozen: reply with one short sentence and do not edit anything else.\n\n## Hard rules\n- Never edit, add or delete tests, `conftest.py`, `pytest.ini`, CI or packaging files. Hidden tests are applied after you finish.\n- Keep public APIs backward compatible unless the issue explicitly asks for a change.\n- Scratch files go to /tmp only, and only through `run_command`. `write_file`, `read_file` and `edit_file` accept only paths inside /workspace and reject `/tmp/...`. Anything left in /workspace becomes part of your patch.\n- The environment is pre-built and offline: do not try to install packages. `rg` and `tree` are not installed; use `git grep`, `grep`, `find`, `sed`.\n- Always finish by calling `submit_patch`. A careful best-effort fix beats no patch, and an empty patch always scores zero.\n\n## Workflow\n1. **Understand**: state the expected vs. actual behaviour to yourself in one or two sentences. The issue may be a pull-request description with template text (checklists, HTML comments): ignore the template and implement the change it describes, which can be a new feature, not only a bug fix.\n2. **Localize**:\n   - Call the `code_analyzer` tool with the full issue text first. It returns LOCATION / ROOT CAUSE / FIX PLAN. Verify its claim by reading those exact lines before editing.\n   - Extract every identifier, error message and file name from the issue and search for them: `git grep -n \"<identifier>\" -- '*.py' | head -30`.\n   - Read only the lines you need (`read_file` with a line range or `sed -n 'START,ENDp' FILE`).\n   - If a search finds nothing, do not repeat it: change the term (shorter name, class instead of method, error text) or search the tests for the behaviour instead.\n3. **Reproduce**: create a minimal script with `run_command`, for example `cat > /tmp/repro.py <<'EOF'` ... `EOF`, then run `python /tmp/repro.py`. If the observed behaviour contradicts the code you read, check which copy is imported: `python -c \"import <pkg>; print(<pkg>.__file__)\"` must point inside /workspace.\n4. **Fix**: edit source files with `edit_file`. Copy `old_string` verbatim from the file, *including leading indentation*, and strip any line-number prefixes. Keep `old_string` short but unique. One logical change per edit. Fix the root cause, not the symptom, and also handle the edge cases the issue mentions. When the issue needs a new source module or a new documentation example (for example under `docs_src/`), create it with `write_file` at its real repository path.\n5. **Verify**: run `python -m py_compile <file>` after every edit, rerun /tmp/repro.py, then run the closest existing tests: `python -m pytest <tests/path> -x -q -p no:anyio` (narrow with `-k`). Tests that already fail for unrelated reasons (missing fixtures, no network) are not your task.\n6. **Submit**: run `git status --short` and `git diff`, make sure only intended source changes remain (delete any scratch file inside /workspace), then call `submit_patch`.\n\n## Budget discipline\n- Call `get_status` every ~8 tool calls. When less than 25% of turns or time remain, stop exploring and go straight to Fix → Verify → Submit.\n- Make your first source edit as soon as the cause is clear; do not reproduce or re-explain the same failure repeatedly.\n- Keep outputs short: pipe through `head`, use `grep -n`, `pytest -q`. Never print whole large files.\n- Never run the same command twice without changing it.\n- If an edit fails twice, print the exact lines with `sed -n 'START,ENDp' FILE` and retry with a smaller unique snippet (2-3 lines). If it still fails, rewrite that small region with a short Python script run through `run_command` that asserts the old text occurs exactly once.\n\n## Quality bar\n- Match the surrounding code style, type hints and naming.\n- Prefer a small, targeted change over a refactor. Touch other files only when the fix requires it.\n\"\"\"\nANALYZER_PROMPT = r\"\"\"You are `code_analyzer`, a read-only code navigation specialist. You never modify files.\nGiven an issue, find exactly where it must be fixed.\n\n## Tools\n- `run_command` for READ-ONLY commands only: `git grep -n`, `grep -rn`, `ls`, `sed -n`. `rg` is not installed, and the git history stops at the current commit, so `git log` cannot show the fix.\n- `search_similar_code` with a symbol name taken from the code (for example `APIKeyHeader` or `parse_header`), never a sentence. Its ranking is weak: confirm every hit by reading the code.\n- `get_code_neighbors` to walk callers and callees. The graph has only synchronous \"calls\" edges and no async functions; a missing node proves nothing.\n- `get_code_subgraph` to see how a few candidate symbols connect\n- `read_file` with tight line ranges to confirm\n- If a graph tool returns an error, stop using graph tools and search the source.\n\n## Method\n1. Extract identifiers from the issue: function/class names, error messages, file paths, options. If the issue is a pull-request description, ignore its template text.\n2. Search for each one, then follow the call chain until you reach the line where behaviour diverges from what the issue expects. Do not repeat a search that returned nothing; change the term.\n3. Confirm by reading the actual code. Never guess line numbers.\n4. If the issue needs code that does not exist yet (a new function, module or documentation example), say so and name the file where it belongs.\n\n## Answer format (at most 250 words, nothing else)\nLOCATION: <path>:<start>-<end> (<function or class>)\nROOT CAUSE: <one or two sentences>\nFIX PLAN: <concrete change>\nRELATED: <other call sites or files needing the same change, or \"none\">\nTESTS: <existing test files that exercise this code>\nCONFIDENCE: high | medium | low\n\"\"\"\ndetails(\"📝 prompts/system.md\", pre(SYSTEM_PROMPT, 120))\nif ANALYZER_PROMPT:\n    details(\"🔍 prompts/analyzer.md\", pre(ANALYZER_PROMPT, 120))","metadata":{},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"est_tokens = lambda s: int(len(s) / 3.6)            # rough heuristic for English + code, not a real tokenizer\n\nwith safe(\"context budget\"):\n    tool_schema_tokens = 130 * (len(AVAILABLE_TOOLS) + (1 if ARCH == \"analyzer+coder\" else 0))\n    issue_tok = df[\"problem_statement\"].map(est_tokens)\n    scen = {\"median issue\": int(issue_tok.median()), \"p90 issue\": int(issue_tok.quantile(.9)), \"max issue\": int(issue_tok.max())}\n    comp_names = [\"system prompt\", \"tool schemas\", \"issue text\", \"reserved for output\"]\n    colors = [PAL[0], PAL[4], PAL[2], PAL[1]]\n    fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(14, 3.6))\n    for yi, (label, itok) in enumerate(scen.items()):\n        parts = [est_tokens(SYSTEM_PROMPT), tool_schema_tokens, itok, CFG[\"max_output_tokens\"]]\n        left = 0\n        for name, v, c in zip(comp_names, parts, colors):\n            ax.barh(yi, v, left=left, color=c, label=name if yi == 0 else None, edgecolor=\"white\")\n            left += v\n        free = CONTEXT_LIMIT - left\n        ax.barh(yi, max(free, 0), left=left, color=\"#E8EAED\", label=\"left for the trajectory\" if yi == 0 else None, edgecolor=\"white\")\n        ax.text(min(left, CONTEXT_LIMIT) + 200, yi, f\"{max(free, 0):,} tok free ({max(free, 0) / CONTEXT_LIMIT:.0%})\", va=\"center\", fontsize=9)\n    ax.axvline(CONTEXT_LIMIT, color=\"#202124\", ls=\"--\"); ax.set_yticks(range(len(scen)), list(scen))\n    ax.set_xlim(0, CONTEXT_LIMIT * 1.08); ax.set_xlabel(\"tokens (approx.)\")\n    ax.set_title(f\"Where does a {CONTEXT_LIMIT:,}-token context go before the first tool call?\")\n    ax.legend(ncol=5, loc=\"upper center\", bbox_to_anchor=(.5, -.28), frameon=False)\n    plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()\n    if CFG[\"max_output_tokens\"] > 0.35 * CONTEXT_LIMIT:\n        callout(f\"<code>max_output_tokens={CFG['max_output_tokens']:,}</code> reserves more than 35% of the context. \"\n                \"Every tool result competes for the remainder, so consider lowering it.\", \"warn\")\n    callout(\"Each <code>read_file</code> or command output lands in this grey area. That is why the analyzer sub-agent helps: \"\n            \"its exploration happens in <i>its own</i> context and only a short report comes back to the coder.\", \"info\")","metadata":{},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"<a id=\"bundle\"></a>\n# 🏗️ 10. Agent bundle builder\n**Step 1: learn from the official sample.** We locate `sample_submission` (as a folder or a zip), show its files, and detect the model alias, root file name and whether it uses `!include`.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"ROOT_NAMES = [\"agent.yaml\", \"agent.yml\", \"root_agent.yaml\", \"root_agent.yml\"]\n\nclass TolerantLoader(yaml.SafeLoader):\n    \"\"\"SafeLoader that keeps custom tags such as !include as {'__tag__': '!include', 'value': ...}.\"\"\"\ndef _tagged(loader, suffix, node):\n    if isinstance(node, yaml.ScalarNode):\n        val = loader.construct_scalar(node)\n    elif isinstance(node, yaml.SequenceNode):\n        val = loader.construct_sequence(node, deep=True)\n    else:\n        val = loader.construct_mapping(node, deep=True)\n    return {\"__tag__\": \"!\" + suffix, \"value\": val}\nTolerantLoader.add_multi_constructor(\"!\", _tagged)\n\ndef load_yaml(path):\n    return yaml.load(Path(path).read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\"), Loader=TolerantLoader)\n\ndef find_sample_submission():\n    if DEMO_MODE:\n        return None\n    for c in sorted(p for p in DATA_DIR.iterdir() if \"sample\" in p.name.lower()):\n        if c.is_dir():\n            ymls = sorted((p for p in c.rglob(\"*\") if p.suffix in (\".yaml\", \".yml\")), key=lambda p: len(p.parts))\n            if ymls:\n                return ymls[0].parent\n            zips = sorted(c.rglob(\"*.zip\"))\n            if zips:\n                c = zips[0]\n        if c.is_file() and zipfile.is_zipfile(c):\n            out = WORK / \"_sample_submission\"\n            shutil.rmtree(out, ignore_errors=True)\n            with zipfile.ZipFile(c) as z:\n                z.extractall(out)\n            ymls = sorted((p for p in out.rglob(\"*\") if p.suffix in (\".yaml\", \".yml\")), key=lambda p: len(p.parts))\n            return ymls[0].parent if ymls else out\n    return None\n\nSAMPLE_ROOT = find_sample_submission()\nSAMPLE = {\"root_file\": None, \"models\": set(), \"uses_include\": True, \"top_keys\": [], \"text\": \"\"}\nif SAMPLE_ROOT:\n    ytexts = {p: p.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"ignore\") for p in SAMPLE_ROOT.rglob(\"*\") if p.suffix in (\".yaml\", \".yml\")}\n    SAMPLE[\"text\"] = \"\\n\".join(ytexts.values())\n    SAMPLE[\"root_file\"] = next((SAMPLE_ROOT / n for n in ROOT_NAMES if (SAMPLE_ROOT / n).exists()), None)\n    SAMPLE[\"models\"] = {m for t in ytexts.values() for m in re.findall(r\"^\\s*model:\\s*['\\\"]?([^'\\\"\\s#]+)\", t, flags=re.M)}\n    SAMPLE[\"uses_include\"] = \"!include\" in SAMPLE[\"text\"]\n    with safe(\"sample root parsing\"):\n        if SAMPLE[\"root_file\"]:\n            SAMPLE[\"top_keys\"] = list((load_yaml(SAMPLE[\"root_file\"]) or {}).keys())\n    files = sorted(p for p in SAMPLE_ROOT.rglob(\"*\") if p.is_file())\n    display(Markdown(f\"**sample submission** at `{SAMPLE_ROOT}` · {len(files)} files\"))\n    for p in files[:25]:\n        if p.suffix.lower() in TEXT_EXTS:\n            details(f\"📄 {p.relative_to(SAMPLE_ROOT)} ({human_bytes(p.stat().st_size)})\",\n                    pre(p.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"ignore\"), 60))\nelse:\n    callout(\"No sample submission found. The bundle will be built from this notebook's templates only.\", \"warn\")\n\nif CFG[\"model_alias\"]:\n    MODEL_ALIAS, model_src = CFG[\"model_alias\"], \"CFG override\"\nelif len(SAMPLE[\"models\"]) == 1:\n    MODEL_ALIAS, model_src = next(iter(SAMPLE[\"models\"])), \"sample submission\"\nelse:\n    MODEL_ALIAS, model_src = CFG[\"fallback_model_alias\"], \"fallback (verify in HARNESS_README!)\"\nINCLUDE_STYLE = \"include\" if SAMPLE[\"uses_include\"] else \"inline\"\nROOT_FILE_NAME = SAMPLE[\"root_file\"].name if SAMPLE[\"root_file\"] else \"agent.yaml\"\nkpis([(\"Model alias\", MODEL_ALIAS), (\"alias source\", model_src), (\"Root file\", ROOT_FILE_NAME),\n      (\"Prompt style\", INCLUDE_STYLE), (\"Architecture\", ARCH)])","metadata":{},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Step 2: write the bundle.** Two architectures are available:\n\n| | `single` | `analyzer+coder` *(default)* |\n|:--|:--|:--|\n| Agents | one coder with every tool | coder + read-only `code_analyzer` wrapped as a tool |\n| Context | exploration fills the coder's context | exploration stays in the analyzer's context, and only a short report returns |\n| Best when | short issues, strong hints | vague issues, large repos |\n\nWith `bundle_base=\"sample\"`, the official sample is copied untouched and **only its instruction is replaced** with our system prompt. This is the most conservative option if the validator reports schema differences.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"BUNDLE = WORK / CFG[\"bundle_dir\"]\nshutil.rmtree(BUNDLE, ignore_errors=True)\nBUNDLE.mkdir(parents=True)\n\nSAMPLING_YAML = f\"\"\"temperature: {CFG['temperature']}\ntop_p: {CFG['top_p']}\ntop_k: {CFG['top_k']}\nmax_output_tokens: {CFG['max_output_tokens']}\nthinking_config:\n  thinking_budget: {CFG['thinking_budget']}\n  include_thoughts: false\n\"\"\"\n\ndef field_instruction(rel_path, text):\n    if INCLUDE_STYLE == \"include\":\n        return f\"instruction: !include {rel_path}\"\n    return \"instruction: |\\n\" + textwrap.indent(text.rstrip() + \"\\n\", \"  \").rstrip()\n\ndef field_sampling(rel_path):\n    if INCLUDE_STYLE == \"include\":\n        return f\"generate_content_config: !include {rel_path}\"\n    return \"generate_content_config:\\n\" + textwrap.indent(SAMPLING_YAML, \"  \").rstrip()\n\ndef write(rel, text):\n    p = BUNDLE / rel\n    p.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n    p.write_text(text.rstrip() + \"\\n\", encoding=\"utf-8\")\n\nT = lambda name: name in AVAILABLE_TOOLS\nCODER_TOOLS = [t for t in [\"run_command\", \"read_file\", \"edit_file\", \"write_file\", \"get_status\", \"submit_patch\"] if T(t)]\nif ARCH == \"single\":\n    CODER_TOOLS += [t for t in [\"search_similar_code\", \"get_code_neighbors\", \"get_code_subgraph\"] if T(t)]\nANALYZER_TOOLS = [t for t in [\"run_command\", \"read_file\", \"search_similar_code\", \"get_code_neighbors\", \"get_code_subgraph\"] if T(t)]\nif \"submit_patch\" not in CODER_TOOLS:\n    callout(\"<code>submit_patch</code> was not verified in the harness files, so the agent may be unable to finish. Check the README.\", \"err\")\n\nif CFG[\"bundle_base\"] == \"sample\" and SAMPLE_ROOT and SAMPLE[\"root_file\"]:\n    shutil.copytree(SAMPLE_ROOT, BUNDLE, dirs_exist_ok=True)\n    sample_tools = [t for t in ((load_yaml(SAMPLE[\"root_file\"]) or {}).get(\"tools\") or []) if isinstance(t, str)]\n    SYSTEM_PROMPT = build_system_prompt(\"single\", tools=sample_tools)     # only mention tools this agent really has\n    not_used = [t for t in CODER_TOOLS if t not in sample_tools]\n    if not_used:\n        callout(\"The sample agent does not declare these verified tools: \" + \", \".join(f\"<code>{t}</code>\" for t in not_used)\n                + \". The prompt was adapted to the sample's tools. Adding them is a likely easy win.\", \"info\")\n    root_path = BUNDLE / ROOT_FILE_NAME\n    lines = root_path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\").split(\"\\n\")\n    idx = next((i for i, l in enumerate(lines) if l.startswith(\"instruction:\")), None)\n    if idx is None:\n        callout(\"Sample root config has no top-level <code>instruction:</code>. Prompt not replaced.\", \"err\")\n    else:\n        m = re.match(r\"instruction:\\s*!include\\s+(\\S+)\", lines[idx])\n        if m:\n            (root_path.parent / m.group(1)).write_text(SYSTEM_PROMPT, encoding=\"utf-8\")\n        else:\n            end = idx + 1\n            while end < len(lines) and (lines[end].startswith((\" \", \"\\t\")) or not lines[end].strip()):\n                end += 1\n            lines[idx:end] = [\"instruction: |\", *textwrap.indent(SYSTEM_PROMPT.rstrip(), \"  \").split(\"\\n\")]\n            root_path.write_text(\"\\n\".join(lines), encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    callout(\"Bundle = official sample + our system prompt.\", \"ok\")\nelse:\n    write(\"prompts/system.md\", SYSTEM_PROMPT)\n    write(\"configs/sampling.yaml\", SAMPLING_YAML)\n    tools_yaml = \"\\n\".join(f\"  - {t}\" for t in CODER_TOOLS)\n    if ARCH == \"analyzer+coder\":\n        write(\"prompts/analyzer.md\", ANALYZER_PROMPT)\n        write(\"sub_agents/code_analyzer.yaml\", \"\\n\".join([\n            \"name: code_analyzer\",\n            f\"model: {MODEL_ALIAS}\",\n            \"description: Read-only code navigator. Given an issue, returns the exact location, root cause and a fix plan.\",\n            field_instruction(\"../prompts/analyzer.md\", ANALYZER_PROMPT),\n            field_sampling(\"../configs/sampling.yaml\"),\n            \"tools:\", *[f\"  - {t}\" for t in ANALYZER_TOOLS]]))\n        tools_yaml += \"\\n  - agent_tool:\\n      config_path: sub_agents/code_analyzer.yaml\\n      skip_summarization: true\"\n    write(ROOT_FILE_NAME, \"\\n\".join([\n        \"name: swe_coder\",\n        f\"model: {MODEL_ALIAS}\",\n        \"description: Autonomous software engineer that fixes repository issues with minimal, verified patches.\",\n        field_instruction(\"prompts/system.md\", SYSTEM_PROMPT),\n        field_sampling(\"configs/sampling.yaml\"),\n        \"tools:\", tools_yaml]))\n\ndef tree_lines(d, prefix=\"\"):\n    kids = sorted(d.iterdir(), key=lambda p: (p.is_file(), p.name))\n    out = []\n    for i, k in enumerate(kids):\n        last = i == len(kids) - 1\n        out.append(f\"{prefix}{'└── ' if last else '├── '}{k.name}{'/' if k.is_dir() else f'  ({human_bytes(k.stat().st_size)})'}\")\n        if k.is_dir():\n            out += tree_lines(k, prefix + (\"    \" if last else \"│   \"))\n    return out\n\ndef show_bundle(root):\n    files = sorted(p for p in root.rglob(\"*\") if p.is_file())\n    display(HTML(pre(\"\\n\".join([f\"{root.name}/\", *tree_lines(root)]))))\n    for p in files:\n        if p.suffix in (\".yaml\", \".yml\"):\n            details(f\"📄 {p.relative_to(root)}\", pre(p.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\")))\n\nshow_bundle(BUNDLE)\n\nif SAMPLE[\"top_keys\"] and CFG[\"bundle_base\"] != \"sample\":\n    ours = list((load_yaml(BUNDLE / ROOT_FILE_NAME) or {}).keys())\n    extra, absent = [k for k in ours if k not in SAMPLE[\"top_keys\"]], [k for k in SAMPLE[\"top_keys\"] if k not in ours]\n    if extra or absent:\n        callout(f\"Schema differs from the sample root config. Ours adds <code>{esc(extra)}</code>, sample-only <code>{esc(absent)}</code>. \"\n                \"Check HARNESS_README, or set <code>CFG['bundle_base']='sample'</code>.\", \"warn\")\n    else:\n        callout(\"Root config uses the same top-level keys as the official sample.\", \"ok\")","metadata":{},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"<a id=\"validate\"></a>\n# 🛡️ 11. Validator & packaging\nA pre-flight check modelled on the official rules. The allowed extensions and size limit are **parsed from the README when present**, and the source of every limit is shown. Checks: root config, YAML parses, every `!include`/`config_path` resolves *inside* the bundle, single base model, alias matches the sample, verified tools only, file types, size, hidden files, adapter completeness.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def readme_extensions():\n    for line in README.splitlines():\n        if re.search(r\"allowed|permitted|extension\", line, re.I):\n            exts = set(re.findall(r\"(?<![\\w/])\\.(yaml|yml|md|txt|py|json|safetensors|bin|toml|jinja|j2)\\b\", line, re.I))\n            if len(exts) >= 3:\n                return {\".\" + e.lower() for e in exts}, line.strip()\n    return None, None\n\ndef readme_size_limit():\n    for line in README.splitlines():\n        if re.search(r\"size|archive|limit|uncompressed\", line, re.I):\n            m = re.search(r\"(\\d+(?:\\.\\d+)?)\\s*(GiB|GB|MiB|MB)\\b\", line)\n            if m:\n                mult = {\"gib\": 1024**3, \"gb\": 1024**3, \"mib\": 1024**2, \"mb\": 1024**2}[m.group(2).lower()]\n                return int(float(m.group(1)) * mult), line.strip()\n    return None, None\n\n_exts, _ext_line = readme_extensions()\nALLOWED_EXTS = _exts or {\".yaml\", \".yml\", \".md\", \".txt\", \".py\", \".json\", \".safetensors\"}\n_size, _size_line = readme_size_limit()\nMAX_BYTES = _size or 3 * 1024**3\n\ndef walk_yaml(obj, fn, key=None):\n    fn(key, obj)\n    if isinstance(obj, dict):\n        for k, v in obj.items():\n            walk_yaml(v, fn, k)\n    elif isinstance(obj, list):\n        for v in obj:\n            walk_yaml(v, fn, key)\n\ndef validate_bundle(root, verbose=True):\n    root = Path(root).resolve()\n    checks = []\n    add = lambda status, name, detail=\"\": checks.append({\"status\": status, \"check\": name, \"detail\": detail})\n\n    roots = [n for n in ROOT_NAMES if (root / n).exists()]\n    add(\"✅\" if len(roots) == 1 else \"❌\", \"exactly one root config\", \", \".join(roots) or \"none found\")\n\n    parsed, models, tools_used, bad_refs = {}, set(), set(), []\n    for y in sorted(p for p in root.rglob(\"*\") if p.suffix in (\".yaml\", \".yml\")):\n        try:\n            parsed[y] = load_yaml(y)\n        except Exception as e:\n            add(\"❌\", f\"YAML parses: {y.relative_to(root)}\", str(e)[:120])\n    add(\"✅\" if parsed else \"❌\", \"YAML files parse\", f\"{len(parsed)} file(s)\")\n\n    for y, data in parsed.items():\n        def visit(key, v, y=y):\n            if isinstance(v, dict) and v.get(\"__tag__\") == \"!include\":\n                refs = [v[\"value\"]] if isinstance(v[\"value\"], str) else []\n            elif key == \"config_path\" and isinstance(v, str):\n                refs = [v]\n            else:\n                refs = []\n            for ref in refs:\n                target = (y.parent / ref).resolve()\n                if not target.exists() or root not in target.parents:\n                    bad_refs.append(f\"{y.relative_to(root)} → {ref}\")\n            if key == \"model\" and isinstance(v, str):\n                models.add(v)\n            if key == \"tools\" and isinstance(v, list):\n                for t in v:\n                    if isinstance(t, str):\n                        tools_used.add(t)\n        walk_yaml(data, visit)\n    add(\"✅\" if not bad_refs else \"❌\", \"all !include / config_path references resolve inside the bundle\", \"; \".join(bad_refs) or \"ok\")\n    add(\"✅\" if len(models) == 1 else \"❌\", \"single base model\", \", \".join(sorted(models)) or \"no model key found\")\n    if SAMPLE[\"models\"]:\n        add(\"✅\" if models <= SAMPLE[\"models\"] else \"⚠️\", \"model alias matches sample submission\", f\"sample: {sorted(SAMPLE['models'])}\")\n    unknown = sorted(tools_used - set(AVAILABLE_TOOLS))\n    add(\"✅\" if not unknown else \"⚠️\", \"only verified tools referenced\", \", \".join(unknown) or f\"{len(tools_used)} tools\")\n\n    files = [p for p in root.rglob(\"*\") if p.is_file()]\n    bad_ext = [str(p.relative_to(root)) for p in files if p.suffix.lower() not in ALLOWED_EXTS]\n    add(\"✅\" if not bad_ext else \"❌\", \"allowed file types\", \", \".join(bad_ext) or\n        f\"{sorted(ALLOWED_EXTS)} ({'README' if _exts else 'default, unverified'})\")\n    hidden = [str(p.relative_to(root)) for p in files if any(part.startswith(\".\") or part == \"__pycache__\" for part in p.relative_to(root).parts)]\n    add(\"✅\" if not hidden else \"⚠️\", \"no hidden / cache files\", \", \".join(hidden) or \"ok\")\n    total = sum(p.stat().st_size for p in files)\n    add(\"✅\" if total <= MAX_BYTES else \"❌\", \"size limit\", f\"{human_bytes(total)} / {human_bytes(MAX_BYTES)} ({'README' if _size else 'default, unverified'})\")\n    for st in [p for p in files if p.suffix == \".safetensors\"]:\n        ok = (st.parent / \"adapter_config.json\").exists()\n        add(\"✅\" if ok else \"⚠️\", f\"adapter config next to {st.relative_to(root)}\", \"\" if ok else \"adapter_config.json missing\")\n    prompt_tok = sum(est_tokens(p.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"ignore\")) for p in files if p.suffix == \".md\")\n    add(\"✅\" if prompt_tok < 0.15 * CONTEXT_LIMIT else \"⚠️\", \"prompt size vs context\", f\"~{prompt_tok:,} tokens across .md files\")\n\n    report = pd.DataFrame(checks)\n    passed = not (report[\"status\"] == \"❌\").any()\n    if verbose:\n        colour = lambda v: {\"✅\": \"background-color:#E6F4EA\", \"⚠️\": \"background-color:#FEF7E0\", \"❌\": \"background-color:#FCE8E6\"}.get(v, \"\")\n        display(report.style.hide(axis=\"index\").map(colour, subset=[\"status\"]).set_properties(**{\"text-align\": \"left\"}))\n        callout(\"All blocking checks passed.\" if passed else \"Blocking issues found. Fix them before submitting.\", \"ok\" if passed else \"err\")\n    return passed, report\n\nVALID, VALIDATION = validate_bundle(BUNDLE)","metadata":{},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def package(bundle, zip_path):\n    \"\"\"Deterministic zip: sorted entries, fixed timestamps → identical bytes for identical bundles.\"\"\"\n    zip_path = Path(zip_path)\n    zip_path.unlink(missing_ok=True)\n    with zipfile.ZipFile(zip_path, \"w\", zipfile.ZIP_DEFLATED) as z:\n        for p in sorted(p for p in Path(bundle).rglob(\"*\") if p.is_file()):\n            info = zipfile.ZipInfo(str(p.relative_to(bundle)).replace(os.sep, \"/\"), date_time=(1980, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0))\n            info.compress_type = zipfile.ZIP_DEFLATED\n            info.external_attr = 0o644 << 16\n            z.writestr(info, p.read_bytes())\n    return zip_path\n\nZIP_PATH = package(BUNDLE, WORK / CFG[\"zip_name\"])\nSHA = hashlib.sha256(ZIP_PATH.read_bytes()).hexdigest()\n\n# round trip: unzip into a temp dir and validate again (catches path/packaging mistakes)\n_rt = WORK / \"_roundtrip\"\nshutil.rmtree(_rt, ignore_errors=True)\nwith zipfile.ZipFile(ZIP_PATH) as z:\n    z.extractall(_rt)\n    names = z.namelist()\nRT_OK, _ = validate_bundle(_rt, verbose=False)\nshutil.rmtree(_rt, ignore_errors=True)\n\ncard = {\"created_utc\": datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat(timespec=\"seconds\"), \"zip\": ZIP_PATH.name, \"sha256\": SHA,\n        \"architecture\": ARCH, \"bundle_base\": CFG[\"bundle_base\"], \"model_alias\": MODEL_ALIAS, \"tools\": AVAILABLE_TOOLS,\n        \"context_limit\": CONTEXT_LIMIT, \"sampling\": {k: CFG[k] for k in [\"temperature\", \"top_p\", \"top_k\", \"max_output_tokens\", \"thinking_budget\"]},\n        \"prompt_sha\": hashlib.sha256(SYSTEM_PROMPT.encode()).hexdigest()[:12], \"validation_passed\": bool(VALID and RT_OK)}\n(WORK / \"experiment_card.json\").write_text(json.dumps(card, indent=2))\n\nkpis([(\"submission.zip\", human_bytes(ZIP_PATH.stat().st_size)), (\"Files\", len(names)), (\"sha256\", SHA[:12] + \"…\"),\n      (\"Validator\", \"PASSED ✅\" if VALID else \"FAILED ❌\"), (\"Round-trip\", \"PASSED ✅\" if RT_OK else \"FAILED ❌\")])\ndisplay(HTML(pre(\"\\n\".join(names))))\ncallout(f\"<b>{esc(ZIP_PATH)}</b> is ready. <code>experiment_card.json</code> records the config and hash, so you can log every submission \"\n        \"and know exactly which prompt produced which score.\", \"ok\" if VALID and RT_OK else \"warn\")","metadata":{},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"<a id=\"playbook\"></a>\n# 🚀 12. Playbook: what to try next, ranked by expected value\n\n| # | Idea | Why (evidence from this notebook) | Effort |\n|:-:|:--|:--|:-:|\n| 1 | **Prompt iteration with an experiment log** | Every run writes `experiment_card.json`. Change one thing at a time and keep the table below up to date. | 🟢 |\n| 2 | **Grep-first localization rule** | The mention boost in §7 shows how much exact identifier search adds over fuzzy retrieval. | 🟢 |\n| 3 | **Mandatory reproduce → compile → test loop** | §8 shows how edits can apply cleanly yet break the file. Many tasks add new tests (§4) that check the *described* behaviour. | 🟢 |\n| 4 | **Analyzer sub-agent tuning** | §9: the coder's context fills fast. Tighten the analyzer's answer format, or give it graph tools only for vague issues. | 🟡 |\n| 5 | **Repository-specific hints** | Hot files (§5) and per-repo test commands can go in a short appendix of the system prompt. Keep it small, since it costs context on every turn. | 🟡 |\n| 6 | **Reviewer sub-agent** | A read-only agent that inspects `git diff` against the issue before `submit_patch`. It catches scratch files, test edits and partial fixes (see `check_patch`). | 🟡 |\n| 7 | **Sampling & thinking budget sweep** | Lower temperature for editing, and a thinking budget that does not starve tool calls. Track the effect in the log. | 🟢 |\n| 8 | **LoRA adapter** (if the rules allow) | Train on *trajectories* (tool calls + edits) from repositories **other** than the benchmark ones. Tuning on the 129 public tasks risks overfitting and may conflict with the rules, so check them first. | 🔴 |\n\n#### 🧾 Experiment log template\n| run | architecture | prompt sha | temp | thinking | LB score | notes |\n|:--|:--|:--|:-:|:-:|:-:|:--|\n| baseline | analyzer+coder | *(from card)* | 0.2 | 4096 | | |\n\n#### 🔧 How to adapt this starter\n- **Everything** is controlled from `CFG` (§1). Prompts are plain functions (`build_system_prompt`, `build_analyzer_prompt`), so edit them directly.\n- New data format? Extend `to_graph`, `unpack_embeddings` or `read_py_files`. The rest of the notebook picks it up.\n- Validation fails on schema? Set `CFG[\"bundle_base\"] = \"sample\"` to build on top of the official sample.\n\n---\n<div style=\"text-align:center;padding:12px;color:#5F6368\">\nThanks to the community notebooks that explored this dataset first. This starter was rebuilt from scratch with corrected diff statistics and runtime-verified harness facts.<br>\n<b>If this notebook saved you time, an upvote helps others find it. Good luck on the leaderboard! 🍀</b>\n</div>","metadata":{}}]}

